window.MENU = {"event": "Devoxx Belgium 2026", "dates": "5-9 October 2026", "venue": "Kinepolis Antwerp", "signals": {"snapshot": "2026-10-26T10:09:57+00:00", "hasRatings": false, "avgLikeRate": 0.85, "thinBelowLikes": 20, "note": "views = reach; likes per 100 views = approval, noise under 20 likes; hearts = interest before the talk"}, "recap": {"intro": "Devoxx Belgium 2026 was mostly about working with coding agents: about a third of the sessions sat in the agentic engineering track, and most asked less what agents can do than how to check, review and contain them. The Java platform team showed a runtime that keeps getting leaner and faster, from compact object headers (on by default in JDK 27, speakers said) to Leyden's AOT cache and Valhalla value classes. Architecture and people talks argued for clear boundaries and for keeping the skills that AI makes easy to skip.", "trends": [{"title": "The harness outweighs the model", "body": "Several speakers said the system around an agent decides results more than the model does. Darwish cited Terminal-Bench, where the same Claude Opus 4.5 scored differently per harness. Rules in CLAUDE.md or AGENTS.md proved unreliable: Podlesniuk saw one of three runs ignore a word-for-word rule, and Petruzalek watched an agent edit a blocking hook script. The fix shown repeatedly was executable checks (ArchUnit, Error Prone, Maven Enforcer, OpenRewrite) whose failure messages become the agent's context. Speakers split on tooling: Grall and Charrière favoured plain CLIs and bash over MCP, while Ward and Tzolov's eval had an MCP docs server answer in 19 seconds against 318 for a shell agent.", "count": 23, "talkIds": [4087, 5507, 16414, 25671, 21690, 23529], "consensus": "The harness around an agent shapes results more than the model does, and rules that matter belong in executable checks, not in CLAUDE.md prose.", "debate": {"question": "Should coding agents reach tools through MCP servers or plain CLIs?", "sides": [{"stance": "CLIs and bash are lighter. Grall said agents increasingly prefer CLIs like gh over MCP servers; Charrière replaced a catalogue of tools with one bash tool, since every tool description eats a small model's context.", "who": ["Tugdual Grall", "Philippe Charrière"], "talkIds": [23529, 22911]}, {"stance": "MCP pays off for current documentation. In Ward and Tzolov's eval the javadocs.dev MCP arm answered in about 19 seconds, while a shell agent grepping jars took 318. Petruzalek keeps MCP mainly for always-current docs.", "who": ["James Ward", "Christian Tzolov", "Daniela Petruzalek"], "talkIds": [21690, 25671]}]}}, {"title": "Review is the new bottleneck", "body": "Agents make code cheap, but speakers agreed human review time has not shrunk. Martignole described huge PRs and an $86,000 bill as Back Market grew to 350 engineers on Claude Code. Rentea wants a fresh-context, adversarial AI to review before any human does; Cavalcanti showed such a review confidently reporting a bug that did not exist. Rivas and Martinelli review a short spec or plan instead of a 5,000-line PR, and Lippert and Gavrilescu want tools that show changes at architecture level. Hoogendoorn's team has dropped pull requests and code review for pairing and trunk-based development, while Schrijver insisted that if you bring it to review, it is your code. Burns's controlled experiment on Cargo Tracker found that Java build-time guardrails (strict lint, Maven Enforcer, SpotBugs) rarely changed what agents produced; code review stayed the last line of defence. Van Rijn's team got pull requests down to about 100 lines by slicing specs small in joint refinement.", "count": 20, "talkIds": [8171, 31452, 25042, 7008, 16414, 17782, 21687, 23531], "consensus": "Agents produce code faster than people can review it, so human attention has to move to smaller or higher-level units: specs, plans, phases or architecture views.", "debate": {"question": "How much human code review does agent-written code still need?", "sides": [{"stance": "None as a separate step. Hoogendoorn's team at iBOOD dropped pull requests and code reviews; quality comes from pairing, trunk-based development and thousands of automated tests, at about 100 deploys a day.", "who": ["Sander Hoogendoorn"], "talkIds": [7008]}, {"stance": "Less over time, with AI reviewing first. Rentea wants a fresh-context adversarial AI before any human, then halving review after months of good PRs; Petruzalek's parallel review sub-agents replace much of her line-by-line review.", "who": ["Victor Rentea", "Daniela Petruzalek"], "talkIds": [31452, 25671]}, {"stance": "A human must still own it. Cavalcanti saw a fresh-context Claude review invent a bug; Gavrilescu stopped reviewing and found 20 copies of one button; Schrijver: if you bring it to review, it's your code.", "who": ["Renato Guerra Cavalcanti", "Alex Gavrilescu", "Bert Jan Schrijver"], "talkIds": [25042, 25652, 19706]}]}}, {"title": "Treat coding agents as untrusted code", "body": "Security speakers agreed that prompt injection cannot be fixed with prompts. Gross called a coding agent remote code execution by design. Moraes showed \"treat as data\" wrappers beaten by forged closing tags and cited AgentDojo attack success of 45.5% on Qwen3 4B and about 5% on GPT-5. Vermeer demoed poisoned MCP tool descriptions and hidden instructions in skills, and argued pinned versions beat approval prompts. The shared answer was isolation: microVM sandboxes rather than containers (Šelajev noted the Docker socket gives root), host allowlists, and credentials swapped in by a proxy so they never enter the sandbox. Mehmandarov kept tag sandwiching, paired with closing-tag detection and canary tokens.", "count": 16, "talkIds": [7011, 21654, 9035, 25657, 48301, 5502], "consensus": "Prompt injection cannot be prevented reliably, so agents need deterministic limits outside the model: microVM sandboxes, host allowlists, server-scoped tools and credentials injected by a proxy.", "debate": {"question": "Are prompt-level defences against injection worth keeping at all?", "sides": [{"stance": "No, they are only suggestions. Moraes showed \"treat as data\" wrappers beaten by forged closing tags, Gross said probabilistic security is no security, and Šelajev got a classifier to run a secret-stealing script.", "who": ["Elder Moraes", "Richard Gross", "Oleg Šelajev"], "talkIds": [21654, 7011, 25657]}, {"stance": "Yes, as one layer. Mehmandarov keeps tag sandwiching with closing-tag detection, canary tokens and a cheap intent-classifier model, then backs them with deterministic Bean Validation because all of it stays probabilistic.", "who": ["Rustam Mehmandarov"], "talkIds": [5502]}]}}, {"title": "AI-found CVEs force faster patching", "body": "Several speakers said models now find vulnerabilities faster than teams patch them. According to Salm, frontier-model scanning found over 450 possible vulnerabilities in Spring since March, leading to more than 200 fixes; Cvetkovic said Spring fixed more CVEs in a few months of 2026 than in the previous 13 years. Belfius said reported vulnerabilities roughly doubled around Claude Mythos's release, and Semeteys noted curl ended its bug bounty under duplicate and fabricated reports. Advice converged: automate upgrades and tests, rank CVEs by reachability and exposure rather than CVSS (Finkelshtein: Spring4Shell only hits WAR deployments on standalone Tomcat), and meet the Cyber Resilience Act's reporting duties, live since September 2026.", "count": 14, "talkIds": [50107, 50109, 49254, 8194, 4051, 52102], "consensus": "AI-assisted research is finding vulnerabilities faster than before, so teams need automated upgrades and tests, and should rank CVEs by reachability and exposure rather than CVSS alone.", "debate": {"question": "Does the flood of AI-found vulnerabilities make software safer?", "sides": [{"stance": "The findings are real and become fixes. Salm said frontier-model scanning found over 450 possible Spring vulnerabilities, leading to more than 200 fixes; Cvetkovic said Spring fixed more CVEs in months than in 13 years.", "who": ["Timo Salm", "Neven Cvetkovic"], "talkIds": [50107, 50109]}, {"stance": "Volume is not security when human triage is the bottleneck. Semeteys credited Mozilla's 22 Claude-found Firefox bugs, but said duplicate and fabricated reports swamp security teams, and curl ended its bug bounty.", "who": ["Raphael Semeteys"], "talkIds": [11562]}]}}, {"title": "The JVM gets leaner and faster", "body": "Pressler's keynote argued that if AI makes syntax matter less, the runtime matters more; platform talks brought numbers. Speakers said compact object headers are the default in JDK 27; van Dijk and Przybyl saw a 10-million-object heap drop from about 190 MB to 114 MB, though Parlog said to measure decompression's CPU cost. Leyden's AOT cache took a Quarkus REST app from about 350 ms to 80 ms startup, with caveats on custom class loaders. JEP 401 value classes are planned as a preview in JDK 28 according to the speakers, but Paumard and Forax warned generic code loses scalarization and null-safety remains open. Structured concurrency, they said, is proposed as final in JDK 28. On the last day Simms explained why JEP 401 took about 3,000 commits: HotSpot flattens values only within limits set by nullability and atomicity, and C2 can always scalarize them through a new calling convention. De Tastes and Forax's ray tracer went from 128,000 GCs to none with value records.", "count": 21, "talkIds": [49251, 23543, 15354, 5510, 9752, 29351, 21694, 17794], "consensus": "Upgrading the JDK brings real memory and startup gains with little or no code change, from compact object headers to Leyden's AOT cache, each with some caveats.", "debate": {"question": "Will Valhalla value classes close Java's memory-layout gap?", "sides": [{"stance": "Pressler called array memory layout Java's remaining structural handicap against low-level languages, and said Valhalla removes it by flattening values based on declared intent rather than manual layout control.", "who": ["Ron Pressler"], "talkIds": [49252]}, {"stance": "Paumard and Forax measured limits: streams and other generic code lose scalarization to erasure, heap flattening stops at about 64 bits, small flattened arrays were slower than pointers, and null-safety stays unsolved.", "who": ["José Paumard", "Remi Forax"], "talkIds": [15354]}]}}, {"title": "Java reaches native code and GPUs", "body": "A cluster of talks used FFM, the Vector API and Project Babylon to run numeric and AI work in Java without JNI or Python. Fumero's Babylon-based HAT brought a tiled matrix multiply to 78 µs against 77 µs for cuTile Python. TornadoVM's jitLLM reported decode at 93% of llama.cpp, and Peterssen's 3.3 MB Quixotic jar runs 17 model families on the CPU. Speakers differed on how far pure Java goes: Peterssen said only matrix multiply needs native code, while Przybyl showed the Vector API's gain halving against a well-written scalar loop and noted Elasticsearch now calls a C++ kernel through FFM. Sevarac found packaging CUDA the hard part.", "count": 12, "talkIds": [21657, 14452, 17556, 19730, 22947, 22916], "consensus": "FFM, the Vector API and Project Babylon now let Java run numeric and AI workloads near native speed, without JNI or a Python runtime.", "debate": {"question": "Can pure Java kernels match native code for AI workloads?", "sides": [{"stance": "Nearly everywhere. Peterssen said only matrix multiply needs native code, with attention at full speed on the Vector API; Fumero's Babylon-based HAT tile kernel ran in 78 µs against 77 µs for cuTile Python.", "who": ["Alfonso² Peterssen", "Juan Fumero"], "talkIds": [14452, 21657]}, {"stance": "Native libraries still win the hottest paths. Przybyl saw the Vector API's gain halve against a tuned scalar loop, and Elasticsearch now calls a C++ kernel through FFM; jitLLM sends prefill matrix multiplies to cuBLAS.", "who": ["Piotr Przybyl", "Michalis Papadimitriou", "Orion Papadakis"], "talkIds": [17556, 19730]}]}}, {"title": "Boundaries before services", "body": "Architecture talks pushed back on service count as a goal. Klaver's Port of Rotterdam had more than 4.5 microservices per engineer and is consolidating into Spring Modulith services along event-storming boundaries; Hillaert argued bounded contexts need not map one-to-one to services. Van de Ven warned that hexagonal layers relocate dependencies rather than remove them, while Vandeperre and van der Meer used separate build modules and ArchUnit to keep agent-written code clean. On consistency, Trandafir, Zinck and Sulyz all started from the dual-write problem, then covered outbox or Debezium CDC (with heartbeats so Postgres WAL cannot fill the disk), sagas with idempotent compensations, and reconciliation as the final net. Topçu and Chevalier traced design debt to one domain model reused for unrelated problems, which spread into synchronised releases, dozens of feature flags and unclear ownership; Raneland made the case for strangling a legacy system piece by piece rather than rewriting it.", "count": 18, "talkIds": [8153, 12751, 8186, 17791, 10034, 16418, 10023, 5538], "consensus": "Good business boundaries matter more than service count, and consistency across boundaries needs explicit patterns: outbox or CDC, sagas with idempotent compensations, and reconciliation.", "debate": {"question": "Are hexagonal and clean-architecture layers worth their cost?", "sides": [{"stance": "Only where change justifies them. Van de Ven said layers relocate dependencies rather than remove them, mappers cannot protect against dropped columns, and hexagonal adds distance that costs money when code rarely changes.", "who": ["Ties van de Ven"], "talkIds": [8186]}, {"stance": "Yes, especially with agents writing code. Vandeperre and van der Meer refactored a vibe-coded app into a framework-free domain with ports and adapters, separate build modules and ArchUnit tests, so drift fails the build.", "who": ["Maarten Vandeperre", "Lutske van der Meer"], "talkIds": [5503]}]}}, {"title": "From writing code to judging it", "body": "Speakers agreed developer roles are shifting, not disappearing, but disagreed on what to hold on to. Clark and Rohrer traced \"programmers will be unnecessary\" predictions back to Fortran in 1956 and named de-skilling as the real risk. Rentea's keynote urged moves toward product, architecture or quality work and concluded that hand debugging no longer pays, while Cavalcanti cited Bainbridge's automation paradox: people who only monitor lose the skills for what cannot be automated. Schrijver cited measured gains of 5-15% and METR's finding that developers felt faster while being 19% slower. Debois and ING treated scaling as an enablement problem, with shared, reviewed skills and agents rather than individual habits. Cougil put seven XP practices on trial against AI and concluded each matters more, since AI amplifies bad habits as much as good ones.", "count": 21, "talkIds": [51951, 8164, 25042, 19706, 21671, 9023, 15359], "consensus": "Developer roles are shifting toward specifying, judging and owning results rather than disappearing, and accountability for agent output stays with people.", "debate": {"question": "Should developers keep hands-on skills like debugging?", "sides": [{"stance": "Let them go. Rentea said Claude found 17 memory leaks and 10 bottlenecks in 30 minutes, called hand debugging and tuning \"unethical\" at work, and set a target of over 95% generated code.", "who": ["Victor Rentea"], "talkIds": [51951]}, {"stance": "Keep them on purpose. Cavalcanti cited Bainbridge: people who only monitor automation lose the skills for what cannot be automated, so he still writes API shapes himself; Clark and Rohrer called de-skilling the real risk.", "who": ["Renato Guerra Cavalcanti", "Katie Clark", "Simon Rohrer"], "talkIds": [25042, 8164]}]}}]}, "picks": [{"verdict": "The harness outweighs the model", "talkId": 16414, "why": "You need AI-generated code to meet a regulated enterprise's standards and want concrete harness techniques that worked under tight tooling restrictions."}, {"verdict": "Review is the new bottleneck", "talkId": 31452, "why": "Your team ships agent-written code and is debating how much human review it still needs, and you want a concrete, step-by-step workflow rather than a slogan."}, {"verdict": "Treat coding agents as untrusted code", "talkId": 9035, "why": "You or your team install MCP servers and skills into coding agents, especially in auto-approve mode, and want to see concretely how they get abused."}, {"verdict": "AI-found CVEs force faster patching", "talkId": 8194, "why": "You own Java or Node services and want a broad, demo-heavy tour of how attacks chain together and which supply-chain controls to add first."}, {"verdict": "The JVM gets leaner and faster", "talkId": 15354, "why": "You want a precise picture of what value classes change in Java semantics and performance before they preview in JDK 28, including the limitations."}, {"verdict": "Java reaches native code and GPUs", "talkId": 14452, "why": "You want local LLM, speech or embedding inference inside a Java application without Python or native servers, and want to see how fast pure Java can be on a CPU."}, {"verdict": "Boundaries before services", "talkId": 8153, "why": "Your team owns far more microservices than people and you want a candid, in-progress account of consolidating with DDD and Spring Modulith."}, {"verdict": "From writing code to judging it", "talkId": 51951, "why": "You feel stuck between agent hype and exhaustion and want one opinionated practitioner's checklist for retraining, delivered in 20 minutes."}], "picksNote": "provisional, from the signals; editor's picks to follow", "talks": [{"id": 24228, "title": "Java, Raspberry Pi, and the Art of Overengineering Everyday Life", "speakers": [{"name": "Matti Tahvonen", "company": "Vaadin"}], "room": "Room 9", "start": "2026-10-07T12:00:00Z", "end": "2026-10-07T12:50:00Z", "type": "Conference", "track": "Mind the Geek", "level": "BEGINNER", "minutes": 44, "keywords": ["raspberry pi", "sensors", "java", "home automation"], "themes": ["Hardware, robots & fun", "Developer experience & tooling"], "tldr": "Tahvonen shows how a Java web developer can build real Raspberry Pi projects, from a BBQ smoker controller to a jetty camera, entirely in Java, and shares a starter project.", "watchIf": "You are a Java developer who wants to try Raspberry Pi projects without switching to Python, and want a practical setup for development, deployment and remote access.", "takeaway": "Pick a real problem from your own life and build it in Java instead of working through breadboard tutorials.", "videoId": "5W4o_aYOaIY", "hearts": 230, "views": 454, "likes": 2, "likeRate": 0.7, "thin": true, "rating": null, "numRatings": null, "seat": true}, {"id": 8156, "title": "I Hired a Real Robot as My Junior Dev — and Now My Kids Don't Want to Learn Java Anymore", "speakers": [{"name": "Jan Van Wassenhove", "company": "Sopra Steria"}], "room": "Room 6", "start": "2026-10-07T10:00:00Z", "end": "2026-10-07T10:50:00Z", "type": "Conference", "track": "Mind the Geek", "level": "INTERMEDIATE", "minutes": 48, "keywords": ["robotics", "architecture", "software", "agents"], "themes": ["AI agents & agentic coding", "Architecture & design", "Hardware, robots & fun"], "tldr": "Van Wassenhove gave a Reachy Mini robot an agent-built brain; six failures from running it at home show that specifying and verifying, not coding, are now the hard parts.", "watchIf": "You build agentic systems and want concrete failure stories about trust boundaries, memory, guardrails and silent fallbacks, told through a home robot.", "takeaway": "Write security claims with an attacker in the sentence (\"someone who copies this folder gets nothing because the key is not in it\") and check them.", "videoId": "P14Fy05Gs38", "hearts": 304, "views": 591, "likes": 2, "likeRate": 0.64, "thin": true, "rating": null, "numRatings": null, "seat": true}, {"id": 7011, "title": "Secure Your Coding Agent Like It’s Malware", "speakers": [{"name": "Richard Gross", "company": "MaibornWolff GmbH"}], "room": "Room 8", "start": "2026-10-05T14:50:00Z", "end": "2026-10-05T15:20:00Z", "type": "Tools-in-Action", "track": "Security, Trust & Compliance", "level": "BEGINNER", "minutes": 31, "keywords": ["coding agents", "sandbox security", "prompt injection", "data exfiltration"], "themes": ["Security & supply chain", "AI agents & agentic coding"], "tldr": "Prompt injection cannot be fully prevented, so Gross argues for treating coding agents as untrusted and running them in microVM sandboxes with network allowlists and externally injected credentials.", "watchIf": "You run coding agents on your laptop or in CI and want a quick map of the injection threats and a concrete sandboxing setup.", "takeaway": "Apply the lethal trifecta: never give one agent private data, external write or network access, and untrusted input all at once.", "videoId": "GLJGhkxMdgU", "hearts": 335, "views": 936, "likes": 9, "likeRate": 0.91, "thin": true, "rating": null, "numRatings": null, "seat": true}, {"id": 7012, "title": "Destructoring is the future of Javas Encapsulation", "speakers": [{"name": "Richard Gross", "company": "MaibornWolff GmbH"}], "room": "Room 5", "start": "2026-10-05T10:40:00Z", "end": "2026-10-05T11:20:00Z", "type": "Lunch Talk", "track": "Java Language & Platform", "level": "BEGINNER", "minutes": 37, "keywords": ["encapsulation", "serialization", "destructuring", "records"], "themes": ["Java language & JDK", "Security & supply chain"], "tldr": "Gross explains how serialization and deep reflection break encapsulation, and how Project Amber's planned deconstruction patterns would give Java safe serialization, pattern matching for classes and withers.", "watchIf": "You want to understand why deserialized Java objects can violate their own invariants and what Project Amber's deconstruction work is likely to bring.", "takeaway": "Do not assume constructor validation holds for objects created by Jackson, Hibernate or Java serialization; validate after deserialization or use records.", "videoId": "wcghmWdu-HY", "hearts": 180, "views": 1283, "likes": 10, "likeRate": 0.81, "thin": true, "rating": null, "numRatings": null, "seat": true}, {"id": 5507, "title": "Stopping AI-Generated Architectural Drift: Deterministic Guardrails for Agentic Coding", "speakers": [{"name": "Sasha Podlesniuk", "company": "INSHUR"}], "room": "Room 8", "start": "2026-10-05T15:35:00Z", "end": "2026-10-05T16:05:00Z", "type": "Tools-in-Action", "track": "Architecture & Modernization", "level": "INTERMEDIATE", "minutes": 24, "keywords": ["agentic", "guardrails", "linters", "archunit"], "themes": ["AI agents & agentic coding", "Architecture & design", "Testing & quality"], "tldr": "Podlesniuk shows how INSHUR turns natural-language agent rules into executable ArchUnit, Error Prone and Checkstyle checks that coding agents hit during the build and fix themselves.", "watchIf": "Your team merges a lot of agent-written code and you want concrete ways to enforce architecture rules in the build instead of in prompts.", "takeaway": "Turn recurring PR review comments into ArchUnit or Checkstyle rules that fail the build with a specific reason and location.", "videoId": "K3kD89Y1YCk", "hearts": 268, "views": 909, "likes": 9, "likeRate": 0.92, "thin": true, "rating": null, "numRatings": null, "seat": true}, {"id": 23527, "title": "Project Detroit: From Foreign Libraries to Foreign Language Runtimes", "speakers": [{"name": "Maurizio Cimadamore", "company": "Oracle"}], "room": "Room 10", "start": "2026-10-08T07:30:00Z", "end": "2026-10-08T08:20:00Z", "type": "Conference", "track": "Java Language & Platform", "level": "INTERMEDIATE", "minutes": 50, "keywords": ["python", "ffm", "v8", "javascript"], "themes": ["Java language & JDK", "JVM performance & runtime"], "tldr": "Cimadamore explains why Nashorn failed and how Project Detroit instead embeds V8 and CPython behind the javax.script API, and what that costs in upcall performance, lifetimes and virtual-thread pinning.", "watchIf": "You run JavaScript or Python from Java, or are interested in what bridging two language runtimes involves in garbage collection, threading and native interop.", "takeaway": "If you still depend on Nashorn or javax.script, try the experimental Detroit engines and report compatibility and performance issues to the project.", "videoId": "JWFZY0aJTjg", "hearts": 106, "views": 2795, "likes": 14, "likeRate": 0.58, "thin": true, "rating": null, "numRatings": null, "seat": true}, {"id": 25042, "title": "Owning the Code You Didn't Type", "speakers": [{"name": "Renato Guerra Cavalcanti", "company": "Octo Cogito"}], "room": "Room 5", "start": "2026-10-08T14:30:00Z", "end": "2026-10-08T15:20:00Z", "type": "Conference", "track": "Development Practices", "level": "BEGINNER", "minutes": 50, "keywords": ["readability", "understanding", "control", "code"], "themes": ["AI agents & agentic coding", "Teams, careers & leadership", "Developer experience & tooling"], "tldr": "Cavalcanti argues that delegating coding to AI removes the activity that built developers' understanding, and shares practices for staying in control: writing rules, files over chat, walk-throughs, phased delivery.", "watchIf": "You use coding agents daily and feel your grasp of your own codebase slipping, and want specific habits for staying in control.", "takeaway": "Add writing rules that demand plain, clear English from your agent, and re-inject them with a prompt hook if they stop being followed.", "videoId": "SQUrHzMie3o", "hearts": 371, "views": 391, "likes": 4, "likeRate": 0.9, "thin": true, "rating": null, "numRatings": null, "seat": true}, {"id": 11556, "title": "223 Pull Requests in 11 Days: The Recipe for Building Real Software with AI Agents", "speakers": [{"name": "Julien Dubois", "company": "GitHub"}], "room": "Room 8", "start": "2026-10-08T07:30:00Z", "end": "2026-10-08T08:20:00Z", "type": "Conference", "track": "Development Practices", "level": "INTERMEDIATE", "minutes": 50, "keywords": ["developer", "ai", "spring boot", "automation"], "themes": ["AI agents & agentic coding", "Developer experience & tooling", "Spring & frameworks"], "tldr": "Dubois built Boot UI, a Spring Boot developer console, in 11 days through 223 agent-written pull requests, and shares six ingredients: model choice, specs, tests, tools, parallelism, overnight runs.", "watchIf": "You want a measured account of shipping a real Java project with a fleet of coding agents, including the workflow, costs and token-caching details.", "takeaway": "Write an AGENTS.md that pins your build tools, test approach and formatting so parallel agents produce consistent code.", "videoId": "0A2jqCqSIjg", "hearts": 626, "views": 2976, "likes": 20, "likeRate": 0.71, "thin": false, "rating": null, "numRatings": null, "seat": true}, {"id": 4090, "title": "Help! I hate my team mate!", "speakers": [{"name": "Jessica Siewert", "company": "Het ConsultancyHuis"}], "room": "Room 5", "start": "2026-10-08T15:40:00Z", "end": "2026-10-08T16:30:00Z", "type": "Conference", "track": "People, Practices & Career", "level": "BEGINNER", "minutes": 52, "keywords": ["cooperation", "communication", "conflict", "teamwork"], "themes": ["Teams, careers & leadership"], "tldr": "Siewert combines conflict theory, Dutch workplace statistics and two cases from her own career into five ways to get out of team conflicts and three ways to prevent them.", "watchIf": "You are stuck in friction with a teammate, or lead a team, and want practical, experience-based approaches to defusing conflict.", "takeaway": "When frustrated with a teammate, ask yourself whether you are prejudging them based on earlier experiences.", "videoId": "sWP9osLV45I", "hearts": 326, "views": 472, "likes": 3, "likeRate": 0.77, "thin": true, "rating": null, "numRatings": null, "seat": true}, {"id": 52105, "title": "Keynote overflow room", "speakers": [{"name": "Stephan Janssen", "company": "Devoxx"}], "room": "Room 5", "start": "2026-10-07T07:30:00Z", "end": "2026-10-07T09:30:00Z", "type": "Keynote Overflow", "track": "Java Language & Platform", "level": "BEGINNER", "minutes": 120, "keywords": ["keynote overflow room", "overflow", "keynote", "room"], "themes": ["Developer experience & tooling"], "tldr": "Placeholder entry for the keynote overflow room; the listing contains no session content beyond the room name.", "watchIf": "You want to know whether this entry has content of its own; it does not.", "takeaway": "Watch the main keynote recording instead of this entry for actual content.", "videoId": null, "hearts": 43, "views": null, "likes": null, "likeRate": null, "thin": false, "rating": null, "numRatings": null, "seat": true}, {"id": 21654, "title": "Your AI Agent Trusts Strangers. Let Me Show You.", "speakers": [{"name": "Elder Moraes", "company": "IBM"}], "room": "Room 5", "start": "2026-10-05T14:50:00Z", "end": "2026-10-05T15:20:00Z", "type": "Tools-in-Action", "track": "Agentic Engineering & Tooling", "level": "INTERMEDIATE", "minutes": 29, "keywords": ["agents", "threat-model", "mitigations", "prompt-injection"], "themes": ["Security & supply chain", "AI agents & agentic coding", "LLMs in Java apps"], "tldr": "Through recorded attack rounds against a Quarkus/LangChain4j support agent, Moraes shows prompt guardrails and \"treat as data\" wrappers fail against indirect injection, while removing tools, output guardrails, CSP and server-scoped tools hold.", "watchIf": "You are building a Java agent with tools that reads content from outside your organisation and want to see which defences hold up against an attacker who adapts.", "takeaway": "Map each agent against the lethal trifecta: untrusted input, private data, a way out. Then remove at least one leg.", "videoId": "j3aEB0i3Up8", "hearts": 199, "views": 669, "likes": 8, "likeRate": 1.0, "thin": true, "rating": null, "numRatings": null, "seat": true}, {"id": 31455, "title": "Half of a billion Idle GPUs: The Case for On-Device AI", "speakers": [{"name": "Prince Canuma", "company": "Neywa Labs"}], "room": "Room 8", "start": "2026-10-07T08:10:00Z", "end": "2026-10-07T08:30:00Z", "type": "Keynote", "track": "Agentic Engineering & Tooling", "level": "INTERMEDIATE", "minutes": 20, "keywords": ["applesilicon", "quantization", "on-device", "inference"], "themes": ["Developer experience & tooling", "Hardware, robots & fun"], "tldr": "Canuma argues that the billion-plus Apple Silicon devices already in use can run most AI workloads locally, and shows the MLX-VLM/MLX-Audio engine and Nativ app built to do that.", "watchIf": "You are deciding whether an AI feature should run on users' Macs and iPhones or in the cloud and want a short argument for local inference with numbers.", "takeaway": "Try running your agent or coding workflow against a local open model on Apple Silicon before paying for extra cloud usage.", "videoId": "qFTuteOEtsE", "hearts": 445, "views": 623, "likes": 2, "likeRate": 0.62, "thin": true, "rating": null, "numRatings": null, "seat": true}, {"id": 16426, "title": "No Country For Old APIs. Wrapping legacy Java services as MCP tools without rewriting them.", "speakers": [{"name": "Željko Kozina", "company": "Chronomill"}], "room": "Room 4", "start": "2026-10-07T15:50:00Z", "end": "2026-10-07T16:40:00Z", "type": "Conference", "track": "Architecture & Modernization", "level": "INTERMEDIATE", "minutes": 45, "keywords": ["authentication", "brownfield", "facade", "mcp"], "themes": ["Legacy modernization", "AI agents & agentic coding", "Spring & frameworks"], "tldr": "Kozina puts a Spring for GraphQL facade in front of legacy REST and SOAP services and exposes curated GraphQL operations as MCP tools, adding Keycloak scopes and a three-tool dynamic mode for very large schemas.", "watchIf": "You have to expose old REST or SOAP Java services to AI agents and want a concrete Spring-based pattern for curation, testing and auth instead of a greenfield demo.", "takeaway": "Start by exposing a few hand-picked read-only operations as MCP tools and review tool definitions in pull requests like code.", "videoId": "a5cQAl0g2AM", "hearts": 184, "views": 366, "likes": 3, "likeRate": 0.84, "thin": true, "rating": null, "numRatings": null, "seat": true}, {"id": 8194, "title": "Write Java Code Like a Seasoned Hacker: 2026 Edition", "speakers": [{"name": "Soroosh Khodami", "company": "Rabobank"}], "room": "Room 9", "start": "2026-10-06T11:30:00Z", "end": "2026-10-06T14:30:00Z", "type": "Deep Dive", "track": "Security, Trust & Compliance", "level": "ADVANCED", "minutes": 167, "keywords": ["owasp", "java", "security", "devsecops"], "themes": ["Security & supply chain", "AI agents & agentic coding"], "tldr": "A three-hour security session arguing that AI makes attacks cheaper but the game is unchanged: learn classic vulnerabilities, shift checks left, monitor production, and lock down your dependency supply chain.", "watchIf": "You own Java or Node services and want a broad, demo-heavy tour of how attacks chain together and which supply-chain controls to add first.", "takeaway": "Add Gitleaks or TruffleHog as a pre-commit hook and pipeline step.", "videoId": "Upd5ZqUn1qY", "hearts": 263, "views": 3360, "likes": 26, "likeRate": 0.79, "thin": false, "rating": null, "numRatings": null, "seat": true}, {"id": 31454, "title": "Run Frontier Open Models on consumer hardware", "speakers": [{"name": "Prince Canuma", "company": "Neywa Labs"}], "room": "Room 3", "start": "2026-10-07T12:00:00Z", "end": "2026-10-07T12:50:00Z", "type": "Conference", "track": "Agentic Engineering & Tooling", "level": "INTERMEDIATE", "minutes": 50, "keywords": ["memory", "quantization", "latency", "inference"], "themes": ["Developer experience & tooling", "AI agents & agentic coding"], "tldr": "Canuma explains the accuracy/latency/memory trade-offs of local inference on Apple Silicon and spends most of the hour demoing Nativ, his app built on MLX-VLM, running coding agents and voice on a laptop.", "watchIf": "You want to run open models on a Mac for coding or voice work and want a practical tour of an MLX-based stack. Don't expect benchmark-heavy engine internals: the talk is mostly a product demo.", "takeaway": "Before buying a Mac for local models, divide its memory bandwidth by the model size to estimate decode speed.", "videoId": "DseVLPC4Qjs", "hearts": 324, "views": 437, "likes": 0, "likeRate": 0.55, "thin": true, "rating": null, "numRatings": null, "seat": true}, {"id": 9752, "title": "Learning modern Java the playful way", "speakers": [{"name": "Marit van Dijk", "company": "JetBrains"}, {"name": "Piotr Przybyl", "company": "Elastic"}], "room": "Room 5", "start": "2026-10-06T07:30:00Z", "end": "2026-10-06T10:30:00Z", "type": "Deep Dive", "track": "Java Language & Platform", "level": "BEGINNER", "minutes": 179, "keywords": ["java", "structuredconcurrency", "stream", "ide"], "themes": ["Java language & JDK", "JVM performance & runtime", "Developer experience & tooling"], "tldr": "Van Dijk and Przybyl upgrade a Java 8-era image-search app with features up to Java 28 in IntelliJ IDEA, and argue that non-LTS upgrades pay for themselves in performance.", "watchIf": "You are on Java 8 to 21 and want an unhurried, demo-heavy tour of what newer Java (up to 28 previews) changes in real code, plus arguments to convince management to upgrade.", "takeaway": "Replace parallel streams that do I/O with Gatherers.mapConcurrent, and fan-out/fan-in CompletableFuture code with StructuredTaskScope.", "videoId": "QBQ11aL2aZc", "hearts": 189, "views": 849, "likes": 5, "likeRate": 0.72, "thin": true, "rating": null, "numRatings": null, "seat": true}, {"id": 8186, "title": "The Hexagonal Hangover", "speakers": [{"name": "Ties van de Ven", "company": "Alliander"}], "room": "Room 8", "start": "2026-10-08T11:50:00Z", "end": "2026-10-08T12:40:00Z", "type": "Conference", "track": "Architecture & Modernization", "level": "INTERMEDIATE", "minutes": 49, "keywords": ["cohesion", "architecture", "abstraction", "coupling"], "themes": ["Architecture & design"], "tldr": "Van de Ven argues that every abstraction layer is a trade-off, and gives a model of cohesion, coupling (depth times connectivity), distance and volatility for deciding when hexagonal architecture is worth its cost.", "watchIf": "You have inherited or are designing a layered or hexagonal Java codebase and want a vocabulary for arguing which abstractions actually earn their keep.", "takeaway": "Before adding an interface or layer, name the concrete change it protects against and check whether that change is likely.", "videoId": "m9viRAIRAUQ", "hearts": 513, "views": 3886, "likes": 65, "likeRate": 1.53, "thin": false, "rating": null, "numRatings": null, "seat": true}, {"id": 14463, "title": "When benchmarks go bad - what we learned from measuring performance wrong", "speakers": [{"name": "Francesco Nigro", "company": "IBM"}, {"name": "Holly Cummins", "company": "IBM"}], "room": "Room 6", "start": "2026-10-07T15:50:00Z", "end": "2026-10-07T16:40:00Z", "type": "Conference", "track": "Development Practices", "level": "INTERMEDIATE", "minutes": 54, "keywords": ["performance", "biases", "measurement", "load testing"], "themes": ["JVM performance & runtime", "Testing & quality"], "tldr": "Cummins and Nigro go through the mistakes they made rebuilding Quarkus's public benchmark against Spring, and how to get reproducibility, realism and relevance right, including coordinated omission and active benchmarking.", "watchIf": "You are about to compare frameworks, JVM settings or code changes by benchmarking them and want to avoid publishing numbers that only measure your laptop, network or load generator.", "takeaway": "Never run the load generator and the system under test on the same unisolated machine, and avoid laptops for numbers you'll publish.", "videoId": "yePre1hOvHE", "hearts": 248, "views": 471, "likes": 4, "likeRate": 0.85, "thin": true, "rating": null, "numRatings": null, "seat": true}, {"id": 17791, "title": "Consistency Patterns in Event-Driven Architecture", "speakers": [{"name": "Emanuel Trandafir", "company": ""}], "room": "Room 5", "start": "2026-10-07T14:40:00Z", "end": "2026-10-07T15:30:00Z", "type": "Conference", "track": "Architecture & Modernization", "level": "INTERMEDIATE", "minutes": 45, "keywords": ["distributedsystems", "inbox", "sagas", "outbox"], "themes": ["Architecture & design"], "tldr": "Trandafir starts from an order method with too many side effects and walks through the dual-write problem, sagas (orchestration versus choreography), the transactional outbox and the inbox for idempotent consumers.", "watchIf": "You publish domain events from a service that also writes to a database, and want a clear, pattern-by-pattern guide to keeping both consistent.", "takeaway": "Move remote calls and message publishing out of database transactions, and check the connection-acquire time in your metrics.", "videoId": "8JuQaWFKk4w", "hearts": 408, "views": 568, "likes": 6, "likeRate": 0.93, "thin": true, "rating": null, "numRatings": null, "seat": true}, {"id": 7035, "title": "Their API Went Down. So Why Is My App on Fire?", "speakers": [{"name": "Ines Panker", "company": ""}], "room": "Room 9", "start": "2026-10-08T15:40:00Z", "end": "2026-10-08T16:30:00Z", "type": "Conference", "track": "Development Practices", "level": "INTERMEDIATE", "minutes": 46, "keywords": ["outages", "ratelimit", "circuitbreaker", "apis"], "themes": ["Architecture & design"], "tldr": "Drawing on a product with about 40 third-party integrations, Panker shows how a slow external API exhausts your workers, and which containment measures stop that: error classification, rate-limit caching, checkpointed pagination, timeouts, circuit breakers, load shedding and bulkheads.", "watchIf": "Your product calls several third-party APIs from shared workers and you want proven containment patterns, with real numbers, rather than a generic resilience library tour.", "takeaway": "Set explicit connect and read timeouts on every outbound HTTP client.", "videoId": "PGpKfdXZrpc", "hearts": 153, "views": 341, "likes": 3, "likeRate": 0.86, "thin": true, "rating": null, "numRatings": null, "seat": true}, {"id": 14459, "title": "Bootiful Security", "speakers": [{"name": "Daniel Garnier-Moiroux", "company": "Spring"}, {"name": "Josh Long", "company": "the Spring team"}], "room": "Room 8", "start": "2026-10-08T15:40:00Z", "end": "2026-10-08T16:30:00Z", "type": "Conference", "track": "Security, Trust & Compliance", "level": "BEGINNER", "minutes": 51, "keywords": ["passwords", "authentication", "spring security", "passkeys"], "themes": ["Security & supply chain", "Spring & frameworks"], "tldr": "Garnier-Moiroux and Long live-code a Spring Security 7 app that moves from hashed passwords to one-time tokens, passkeys and MFA, then to OAuth2 SSO, a BFF gateway and a secured MCP server.", "watchIf": "You maintain Spring apps with username/password login and want to see, in working code, how passkeys, MFA, OAuth2 and BFF fit together in Spring Security 7.", "takeaway": "Migrate password storage to Argon2 behind a DelegatingPasswordEncoder and let hashes upgrade on login.", "videoId": "W-OKVLMtPAw", "hearts": 367, "views": 587, "likes": 17, "likeRate": 1.7, "thin": true, "rating": null, "numRatings": null, "seat": true}, {"id": 10046, "title": "Who will build software after AI?", "speakers": [{"name": "Eddy Vos", "company": "Devoxx4Kids Netherlands"}], "room": "BOF 2", "start": "2026-10-06T17:00:00Z", "end": "2026-10-06T18:00:00Z", "type": "BOF", "track": "Mind the Geek", "level": "BEGINNER", "minutes": 60, "keywords": ["coding", "robotics", "ai-assisted", "education"], "themes": ["Teams, careers & leadership", "Hardware, robots & fun"], "tldr": "An open BOF discussion on whether and how to keep teaching kids to code and build robots now that AI generates code.", "watchIf": "You teach, mentor or parent kids around technology and want to compare notes on coding education in the AI era.", "takeaway": "Bring your experience from teaching or mentoring kids and share what works.", "videoId": null, "hearts": 46, "views": null, "likes": null, "likeRate": null, "thin": false, "rating": null, "numRatings": null, "seat": false}, {"id": 29351, "title": "Structured Concurrency Looms", "speakers": [{"name": "Alan Bateman", "company": "Oracle"}, {"name": "Viktor Klang", "company": "Oracle"}], "room": "Room 5", "start": "2026-10-07T10:00:00Z", "end": "2026-10-07T10:50:00Z", "type": "Conference", "track": "Java Language & Platform", "level": "INTERMEDIATE", "minutes": 51, "keywords": ["projectloom", "structuredconcurrency", "concurrency", "virtualthreads"], "themes": ["Java language & JDK", "JVM performance & runtime"], "tldr": "Bateman and Klang explain why fan-out code with executors is hard to get right and show how StructuredTaskScope handles cancellation, timeouts, joiners, exceptions and observability. It is proposed as final in JDK 28.", "watchIf": "You write fan-out code with executors and futures and want the JDK team's own explanation of how to use StructuredTaskScope correctly before it becomes final.", "takeaway": "Replace hand-rolled ExecutorService fan-out with StructuredTaskScope and virtual threads for independent I/O-bound calls.", "videoId": "aow56RgptLA", "hearts": 299, "views": 1369, "likes": 15, "likeRate": 1.0, "thin": true, "rating": null, "numRatings": null, "seat": true}, {"id": 22946, "title": "Decoding the Cosmos: Voyager's 1970s Algorithms Meet Today's Cutting-Edge Tech", "speakers": [{"name": "Pieter van der Meer", "company": "Dataworkz"}], "room": "Room 7", "start": "2026-10-08T15:40:00Z", "end": "2026-10-08T16:30:00Z", "type": "Conference", "track": "Mind the Geek", "level": "INTERMEDIATE", "minutes": 41, "keywords": ["machinelearning", "decoding", "viterbi", "voyager"], "themes": ["Hardware, robots & fun"], "tldr": "Van der Meer uses Voyager's deep-space link to explain convolutional coding and Viterbi decoding, then shows the same algorithm in named-entity recognition and contrasts it with a TensorFlow denoising model.", "watchIf": "You like space history and want an approachable, visual explanation of convolutional codes and Viterbi decoding. It covers fundamentals, not production techniques.", "takeaway": "Read up on Viterbi decoding if you work with sequence labelling; it is the same dynamic-programming idea behind HMM taggers.", "videoId": "CuvV3Aud3Q0", "hearts": 189, "views": 414, "likes": 2, "likeRate": 0.73, "thin": true, "rating": null, "numRatings": null, "seat": true}, {"id": 4079, "title": "👩‍💻 Developing with AI: What if it were as simple as adding a library? 🧑‍💻", "speakers": [{"name": "Stéphane Philippart", "company": "OVHcloud"}, {"name": "Matthieu Vincent", "company": "Sopra Steria"}], "room": "BOF 1", "start": "2026-10-06T07:30:00Z", "end": "2026-10-06T10:30:00Z", "type": "Hands-on Lab", "track": "Agentic Engineering & Tooling", "level": "BEGINNER", "minutes": 180, "keywords": ["artificial intelligence", "java", "langchain4j", "generative ai"], "themes": ["LLMs in Java apps", "AI agents & agentic coding"], "tldr": "A 3-hour beginner lab where participants add generative AI to Java applications with LangChain4j and Quarkus, covering prompts, RAG, MCP tools and agents.", "watchIf": "You are a Java developer new to generative AI and want a guided, practical first integration.", "takeaway": "Start with LangChain4j in an existing Java project before considering a new stack.", "videoId": null, "hearts": 49, "views": null, "likes": null, "likeRate": null, "thin": false, "rating": null, "numRatings": null, "seat": false}, {"id": 17759, "title": "Kubernetes for Developers", "speakers": [{"name": "Alain Regnier", "company": "SCIAM"}], "room": "Room 8", "start": "2026-10-07T15:50:00Z", "end": "2026-10-07T16:40:00Z", "type": "Conference", "track": "Cloud Native, Data & Infrastructure", "level": "INTERMEDIATE", "minutes": 47, "keywords": ["usage", "companies", "kubernetes", "evolution"], "themes": ["Cloud native & Kubernetes", "Developer experience & tooling"], "tldr": "Regnier covers what developers need to know about Kubernetes without becoming DevOps: Flox and kind locally, image builds without Docker, resources, graceful shutdown, probes, kubectl debug, security and OpenTelemetry.", "watchIf": "You deploy apps to Kubernetes but leave the YAML to the platform team, and you want a checklist of the settings that actually affect your app's reliability and security.", "takeaway": "Add a preStop sleep and handle SIGTERM in your service, and check that your process runs as PID 1.", "videoId": "AxhLQZqZdIg", "hearts": 326, "views": 655, "likes": 9, "likeRate": 1.08, "thin": true, "rating": null, "numRatings": null, "seat": true}, {"id": 24206, "title": "GraalVM Community BOF", "speakers": [{"name": "Thomas Wuerthinger", "company": "Oracle"}, {"name": "Fabio Niephaus", "company": "Oracle"}, {"name": "Alina Yurenko", "company": "Oracle"}], "room": "BOF 1", "start": "2026-10-07T16:50:00Z", "end": "2026-10-07T17:50:00Z", "type": "BOF", "track": "Java Language & Platform", "level": "INTERMEDIATE", "minutes": 60, "keywords": ["graalvm", "nativeimage", "graalwasm", "graalpy"], "themes": ["JVM performance & runtime", "Java language & JDK"], "tldr": "A community BOF with the GraalVM team on Crema, GraalPy, GraalWasm, framework integrations and the roadmap, with room for questions and feedback.", "watchIf": "You use or evaluate GraalVM Native Image and want to influence or preview its direction.", "takeaway": "Bring your Native Image pain points and feedback to the team.", "videoId": null, "hearts": 36, "views": null, "likes": null, "likeRate": null, "thin": false, "rating": null, "numRatings": null, "seat": false}, {"id": 10022, "title": "From code to control: master deployments with Feature Flagging and OpenFeature", "speakers": [{"name": "Alexandre Touret", "company": "Magellan"}, {"name": "Philippe Duval", "company": "Magellan"}], "room": "BOF 2", "start": "2026-10-06T07:30:00Z", "end": "2026-10-06T10:30:00Z", "type": "Hands-on Lab", "track": "Architecture & Modernization", "level": "BEGINNER", "minutes": 180, "keywords": ["observability", "rollouts", "openfeature", "feature-flagging"], "themes": ["Architecture & design", "Developer experience & tooling"], "tldr": "A 3-hour lab where participants add a feature to a Java/Angular app and control it with OpenFeature feature flags, including rollouts, A/B tests and observability.", "watchIf": "You want safer releases and gradual rollouts without committing to a proprietary flagging vendor.", "takeaway": "Try wrapping a risky feature behind an OpenFeature flag with a safe default value.", "videoId": null, "hearts": 67, "views": null, "likes": null, "likeRate": null, "thin": false, "rating": null, "numRatings": null, "seat": false}, {"id": 52102, "title": "Hey Belfius! Meet our new bank AI assistant—and discover how we’re building and securing AI across Belfius.", "speakers": [{"name": "Nicolas Goosse", "company": "Belfius"}], "room": "Room 3", "start": "2026-10-08T08:40:00Z", "end": "2026-10-08T09:30:00Z", "type": "Conference", "track": "Development Practices", "level": "BEGINNER", "minutes": 42, "keywords": ["banking", "artificial intelligence", "security", "personalization"], "themes": ["AI agents & agentic coding", "Security & supply chain"], "tldr": "Belfius describes the agentic harness behind its Hey Belfius assistant, then its security team covers Mythos-era vulnerability discovery, safe AI-assisted development, and live jailbreaks of a chatbot guarded only by its system prompt.", "watchIf": "You are putting an LLM assistant in front of customers in a regulated industry and want a bank's view of harness design plus a concrete demo of why system-prompt guardrails fail.", "takeaway": "Use enterprise LLM licenses with no-training guarantees; never paste company code into personal accounts.", "videoId": "4hXQoN8co80", "hearts": 126, "views": 493, "likes": 2, "likeRate": 0.68, "thin": true, "rating": null, "numRatings": null, "seat": true}, {"id": 49252, "title": "Big Runtimes Make Efficient Programs", "speakers": [{"name": "Ron Pressler", "company": "Oracle"}], "room": "Room 6", "start": "2026-10-07T14:40:00Z", "end": "2026-10-07T15:30:00Z", "type": "Conference", "track": "Java Language & Platform", "level": "INTERMEDIATE", "minutes": 50, "keywords": ["performance", "java", "runtime", "jit"], "themes": ["JVM performance & runtime", "Java language & JDK"], "tldr": "Pressler argues that Java's big runtime, with speculative JIT compilation, generational moving GCs and JFR, exists to remove the overheads low-level languages accumulate in large, evolving programs, making those programs faster in practice.", "watchIf": "You have heard that Java is slow because of its JIT and GC, or you hand-optimize Java on old folklore, and want the reasoning from the Java platform group behind why the runtime is designed this way.", "takeaway": "Profile your actual application, in development and in production with JFR, instead of trusting microbenchmarks.", "videoId": "vhDwy1eCXbs", "hearts": 141, "views": 1104, "likes": 28, "likeRate": 1.82, "thin": false, "rating": null, "numRatings": null, "seat": true}, {"id": 22933, "title": "30 minutes to build your first Vidocq app, the 100% European Sovereign Java stack with zero external dependencies", "speakers": [{"name": "Sebastien Blanc", "company": "SCIAM"}], "room": "Room 6", "start": "2026-10-06T14:50:00Z", "end": "2026-10-06T15:20:00Z", "type": "Tools-in-Action", "track": "Java Language & Platform", "level": "INTERMEDIATE", "minutes": 31, "keywords": ["vidocq", "vibecoding", "microprofile", "jakartaee"], "themes": ["Spring & frameworks", "AI agents & agentic coding"], "tldr": "Blanc live-codes a waffle-shop REST app on Vidocq, a zero-dependency Jakarta EE Core Profile and MicroProfile implementation built by coding agents from specs and TCKs, then secures it with an OIDC server built the same way.", "watchIf": "You are curious whether coding agents plus specs and TCKs can produce a working Jakarta EE runtime, and want to see what building on it looks like today.", "takeaway": "Try Vidocq for a small JAX-RS/CDI/MicroProfile service if you want a dependency-free Jakarta stack.", "videoId": "U9pOWsERF6o", "hearts": 56, "views": 897, "likes": 7, "likeRate": 0.81, "thin": true, "rating": null, "numRatings": null, "seat": true}, {"id": 22909, "title": "What rules the virtual? How our systems obey physical laws.", "speakers": [{"name": "Marianne Hoornenborg", "company": "NLJUG and KVK (Dutch Chamber of Commerce)"}], "room": "Room 10", "start": "2026-10-07T12:00:00Z", "end": "2026-10-07T12:50:00Z", "type": "Conference", "track": "Mind the Geek", "level": "INTERMEDIATE", "minutes": 49, "keywords": ["computation", "information", "quantum", "physics"], "themes": ["Hardware, robots & fun"], "tldr": "Hoornenborg goes down from 'virtual' cloud abstractions through hardware, transistors and Landauer's principle to quantum superposition and qubits, arguing that everything digital is physical and that abstractions deserve credit.", "watchIf": "You want a relaxed, non-specialist tour of the physics under your code, from transistors to qubits. There are no Java techniques here.", "takeaway": "Take apart an old laptop to see the hardware your abstractions sit on.", "videoId": "yB6FIQPgsWE", "hearts": 113, "views": 842, "likes": 4, "likeRate": 0.66, "thin": true, "rating": null, "numRatings": null, "seat": true}, {"id": 21687, "title": "10 boring reasons why Java is the best ecosystem for agentic development", "speakers": [{"name": "Ed Burns", "company": "GitHub"}], "room": "Room 5", "start": "2026-10-09T09:50:00Z", "end": "2026-10-09T10:40:00Z", "type": "Conference", "track": "Agentic Engineering & Tooling", "level": "ADVANCED", "minutes": 50, "keywords": ["observability", "java", "agents", "testing"], "themes": ["AI agents & agentic coding", "Testing & quality"], "tldr": "Burns tested Java build-time guardrails on agent-written code in a Cargo Tracker experiment; they rarely changed what agents produced, and code review stayed the last line of defence.", "watchIf": "You let coding agents change a Java code base and want measured evidence of which build guardrails catch their mistakes and which do not.", "takeaway": "Keep a code reviewer, human or agent, as the last line of defence even when every automated gate is green.", "videoId": "Qg7Uu_XBvpI", "hearts": 267, "views": 1025, "likes": 8, "likeRate": 0.81, "thin": true, "rating": null, "numRatings": null, "seat": true}, {"id": 22923, "title": "Fun with Flags: How OpenFeature Solves Your Feature Flag Headaches", "speakers": [{"name": "Simon Schrottner", "company": "Flagsmith"}], "room": "Room 8", "start": "2026-10-07T10:00:00Z", "end": "2026-10-07T10:50:00Z", "type": "Conference", "track": "Development Practices", "level": "BEGINNER", "minutes": 51, "keywords": ["vendor-agnostic", "software delivery", "feature flags", "openfeature"], "themes": ["Developer experience & tooling", "Architecture & design"], "tldr": "Schrottner introduces OpenFeature's core concepts (evaluation API, providers, evaluation context, hooks) through three common feature-flag problems: vendor lock-in, dynamic targeting and obsolete flags.", "watchIf": "You have homegrown or vendor-specific feature flags spread across several languages and want a standard API with observability built in.", "takeaway": "Code against the OpenFeature evaluation API rather than a vendor SDK, so changing flag backends is a one-line provider swap.", "videoId": "Ij5RsREpjBg", "hearts": 283, "views": 644, "likes": 12, "likeRate": 1.29, "thin": true, "rating": null, "numRatings": null, "seat": true}, {"id": 8169, "title": "How to Architect Green Software", "speakers": [{"name": "Ioannis Kolaxis", "company": "Accenture"}], "room": "Room 6", "start": "2026-10-08T08:40:00Z", "end": "2026-10-08T09:30:00Z", "type": "Conference", "track": "Architecture & Modernization", "level": "INTERMEDIATE", "minutes": 50, "keywords": ["sustainability", "trade-offs", "latency", "serverless"], "themes": ["Sustainability", "Architecture & design"], "tldr": "Kolaxis ranks software's footprint as devices, then networks, then data centers, and walks through the sustainability trade-offs at each layer, arguing AI coding agents now make several greener options affordable.", "watchIf": "You make infrastructure or mobile-platform decisions and want a structured list of where sustainability conflicts with latency, availability, cost and portability.", "takeaway": "Check which old OS versions and low-spec devices your mobile app could keep supporting, and do not drop them lightly.", "videoId": "bqwtnKLU35E", "hearts": 212, "views": 625, "likes": 7, "likeRate": 0.97, "thin": true, "rating": null, "numRatings": null, "seat": true}, {"id": 5510, "title": "Quarkus Meets Leyden at the JVM Performance Edge", "speakers": [{"name": "Georgios Andrianakis", "company": "IBM"}, {"name": "Ana-Maria Mihalceanu", "company": "Oracle"}], "room": "Room 10", "start": "2026-10-07T15:50:00Z", "end": "2026-10-07T16:40:00Z", "type": "Conference", "track": "Java Language & Platform", "level": "INTERMEDIATE", "minutes": 60, "keywords": ["optimization", "startup", "quarkus", "leyden"], "themes": ["JVM performance & runtime", "Spring & frameworks"], "tldr": "Andrianakis and Mihalceanu show how Project Leyden's AOT cache, together with Quarkus changes, took a Quarkus REST app from about 350 ms to about 80 ms startup, and what constraints apply.", "watchIf": "You run Quarkus or another JVM framework where startup time matters and want to know what Leyden gives you today and what it requires from your build and deployment.", "takeaway": "Build your Quarkus app with the AOT jar option on a recent LTS release and let the integration tests act as the training run.", "videoId": "cbSl8UVdMVg", "hearts": 89, "views": 482, "likes": 16, "likeRate": 1.76, "thin": true, "rating": null, "numRatings": null, "seat": true}, {"id": 25652, "title": "In the loop, not in the dark: staying in control in the era of dark software factories", "speakers": [{"name": "Alex Gavrilescu", "company": "Backlog.md"}], "room": "Room 6", "start": "2026-10-05T15:35:00Z", "end": "2026-10-05T16:05:00Z", "type": "Tools-in-Action", "track": "Agentic Engineering & Tooling", "level": "BEGINNER", "minutes": 29, "keywords": ["architecture", "blueprint", "kanban", "agents"], "themes": ["AI agents & agentic coding", "Architecture & design", "Developer experience & tooling"], "tldr": "After an outage in an agent-written app he couldn't debug, Gavrilescu built Groma.md: a live, Markdown-backed C4 map of a repo, so humans review architecture instead of code.", "watchIf": "You let coding agents ship most of your code and want a way to review what the system has become without reading every diff.", "takeaway": "Practise connecting to your production logs and database yourself before an incident, even on agent-built projects.", "videoId": "_dcauZC9ST8", "hearts": 125, "views": 836, "likes": 8, "likeRate": 0.9, "thin": true, "rating": null, "numRatings": null, "seat": true}, {"id": 19733, "title": "Bootiful Spring Boot 4", "speakers": [{"name": "Josh Long", "company": "the Spring team"}], "room": "Room 8", "start": "2026-10-07T13:10:00Z", "end": "2026-10-07T14:00:00Z", "type": "Conference", "track": "Architecture & Modernization", "level": "BEGINNER", "minutes": 49, "keywords": ["graalvm", "java", "virtual threads", "spring boot"], "themes": ["Spring & frameworks", "Java language & JDK", "JVM performance & runtime"], "tldr": "Josh Long live-codes a dog-and-cat Spring Boot 4.1 app on Java 27 to tour API versioning, HTTP interface clients, resilience, SSRF filtering, OpenTelemetry, Spring gRPC, BeanRegistrar, JobRunr and Leyden AOT.", "watchIf": "You maintain Spring Boot 3 apps and want a fast, demo-driven tour of what Boot 4.x and Framework 7 add before you upgrade.", "takeaway": "Set up a fast path to production so you can take Spring patch releases as soon as they ship.", "videoId": "1PtdpItmzTo", "hearts": 516, "views": 1936, "likes": 17, "likeRate": 0.87, "thin": true, "rating": null, "numRatings": null, "seat": true}, {"id": 7017, "title": "The Importance of HITL to Avoid Chaos and EU Regulations for AI", "speakers": [{"name": "Juan Antonio Breña 🇪🇸🇪🇺", "company": "IG"}], "room": "Room 8", "start": "2026-10-06T16:20:00Z", "end": "2026-10-06T16:50:00Z", "type": "Tools-in-Action", "track": "Agentic Engineering & Tooling", "level": "INTERMEDIATE", "minutes": 20, "keywords": ["hitl", "compliance", "autonomy", "microservices"], "themes": ["AI agents & agentic coding", "Security & supply chain", "Architecture & design"], "tldr": "With AI agents speeding up change in brownfield microservices, pull requests must carry evidence of consequences, go to qualified reviewers, and map to EU rules (AI Act, GDPR, CRA, NIS2, DORA).", "watchIf": "You ship AI-assisted changes to regulated microservices in the EU and want a checklist that ties PR review to the AI Act, GDPR, CRA, NIS2 and DORA.", "takeaway": "Label pull requests generated by AI agents in your pipeline.", "videoId": "t9e9FtHB-48", "hearts": 98, "views": 636, "likes": 3, "likeRate": 0.68, "thin": true, "rating": null, "numRatings": null, "seat": true}, {"id": 49256, "title": "ADRs: The Why and How", "speakers": [{"name": "Venkat Subramaniam", "company": ""}], "room": "Room 8", "start": "2026-10-08T14:30:00Z", "end": "2026-10-08T15:20:00Z", "type": "Conference", "track": "Development Practices", "level": "INTERMEDIATE", "minutes": 50, "keywords": ["documentation", "architectural", "decisions", "records"], "themes": ["Architecture & design", "Teams, careers & leadership"], "tldr": "Subramaniam argues that the reasoning behind important decisions is what code never captures, and makes the case for short, immutable, plain-text decision records kept in Git.", "watchIf": "Your team keeps inheriting decisions nobody can explain and you want a lightweight, persuasive argument for recording them.", "takeaway": "Write an ADR for your next high-impact choice, such as a framework or database, listing at least three options and their consequences.", "videoId": "wzN6qq08w0U", "hearts": 671, "views": 1290, "likes": 18, "likeRate": 1.18, "thin": true, "rating": null, "numRatings": null, "seat": true}, {"id": 5051, "title": "🧰 Dev Containers: the ultimate toolbox for developers?", "speakers": [{"name": "Stéphane Philippart", "company": "OVHcloud"}], "room": "Room 8", "start": "2026-10-06T14:50:00Z", "end": "2026-10-06T15:20:00Z", "type": "Tools-in-Action", "track": "Mind the Geek", "level": "INTERMEDIATE", "minutes": 24, "keywords": ["dependencies", "devcontainers", "configuration", "docker"], "themes": ["Developer experience & tooling"], "tldr": "Philippart shows how Dev Containers let Docker be the only thing installed on a laptop, building a Java toolchain up from a devcontainer.json to shared features, templates and prebuilt images.", "watchIf": "You onboard people onto projects with long setup instructions, or want a disposable, reproducible Java environment, and haven't used Dev Containers beyond the basics.", "takeaway": "Add a .devcontainer/devcontainer.json to one project, starting from an official template, and see whether new contributors need anything besides Docker.", "videoId": "eyJ5LO-Kyuw", "hearts": 320, "views": 3994, "likes": 26, "likeRate": 0.68, "thin": false, "rating": null, "numRatings": null, "seat": true}, {"id": 5538, "title": "Strangling the dragon: modernizing legacy systems with the Strangler Fig pattern", "speakers": [{"name": "Daniel Raneland", "company": "factor10"}], "room": "Room 8", "start": "2026-10-09T09:50:00Z", "end": "2026-10-09T10:40:00Z", "type": "Conference", "track": "Architecture & Modernization", "level": "INTERMEDIATE", "minutes": 35, "keywords": ["strangler", "rewrite", "legacy", "conway"], "themes": ["Legacy modernization", "Architecture & design"], "tldr": "Raneland argues against rewriting legacy systems and shows how to replace them piece by piece with the Strangler Fig pattern, backed by TDD, Domain-Driven Design and the Inverse Conway Manoeuvre.", "watchIf": "You maintain a long-lived legacy system and someone is proposing a big-bang rewrite.", "takeaway": "Before touching a legacy area, add tests that pin its current behaviour and make each one fail once to prove it works.", "videoId": "AmL_OCs5F4E", "hearts": 303, "views": 1480, "likes": 8, "likeRate": 0.65, "thin": true, "rating": null, "numRatings": null, "seat": true}, {"id": 7018, "title": "A Year of Agentic AI Evolution: Lessons Learned Building Production-grade Agentic Systems", "speakers": [{"name": "Mario Fusco", "company": "IBM"}, {"name": "Kevin Dubois", "company": "IBM"}], "room": "Room 8", "start": "2026-10-07T12:00:00Z", "end": "2026-10-07T12:50:00Z", "type": "Conference", "track": "Agentic Engineering & Tooling", "level": "INTERMEDIATE", "minutes": 52, "keywords": ["observability", "agentic", "orchestration", "langchain4j"], "themes": ["AI agents & agentic coding", "LLMs in Java apps", "Spring & frameworks"], "tldr": "LangChain4j's agentic module now rests on a pluggable Planner interface, so you can write and mix your own orchestration patterns; Quarkus adds fault tolerance, observability, persistence and guardrails.", "watchIf": "You build multi-agent systems in Java and need more control over orchestration, cost and production resilience than fixed workflow patterns give you.", "takeaway": "When the built-in workflows don't fit, implement the Planner interface rather than forcing a supervisor to do deterministic routing.", "videoId": "nEW0kosJjc8", "hearts": 549, "views": 956, "likes": 7, "likeRate": 0.79, "thin": true, "rating": null, "numRatings": null, "seat": true}, {"id": 24229, "title": "Building Autonomous AI Agents with Spring", "speakers": [{"name": "Timo Salm", "company": "Broadcom"}, {"name": "Sandra Ahlgrimm", "company": "Microsoft / GitHub"}], "room": "BOF 2", "start": "2026-10-05T11:30:00Z", "end": "2026-10-05T14:30:00Z", "type": "Hands-on Lab", "track": "Agentic Engineering & Tooling", "level": "INTERMEDIATE", "minutes": 180, "keywords": ["agentic", "java", "autonomous", "spring"], "themes": ["AI agents & agentic coding", "Spring & frameworks"], "tldr": "A 3-hour lab building a multi-agent system with Java, Spring AI and MCP, including chat memory, RAG and production concerns like observability and security.", "watchIf": "You are a Spring developer who wants hands-on experience building agents beyond a simple chatbot.", "takeaway": "Add observability and security considerations to agent prototypes early, not after launch.", "videoId": null, "hearts": 117, "views": null, "likes": null, "likeRate": null, "thin": false, "rating": null, "numRatings": null, "seat": false}, {"id": 51951, "title": "Agentic Engineering Reconversion", "speakers": [{"name": "Victor Rentea", "company": "Freelance"}], "room": "Room 8", "start": "2026-10-07T09:10:00Z", "end": "2026-10-07T09:30:00Z", "type": "Keynote", "track": "Agentic Engineering & Tooling", "level": "BEGINNER", "minutes": 22, "keywords": ["software", "developers", "ai", "onboarding"], "themes": ["AI agents & agentic coding", "Teams, careers & leadership"], "tldr": "Rentea's fast, comic keynote on retraining yourself as an agentic engineer: learn the basics, protect your brain from agent-driven burnout, shift to judgement, product and people skills, and force the habit.", "watchIf": "You feel stuck between agent hype and exhaustion and want one opinionated practitioner's checklist for retraining, delivered in 20 minutes.", "takeaway": "Build and use at least one skill, and learn how prompt caching and context limits affect cost and quality.", "videoId": "vAfQ4f8wIVI", "hearts": 559, "views": 6574, "likes": 58, "likeRate": 0.88, "thin": false, "rating": null, "numRatings": null, "seat": true}, {"id": 9035, "title": "Toxic Flows in MCP Servers and Skills: What Your Agent Blindly Trusts", "speakers": [{"name": "Brian Vermeer", "company": "Snyk"}], "room": "Room 6", "start": "2026-10-08T07:30:00Z", "end": "2026-10-08T08:20:00Z", "type": "Conference", "track": "Security, Trust & Compliance", "level": "INTERMEDIATE", "minutes": 50, "keywords": ["deserialization", "trust", "mcp", "skills"], "themes": ["Security & supply chain", "AI agents & agentic coding"], "tldr": "Vermeer demos five live attacks through a Java MCP server and Claude Code skills, all exploiting what agents trust, and argues pinned versions, curated inventories and automated scanning beat human approval prompts.", "watchIf": "You or your team install MCP servers and skills into coding agents, especially in auto-approve mode, and want to see concretely how they get abused.", "takeaway": "Pin MCP server versions instead of using latest-tag npx, uvx or JBang install lines, and rescan every update.", "videoId": "c4F7Iy3XqaY", "hearts": 357, "views": 1315, "likes": 16, "likeRate": 1.07, "thin": true, "rating": null, "numRatings": null, "seat": true}, {"id": 22912, "title": "Loop Engineering, beyond prompt, context, and harness engineering", "speakers": [{"name": "Guillaume Laforge", "company": "Google"}, {"name": "Wietse Venema", "company": "Google Cloud"}], "room": "Room 8", "start": "2026-10-06T07:30:00Z", "end": "2026-10-06T10:30:00Z", "type": "Deep Dive", "track": "Agentic Engineering & Tooling", "level": "INTERMEDIATE", "minutes": 149, "keywords": ["codingagents", "loopengineering", "workflows", "automation"], "themes": ["AI agents & agentic coding", "Developer experience & tooling"], "tldr": "Laforge and Venema define loop engineering as designing triggers, goals, checkers, state and stop conditions around coding agents, and demo maker-checker, goal, scheduled and Ralph Wiggum loops built with Antigravity.", "watchIf": "You already use a coding agent interactively and want concrete patterns, guardrails and failure modes for letting it run unattended on goals, schedules or events.", "takeaway": "Pick one repetitive task (release notes, doc sync, dependency updates) and turn it into a scheduled or goal-based loop with an explicit, verifiable exit condition.", "videoId": "A7ZIf8LkSvE", "hearts": 439, "views": 1107, "likes": 7, "likeRate": 0.72, "thin": true, "rating": null, "numRatings": null, "seat": true}, {"id": 7008, "title": "How to survive and thrive as a dev (team) in the exponential age of AI. Real-world AI adoption, smarter ways of working, and lessons from the front lines", "speakers": [{"name": "Sander Hoogendoorn", "company": "ibood.com"}], "room": "Room 5", "start": "2026-10-07T13:10:00Z", "end": "2026-10-07T14:00:00Z", "type": "Conference", "track": "Agentic Engineering & Tooling", "level": "INTERMEDIATE", "minutes": 52, "keywords": ["microteams", "architecture", "ai", "adaptability"], "themes": ["Teams, careers & leadership", "AI agents & agentic coding"], "tldr": "iBOOD's CTO describes how his 13-person team works with AI and without Scrum, sprints, branches, pull requests or code reviews, deploying around 100 times a day through small steps and autonomous micro-teams.", "watchIf": "You lead a small product team and want a provocative, experience-based account of dropping Scrum and code reviews while scaling up AI-assisted development.", "takeaway": "Filter incoming work against explicit goals and admit that the \"someday maybe\" column is a bin.", "videoId": "BLBwQpczk7M", "hearts": 714, "views": 824, "likes": 5, "likeRate": 0.73, "thin": true, "rating": null, "numRatings": null, "seat": true}, {"id": 14457, "title": "Coder, Coach, Catalyst - using questions to make people grow", "speakers": [{"name": "Martin Mazur", "company": "Mazur & Co"}], "room": "Room 5", "start": "2026-10-08T08:40:00Z", "end": "2026-10-08T09:30:00Z", "type": "Conference", "track": "People, Practices & Career", "level": "INTERMEDIATE", "minutes": 52, "keywords": ["questions", "growth", "listening", "coaching"], "themes": ["Teams, careers & leadership"], "tldr": "Mazur gives senior technologists a coaching starter kit (active listening, three coaching formats and five question types) to stop absorbing everyone's problems and help colleagues grow instead.", "watchIf": "You are a senior developer or tech lead who has become the person everyone brings their problems to, and you are running out of time.", "takeaway": "Next time someone says 'can you help?', reply with 'What's on your mind?' and 'And what else?' before offering anything.", "videoId": "yhk1qvy6A_4", "hearts": 410, "views": 718, "likes": 7, "likeRate": 0.91, "thin": true, "rating": null, "numRatings": null, "seat": true}, {"id": 9029, "title": "From a startup stack to 5 microseconds: inside a crypto trading engine", "speakers": [{"name": "Oleg Lobanov", "company": "Bitvavo"}], "room": "Room 4", "start": "2026-10-08T15:40:00Z", "end": "2026-10-08T16:30:00Z", "type": "Conference", "track": "Architecture & Modernization", "level": "ADVANCED", "minutes": 45, "keywords": ["deterministic", "consensus", "trading", "latency"], "themes": ["Architecture & design", "JVM performance & runtime", "Legacy modernization"], "tldr": "Lobanov explains how Bitvavo replaced a Redis/MySQL-centred exchange core with a deterministic, Raft-replicated state machine on Aeron, reaching flat sub-millisecond P99 and 5-microsecond order processing.", "watchIf": "You build latency-sensitive or audit-heavy systems and want a concrete account of event-sourced, deterministic design on the JVM, including its operational costs.", "takeaway": "If you need replayable, auditable processing, consider a single-threaded state machine over an ordered log instead of database-centred concurrency.", "videoId": "vcL6eCAwLwY", "hearts": 140, "views": 408, "likes": 2, "likeRate": 0.73, "thin": true, "rating": null, "numRatings": null, "seat": true}, {"id": 17783, "title": "Building the Trusted Agent Ecosystem", "speakers": [{"name": "Ivan Petrov", "company": "Google DeepMind"}, {"name": "Patrick McGrath", "company": "Google DeepMind"}], "room": "Room 4", "start": "2026-10-05T16:20:00Z", "end": "2026-10-05T16:50:00Z", "type": "Tools-in-Action", "track": "Security, Trust & Compliance", "level": "INTERMEDIATE", "minutes": 35, "keywords": ["agentic", "trusted", "binarytransparency", "confidentialcomputing"], "themes": ["Security & supply chain", "AI agents & agentic coding"], "tldr": "Petrov and McGrath show how Google's open-source Project Oak uses confidential computing, remote attestation and binary transparency to let users and agents verify the tools, models and agents that handle their data.", "watchIf": "You build agents or MCP services that handle personal data and want verifiable guarantees about where that data goes, rather than policy promises.", "takeaway": "Map every component your agent sends user data to (MCP tools, inference, other agents) and decide which ones need verifiable guarantees rather than policy promises.", "videoId": "dWF5HMkKiLg", "hearts": 87, "views": 680, "likes": 9, "likeRate": 1.06, "thin": true, "rating": null, "numRatings": null, "seat": true}, {"id": 23531, "title": "We created our own AI orchestrator and it became so much more!", "speakers": [{"name": "Ricky van Rijn", "company": "MetaFactory"}], "room": "Room 9", "start": "2026-10-09T09:50:00Z", "end": "2026-10-09T10:40:00Z", "type": "Conference", "track": "Development Practices", "level": "INTERMEDIATE", "minutes": 52, "keywords": ["agentic", "requirements", "orchestration", "automation"], "themes": ["AI agents & agentic coding", "Teams, careers & leadership"], "tldr": "Van Rijn tells how MetaFactory's home-built agent orchestrator on Embabel pushed the team from huge AI pull requests and siloed local agents to small shared specs and day-long sprints.", "watchIf": "You lead a team adopting coding agents and want an account of how specs, reviews and roles changed when agents did the coding.", "takeaway": "Keep agent definitions, skills and MCP configuration in one shared repository rather than on each developer's machine.", "videoId": "niBYuCuViGw", "hearts": 284, "views": 4680, "likes": 25, "likeRate": 0.58, "thin": false, "rating": null, "numRatings": null, "seat": true}, {"id": 24244, "title": "Green Maven builds: sustainable CI in practice", "speakers": [{"name": "Jan Boonen", "company": "the/experts."}], "room": "Room 6", "start": "2026-10-08T10:55:00Z", "end": "2026-10-08T11:35:00Z", "type": "Lunch Talk", "track": "Development Practices", "level": "INTERMEDIATE", "minutes": 40, "keywords": ["maven", "energy", "githubactions", "ci"], "themes": ["Sustainability", "Developer experience & tooling"], "tldr": "Boonen shows low-effort Maven and GitLab CI settings (parallel JUnit, quieter logs, no recompilation between jobs, workflow rules) that make builds faster and leaner, and measures them with Eco CI.", "watchIf": "You maintain Maven builds on GitLab or GitHub CI and want concrete settings that cut build time and energy without a rewrite.", "takeaway": "Add a junit-platform.properties with concurrent parallel execution and fix any tests that break.", "videoId": "Mdu5_gndYn0", "hearts": 173, "views": 497, "likes": 4, "likeRate": 0.83, "thin": true, "rating": null, "numRatings": null, "seat": true}, {"id": 17782, "title": "Is the IDE dead ?", "speakers": [{"name": "Martin Lippert", "company": "Broadcom"}], "room": "Room 8", "start": "2026-10-08T10:55:00Z", "end": "2026-10-08T11:35:00Z", "type": "Lunch Talk", "track": "Agentic Engineering & Tooling", "level": "INTERMEDIATE", "minutes": 35, "keywords": ["ai", "terminal", "ide", "agents"], "themes": ["Developer experience & tooling", "AI agents & agentic coding"], "tldr": "Lippert argues the IDE is not dying but splitting into three tools: review tools for high-volume agent changes, managers for fleets of agents, and headless language smartness serving agents.", "watchIf": "You wonder where your IDE fits once agents write most of the code, or you build developer tooling and want a toolsmith's view of the shift.", "takeaway": "Connect a language server plugin for your stack to your coding agent so it gets fast compile and navigation feedback instead of guessing.", "videoId": "jFxR6YYWdmI", "hearts": 432, "views": 1901, "likes": 15, "likeRate": 0.81, "thin": true, "rating": null, "numRatings": null, "seat": true}, {"id": 22905, "title": "When ORM Becomes OMG: Performance Pitfalls in JPA and Friends", "speakers": [{"name": "Jos Roseboom", "company": "EasingYou"}], "room": "Room 9", "start": "2026-10-08T08:40:00Z", "end": "2026-10-08T09:30:00Z", "type": "Conference", "track": "Cloud Native, Data & Infrastructure", "level": "INTERMEDIATE", "minutes": 51, "keywords": ["jpa", "performance", "orm", "transactions"], "themes": ["Data & databases", "Spring & frameworks", "JVM performance & runtime"], "tldr": "Using a toy 'Fun Fact Store', Roseboom shows how Spring Boot and Hibernate defaults hold pooled connections too long and fire hidden queries, and which settings and query styles fix it.", "watchIf": "You run Spring Data JPA in production and see connection pool exhaustion, N+1 queries or unexplained latency.", "takeaway": "Set spring.jpa.open-in-view=false in every Spring Boot app and check what breaks.", "videoId": "ePBmAdRYl10", "hearts": 268, "views": 746, "likes": 13, "likeRate": 1.27, "thin": true, "rating": null, "numRatings": null, "seat": true}, {"id": 7006, "title": "Prompt It Right: Spring Boot Testing in the AI Era", "speakers": [{"name": "Philip Riecks", "company": "PragmaTech GmbH"}], "room": "Room 9", "start": "2026-10-05T07:30:00Z", "end": "2026-10-05T10:30:00Z", "type": "Deep Dive", "track": "Development Practices", "level": "BEGINNER", "minutes": 153, "keywords": ["testcontainers", "ai", "springboot", "testing"], "themes": ["Testing & quality", "Spring & frameworks", "AI agents & agentic coding"], "tldr": "Riecks argues that with AI writing tests, verification is the bottleneck: know unit, slice and integration tests, keep the suite fast, and encode testing standards in agent skills.", "watchIf": "You let an AI agent write Spring Boot tests and want to judge whether they prove anything, or your CI build is slow because of Spring context restarts.", "takeaway": "Inspect your test suite's context reuse with Spring's cache logging or Spring Test Profiler, consolidate on one abstract integration test base class, and remove @DirtiesContext where it is not needed.", "videoId": "d6D8nef-32c", "hearts": 168, "views": 1671, "likes": 12, "likeRate": 0.76, "thin": true, "rating": null, "numRatings": null, "seat": true}, {"id": 5549, "title": "Underneath the Black Marker: AI Techniques for Detecting and Reversing Redactions", "speakers": [{"name": "David vonThenen", "company": "NetApp"}], "room": "Room 10", "start": "2026-10-08T13:00:00Z", "end": "2026-10-08T13:50:00Z", "type": "Conference", "track": "Security, Trust & Compliance", "level": "INTERMEDIATE", "minutes": 50, "keywords": ["computervision", "pdf", "privacy", "redaction"], "themes": ["Security & supply chain", "Hardware, robots & fun"], "tldr": "Using the botched US Department of Justice releases as a case study, vonThenen demos how PDF, email and pixelation redactions get reversed, ending with his own blur-reading OCR model.", "watchIf": "You publish redacted documents, screenshots or video, or build privacy tooling, and want to see the concrete ways visual hiding gets reversed.", "takeaway": "Redact by deleting content, not by drawing shapes over it, and verify by extracting text from the exported PDF.", "videoId": "wJ7R4qkVs8Y", "hearts": 64, "views": 280, "likes": 2, "likeRate": 0.81, "thin": true, "rating": null, "numRatings": null, "seat": true}, {"id": 21152, "title": "Beyond system.out.println: Applying a Debugging Mindset at Scale", "speakers": [{"name": "Cees Bos", "company": "OpenValue"}], "room": "Room 5", "start": "2026-10-06T10:40:00Z", "end": "2026-10-06T11:20:00Z", "type": "Lunch Talk", "track": "Development Practices", "level": "BEGINNER", "minutes": 36, "keywords": ["observability", "debugging", "grafana", "opentelemetry"], "themes": ["Developer experience & tooling", "Cloud native & Kubernetes"], "tldr": "Bos uses three real production incidents (a saturated load balancer, a slow ingress, a JMS server freezing on LDAP timeouts) to show how curiosity, data and proved hypotheses find bugs nobody owns.", "watchIf": "You get pulled into incidents spanning many services and teams and want a practical method for finding root causes that sit in someone else's component.", "takeaway": "Map every component a request touches, including load balancers, ingress, egress and auth servers, and learn how they behave under the hood.", "videoId": "5VjB4t8aFWE", "hearts": 141, "views": 582, "likes": 4, "likeRate": 0.78, "thin": true, "rating": null, "numRatings": null, "seat": true}, {"id": 22914, "title": "Java is for Data Science, Too: Building an End-to-End ML Pipeline Without Leaving the JVM", "speakers": [{"name": "Sven Reimers", "company": "Airbus"}, {"name": "Zoran Sevarac", "company": "Deep Netts "}], "room": "BOF 1", "start": "2026-10-05T08:30:00Z", "end": "2026-10-05T10:30:00Z", "type": "Hands-on Lab", "track": "Java Language & Platform", "level": "BEGINNER", "minutes": 120, "keywords": ["machinelearning", "java", "dataframes", "deeplearning"], "themes": ["Data & databases", "Java language & JDK"], "tldr": "A 2-hour hands-on lab building an end-to-end ML pipeline in pure Java using JTaccuino notebooks, Hardwood, DFLib, DeepNetts and an early look at gog4j.", "watchIf": "You are a Java developer curious about data science and machine learning without switching to Python.", "takeaway": "Try DFLib and a Java notebook for your next data exploration task.", "videoId": null, "hearts": 50, "views": null, "likes": null, "likeRate": null, "thin": false, "rating": null, "numRatings": null, "seat": false}, {"id": 50108, "title": "Introduction to Spring AI", "speakers": [{"name": "Timo Salm", "company": "Broadcom"}], "room": "Room 4", "start": "2026-10-05T10:40:00Z", "end": "2026-10-05T11:20:00Z", "type": "Lunch Talk", "track": "Agentic Engineering & Tooling", "level": "BEGINNER", "minutes": 43, "keywords": ["spring ai", "rag", "genai", "mcp"], "themes": ["LLMs in Java apps", "Spring & frameworks"], "tldr": "Salm walks through Spring AI's core building blocks (ChatModel and ChatClient, structured output, the Advisor API, RAG, @Tool and MCP) as the harness that compensates for what LLMs cannot do alone.", "watchIf": "You are a Spring developer who has not used Spring AI yet and want a compact tour of its core APIs before building RAG or agentic features.", "takeaway": "Learn ChatClient and the Advisor API first; most other Spring AI features are built on them.", "videoId": "JzFelLDiVJQ", "hearts": 143, "views": 1122, "likes": 9, "likeRate": 0.82, "thin": true, "rating": null, "numRatings": null, "seat": true}, {"id": 16414, "title": "From vibe coding to harness engineering: modernizing a regulated environment with AI", "speakers": [{"name": "Anyul Rivas", "company": "GFT"}], "room": "Room 5", "start": "2026-10-08T07:30:00Z", "end": "2026-10-08T08:20:00Z", "type": "Conference", "track": "Agentic Engineering & Tooling", "level": "INTERMEDIATE", "minutes": 41, "keywords": ["specification", "ai", "validation", "harnesses"], "themes": ["AI agents & agentic coding", "Legacy modernization", "Testing & quality"], "tldr": "A bank mainframe-to-Kafka proof of concept was vibe-coded to a demo, then made production-grade by surrounding the agents with deterministic harnesses: build enforcers, OpenRewrite, ArchUnit, scripts and token discipline.", "watchIf": "You need AI-generated code to meet a regulated enterprise's standards and want concrete harness techniques that worked under tight tooling restrictions.", "takeaway": "Turn each recurring agent mistake into a build failure with an actionable message instead of adding another prompt instruction.", "videoId": "zEUSFvDOiCo", "hearts": 537, "views": 3657, "likes": 24, "likeRate": 0.69, "thin": false, "rating": null, "numRatings": null, "seat": true}, {"id": 23539, "title": "Everyday JSON", "speakers": [{"name": "Viktor Klang", "company": "Oracle"}, {"name": "Alan Bateman", "company": "Oracle"}], "room": "Room 6", "start": "2026-10-08T14:30:00Z", "end": "2026-10-08T15:20:00Z", "type": "Conference", "track": "Java Language & Platform", "level": "INTERMEDIATE", "minutes": 51, "keywords": ["java", "data-oriented programming", "api", "json"], "themes": ["Java language & JDK"], "tldr": "Klang and Bateman present JEP 540, a small, strict, immutable JSON API proposed as an incubator for JDK 28, aimed at one-off scripts, configs, tests and the JDK's own tooling rather than replacing Jackson.", "watchIf": "You write small Java tools, tests or scripts that touch JSON and want to know what the built-in API will look like and why it is so strict.", "takeaway": "Download the JEP 540 early-access build and try it on a one-off script or JShell session instead of adding a Jackson dependency.", "videoId": "5y_82lCevHg", "hearts": 227, "views": 628, "likes": 10, "likeRate": 1.17, "thin": true, "rating": null, "numRatings": null, "seat": true}, {"id": 9036, "title": "My old trains have a second life, with TinyGo!", "speakers": [{"name": "Florian FORESTIER", "company": "Zenika"}, {"name": "Justine Bachelard", "company": "BeYs Cloud"}], "room": "Room 4", "start": "2026-10-09T09:50:00Z", "end": "2026-10-09T10:40:00Z", "type": "Conference", "track": "Mind the Geek", "level": "BEGINNER", "minutes": 40, "keywords": ["digital conversion", "model trains", "bluetooth", "embedded systems"], "themes": ["Hardware, robots & fun"], "tldr": "Forestier and Bachelard retrace converting family HO trains to digital control, from Raspberry Pi Pico boards over Wi-Fi to Seeed Studio nRF52840 boards over Bluetooth LE, written in TinyGo.", "watchIf": "You want a beginner-friendly embedded story of TinyGo on microcontrollers, with the hardware mistakes and the move from Wi-Fi REST to Bluetooth LE explained.", "takeaway": "Check that TinyGo supports a board, including its radio, before ordering hardware.", "videoId": "LIGVpEuIrrY", "hearts": 79, "views": 722, "likes": 2, "likeRate": 0.58, "thin": true, "rating": null, "numRatings": null, "seat": true}, {"id": 19708, "title": "Choose your own adventure in agentic design patterns", "speakers": [{"name": "Guillaume Laforge", "company": "Google"}], "room": "Room 7", "start": "2026-10-08T14:30:00Z", "end": "2026-10-08T15:20:00Z", "type": "Conference", "track": "Agentic Engineering & Tooling", "level": "ADVANCED", "minutes": 52, "keywords": ["routing", "reflection", "planning", "agents"], "themes": ["AI agents & agentic coding", "LLMs in Java apps"], "tldr": "Laforge walks through agentic design patterns (programmatic planning, RAG, progressive disclosure, hierarchical decomposition, LLM-as-judge, goal-oriented planning, agent loops) with Java demos in LangChain4j and ADK for Java.", "watchIf": "You build agents in Java and want a catalogue of when to use workflows, sub-agents, judges or goal-based planning, with code for each.", "takeaway": "Reach for plain programmatic orchestration before an agent framework when the steps are known in advance.", "videoId": "w2RTFWet_6c", "hearts": 309, "views": 540, "likes": 2, "likeRate": 0.66, "thin": true, "rating": null, "numRatings": null, "seat": true}, {"id": 8164, "title": "The pizza party of the future 🍕 software teams and product value in the age of coding agents", "speakers": [{"name": "Katie Clark", "company": "Comsysto Reply"}, {"name": "Simon Rohrer", "company": "Saxo Bank"}], "room": "Room 6", "start": "2026-10-07T12:00:00Z", "end": "2026-10-07T12:50:00Z", "type": "Conference", "track": "People, Practices & Career", "level": "BEGINNER", "minutes": 51, "keywords": ["teams", "software", "ai", "agile"], "themes": ["Teams, careers & leadership", "AI agents & agentic coding"], "tldr": "Clark and Rohrer use 70 years of software history to argue that developers won't disappear with coding agents, that Taylorist de-skilling is the real risk, and that team size still depends on context.", "watchIf": "You lead or shape software teams and want historically grounded arguments against 'developers are obsolete' and 'just write the perfect spec' thinking.", "takeaway": "Decide per change whether it is local or systemic, conventional or novel, before handing it to a spec-driven agent pipeline.", "videoId": "Tc2RFLa0ip8", "hearts": 291, "views": 989, "likes": 9, "likeRate": 0.88, "thin": true, "rating": null, "numRatings": null, "seat": true}, {"id": 10023, "title": "You thought technical debt was bad? Meet design debt!", "speakers": [{"name": "Julien Topçu", "company": "Shodo Group"}, {"name": "Josian Chevalier", "company": "Shodo"}], "room": "Room 8", "start": "2026-10-09T08:40:00Z", "end": "2026-10-09T09:30:00Z", "type": "Conference", "track": "Development Practices", "level": "INTERMEDIATE", "minutes": 50, "keywords": ["architecture", "microservices", "design debt", "technical debt"], "themes": ["Architecture & design", "Teams, careers & leadership"], "tldr": "In a Lord of the Rings-styled dialogue, Topçu and Chevalier show how reusing one domain model for unrelated problems creates design debt that spreads into services, processes and organisation.", "watchIf": "Your services keep breaking each other or need coordinated releases, and you want vocabulary and heuristics to trace that back to the domain model.", "takeaway": "When a feature request arrives, ask whether it is a new problem that deserves its own model instead of adding fields or booleans to an existing one.", "videoId": "SoWgxHeAPGU", "hearts": 509, "views": 1142, "likes": 14, "likeRate": 1.07, "thin": true, "rating": null, "numRatings": null, "seat": true}, {"id": 5503, "title": "AI without spaghetti: Clean architecture in the age of AI", "speakers": [{"name": "Maarten Vandeperre", "company": "Red Hat"}, {"name": "Lutske van der Meer", "company": "Craftsmen"}], "room": "Room 9", "start": "2026-10-08T07:30:00Z", "end": "2026-10-08T08:20:00Z", "type": "Conference", "track": "Development Practices", "level": "BEGINNER", "minutes": 46, "keywords": ["refactoring", "cleanarchitecture", "llm", "scalability"], "themes": ["Architecture & design", "AI agents & agentic coding", "LLMs in Java apps"], "tldr": "Van der Meer and Vandeperre vibe-code a recipe app from static HTML to an AI-powered Quarkus app, then refactor it to clean architecture enforced by ArchUnit tests that agents can't edit.", "watchIf": "You're letting coding agents build features and want a concrete way to keep the architecture from decaying.", "takeaway": "Add ArchUnit tests that forbid framework dependencies in your domain package and run them in CI.", "videoId": "E--oDq3X74E", "hearts": 262, "views": 3423, "likes": 15, "likeRate": 0.52, "thin": true, "rating": null, "numRatings": null, "seat": true}, {"id": 8196, "title": "Testing Architecture Made Simple", "speakers": [{"name": "Gunter Rotsaert", "company": "ICT TriOpSys"}], "room": "Room 8", "start": "2026-10-05T16:20:00Z", "end": "2026-10-05T16:50:00Z", "type": "Tools-in-Action", "track": "Architecture & Modernization", "level": "BEGINNER", "minutes": 23, "keywords": ["architecture", "taikai", "archunit", "testing"], "themes": ["Architecture & design", "Testing & quality"], "tldr": "Rotsaert shows how ArchUnit turns architecture rules into JUnit tests and how the Taikai library on top of it provides ready-made, readable rules for Java and Spring projects.", "watchIf": "Your architecture diagrams no longer match the code and you want a quick, low-effort way to enforce layering and conventions in CI.", "takeaway": "Copy the ready-made Taikai test (or the Spring Boot starter example) from its documentation into a project and run it.", "videoId": "asAxisu6EQ0", "hearts": 174, "views": 1369, "likes": 16, "likeRate": 1.05, "thin": true, "rating": null, "numRatings": null, "seat": true}, {"id": 16408, "title": "10 Billion Records vs. The Regulator: An Architectural Post-Mortem of an 11TB Exadata Failure", "speakers": [{"name": "Martin Ladecky", "company": "Commerzbank"}], "room": "Room 9", "start": "2026-10-09T08:40:00Z", "end": "2026-10-09T09:30:00Z", "type": "Conference", "track": "Architecture & Modernization", "level": "ADVANCED", "minutes": 49, "keywords": ["migration", "performance", "compliance", "database"], "themes": ["Data & databases", "Architecture & design", "Legacy modernization"], "tldr": "Ladecky recounts how a cold Exadata cache after a failover exposed a costly read-side deduplication query at Commerzbank, and the tactical fix, write-side redesign and planned PostgreSQL exit.", "watchIf": "You run a large regulated Oracle system and want a candid incident story, from first aid to a migration plan that avoids CDC.", "takeaway": "Check whether your monitoring would catch slow successes, not just errors, and verify what users actually experience.", "videoId": "TB45qqp-OxM", "hearts": 213, "views": 3924, "likes": 12, "likeRate": 0.4, "thin": true, "rating": null, "numRatings": null, "seat": true}, {"id": 14452, "title": "Quixotic: Pure Java Inference at Native Speed", "speakers": [{"name": "Alfonso² Peterssen", "company": "Quixotic AI"}], "room": "Room 4", "start": "2026-10-08T09:50:00Z", "end": "2026-10-08T10:40:00Z", "type": "Conference", "track": "Agentic Engineering & Tooling", "level": "INTERMEDIATE", "minutes": 48, "keywords": ["java", "sovereignty", "ai", "jvm"], "themes": ["LLMs in Java apps", "JVM performance & runtime"], "tldr": "Peterssen presents Quixotic's pure-Java inference engine, a 3.3 MB jar that runs 17 open model families on the CPU and that, by his benchmarks, matches llama.cpp on decode and beats it on prefill.", "watchIf": "You want local LLM, speech or embedding inference inside a Java application without Python or native servers, and want to see how fast pure Java can be on a CPU.", "takeaway": "Try running a small open model in-process from a JBang script before adding a Python sidecar or remote API to a Java service.", "videoId": "S7x4DxwRnk4", "hearts": 78, "views": 1855, "likes": 30, "likeRate": 1.38, "thin": false, "rating": null, "numRatings": null, "seat": true}, {"id": 4098, "title": "From Developer to Builder: A Spec-Driven Development Deep Dive", "speakers": [{"name": "Simon Martinelli", "company": "Martinelli GmbH"}], "room": "Room 8", "start": "2026-10-05T11:30:00Z", "end": "2026-10-05T14:30:00Z", "type": "Deep Dive", "track": "Agentic Engineering & Tooling", "level": "INTERMEDIATE", "minutes": 157, "keywords": ["brownfield projects", "specifications", "spec-driven development", "ai coding agents"], "themes": ["AI agents & agentic coding", "Legacy modernization", "Testing & quality"], "tldr": "Martinelli walks through his AI Unified Process: use cases and entity models kept in the repo as the source of truth, a stack-specific harness, deterministic sensors, and reverse engineering for brownfield modernisation.", "watchIf": "You build long-lived business applications or modernise legacy Java systems and want a stakeholder-readable spec process with concrete harness and traceability techniques.", "takeaway": "Turn every rule you would check in a pull request into an ArchUnit test, linter rule or repo-reading JUnit test instead of a CLAUDE.md line.", "videoId": "z8lal-Yt_04", "hearts": 536, "views": 3247, "likes": 46, "likeRate": 1.3, "thin": false, "rating": null, "numRatings": null, "seat": true}, {"id": 21657, "title": "Reflect, Transform, Accelerate: Bringing GPU Computing to Java", "speakers": [{"name": "Juan Fumero", "company": "Java Platform Group - Oracle"}], "room": "Room 4", "start": "2026-10-08T08:40:00Z", "end": "2026-10-08T09:30:00Z", "type": "Conference", "track": "Java Language & Platform", "level": "INTERMEDIATE", "minutes": 50, "keywords": ["cuda", "reflection", "parallelism", "gpu"], "themes": ["Java language & JDK", "JVM performance & runtime", "LLMs in Java apps"], "tldr": "Fumero shows how HAT, built on Project Babylon's code reflection, JIT-compiles plain Java into CUDA and OpenCL kernels, and how its thread, tensor and tile APIs approach cuBLAS-level matrix multiply.", "watchIf": "You want to see how close plain Java can now get to native GPU performance, and the abstractions Project Babylon proposes for getting there.", "takeaway": "If you need GPU speed now and accept vendor lock-in, call native libraries such as cuBLAS through the FFM API.", "videoId": "0-1IGpVrFLQ", "hearts": 131, "views": 760, "likes": 19, "likeRate": 1.64, "thin": true, "rating": null, "numRatings": null, "seat": true}, {"id": 11558, "title": "BootUI, the missing developer console for Spring Boot and Quarkus", "speakers": [{"name": "Julien Dubois", "company": "GitHub"}], "room": "Room 9", "start": "2026-10-06T14:50:00Z", "end": "2026-10-06T15:20:00Z", "type": "Tools-in-Action", "track": "Development Practices", "level": "BEGINNER", "minutes": 30, "keywords": ["memory usage", "quarkus", "developer console", "spring boot"], "themes": ["Developer experience & tooling", "Spring & frameworks", "AI agents & agentic coding"], "tldr": "Dubois demos BootUI, a drop-in jar that adds 400+ rule checks and a live runtime console to Spring Boot and Quarkus apps, and argues its real users are AI coding agents.", "watchIf": "You build Spring Boot or Quarkus apps and want runtime insight into transactions, SQL and exceptions that you or a coding agent can act on.", "takeaway": "Add the BootUI dependency to a real Spring Boot or Quarkus app in development and run the scorecard.", "videoId": "NkstzPeo400", "hearts": 95, "views": 3209, "likes": 43, "likeRate": 1.24, "thin": false, "rating": null, "numRatings": null, "seat": true}, {"id": 44601, "title": "Agents Shouldn't Control Control Flow", "speakers": [{"name": "Jeremy Adams", "company": "Neo4j"}], "room": "Room 5", "start": "2026-10-08T10:55:00Z", "end": "2026-10-08T11:35:00Z", "type": "Lunch Talk", "track": "Agentic Engineering & Tooling", "level": "BEGINNER", "minutes": 40, "keywords": ["agentic", "workflow", "compiler", "context-graph"], "themes": ["AI agents & agentic coding", "Architecture & design", "Data & databases"], "tldr": "Adams argues that once agents have found a working path, it should be distilled from a Neo4j context graph into deterministic code, leaving the LLM only the final judgement or summary.", "watchIf": "You run tool-calling agents on repeatable business processes and want them auditable, consistent and cheaper.", "takeaway": "Evaluate your agent runs and turn the consistently successful tool sequences into deterministic code or at least a skill file.", "videoId": "oey2TbFvfWE", "hearts": 101, "views": 772, "likes": 2, "likeRate": 0.56, "thin": true, "rating": null, "numRatings": null, "seat": true}, {"id": 23529, "title": "From Autocomplete to Autopilot: How AI Is Changing the Developer Experience", "speakers": [{"name": "Tugdual Grall", "company": "GitHub"}], "room": "Room 8", "start": "2026-10-05T07:30:00Z", "end": "2026-10-05T10:30:00Z", "type": "Deep Dive", "track": "Agentic Engineering & Tooling", "level": "INTERMEDIATE", "minutes": 182, "keywords": ["security", "governance", "copilot", "automation"], "themes": ["AI agents & agentic coding", "Developer experience & tooling", "Security & supply chain"], "tldr": "A three-hour, demo-heavy tour of Grall's move from Copilot completions to parallel autopilot sessions in the Copilot CLI and app, covering cost, review, security and governance.", "watchIf": "Your team uses GitHub Copilot mostly for completions and chat, and you want to see the CLI, desktop app, cloud agents and admin controls in real use.", "takeaway": "Ask your Copilot admin to enable the CLI and Copilot code review if they are blocked, and add review rules to your instruction file.", "videoId": "pMv_39iSXVA", "hearts": 386, "views": 1397, "likes": 6, "likeRate": 0.59, "thin": true, "rating": null, "numRatings": null, "seat": true}, {"id": 22947, "title": "High-Performance Java-Native AI with CUDA and the Vector API", "speakers": [{"name": "Zoran Sevarac", "company": "Deep Netts "}], "room": "Room 7", "start": "2026-10-08T08:40:00Z", "end": "2026-10-08T09:30:00Z", "type": "Conference", "track": "Java Language & Platform", "level": "ADVANCED", "minutes": 49, "keywords": ["java", "vector", "ffm", "gpu"], "themes": ["Java language & JDK", "LLMs in Java apps", "JVM performance & runtime"], "tldr": "Sevarac explains how Deep Netts calls CUDA through jextract-generated FFM bindings and falls back to the Vector API on CPU, with fraud-detection benchmarks showing where each pays off.", "watchIf": "You want to call native GPU libraries from Java with FFM instead of JNI, or need to know whether the Vector API is enough for your ML workload.", "takeaway": "Before buying GPUs for a small model, benchmark it on CPU with the Vector API.", "videoId": "8NURE3I4AJQ", "hearts": 130, "views": 1671, "likes": 15, "likeRate": 0.88, "thin": true, "rating": null, "numRatings": null, "seat": true}, {"id": 23524, "title": "Modern Java in Practice - The Features That Matter", "speakers": [{"name": "Nicolai Parlog", "company": "Oracle"}, {"name": "Falk Sippach", "company": "embarc Software Consulting GmbH"}], "room": "Room 5", "start": "2026-10-05T11:30:00Z", "end": "2026-10-05T14:30:00Z", "type": "Deep Dive", "track": "Java Language & Platform", "level": "INTERMEDIATE", "minutes": 161, "keywords": ["java", "structuredconcurrency", "patternmatching", "virtualthreads"], "themes": ["Java language & JDK", "JVM performance & runtime"], "tldr": "Parlog and Sippach pick the Java 18-28 features that matter most, from data-oriented programming and the AOT cache to structured concurrency, post-quantum TLS, gatherers and JFR, and explain when to use each.", "watchIf": "You are moving from Java 17 or 21 to 25 or later and want a guided tour of which features change how you write, run and secure code, with enough detail to start using them.", "takeaway": "Upgrade to the latest JDK before any manual tuning, then measure with compact object headers and generational ZGC turned on.", "videoId": "zC2tbq7S5UE", "hearts": 364, "views": 3739, "likes": 48, "likeRate": 1.21, "thin": false, "rating": null, "numRatings": null, "seat": true}, {"id": 52103, "title": "The Devoxx Robot Games", "speakers": [{"name": "Stephan Janssen", "company": "Devoxx"}], "room": "Room 10", "start": "2026-10-08T10:55:00Z", "end": "2026-10-08T11:35:00Z", "type": "Lunch Talk", "track": "Mind the Geek", "level": "BEGINNER", "minutes": 41, "keywords": ["robot games", "artificial intelligence", "live demos", "game development"], "themes": ["Hardware, robots & fun", "AI agents & agentic coding"], "tldr": "Nine builders each get four minutes to demo browser games starring the Devoxx robots, built with AI assistants, and to say where the AI helped or failed.", "watchIf": "You want a light lunch session showing how developers who are not game developers used coding agents to build working games, and where the AI fell short.", "takeaway": "Describe spatial layouts to a coding agent in text, zone by zone, rather than expecting it to read floor plans or photos.", "videoId": "NT0wyPM58G8", "hearts": 179, "views": 523, "likes": 5, "likeRate": 0.89, "thin": true, "rating": null, "numRatings": null, "seat": true}, {"id": 49257, "title": "Embabel Agent v1.5", "speakers": [{"name": "Rod Johnson", "company": "Embabel"}], "room": "Room 4", "start": "2026-10-09T08:40:00Z", "end": "2026-10-09T09:30:00Z", "type": "Conference", "track": "Agentic Engineering & Tooling", "level": "INTERMEDIATE", "minutes": 51, "keywords": ["deterministic", "spring", "embabel", "goap"], "themes": ["AI agents & agentic coding", "LLMs in Java apps", "Spring & frameworks"], "tldr": "Rod Johnson introduces Embabel: typed action steps chained by a deterministic GOAP planner, per-call model selection, agentic RAG with chunk expansion, skills and code mode on Spring.", "watchIf": "You build LLM features on Spring and want predictable, testable agent flows with typed steps rather than letting the model drive.", "takeaway": "Break an LLM workflow into typed steps and replace any step you can with deterministic code.", "videoId": "DniA37kVfvI", "hearts": 194, "views": 1391, "likes": 16, "likeRate": 1.04, "thin": true, "rating": null, "numRatings": null, "seat": true}, {"id": 21694, "title": "Why it takes 3000 commits to bring Valhalla's value objects to the JDK", "speakers": [{"name": "David Simms", "company": "Java Platrform Group - Oracle"}], "room": "Room 6", "start": "2026-10-09T07:30:00Z", "end": "2026-10-09T08:20:00Z", "type": "Conference", "track": "Java Language & Platform", "level": "BEGINNER", "minutes": 51, "keywords": ["valueobjects", "valhalla", "openjdk", "javavm"], "themes": ["Java language & JDK", "JVM performance & runtime"], "tldr": "Simms explains what JEP 401 value objects change for Java developers, why Valhalla took a decade to land, and how HotSpot implements them with heap flattening and C2 scalarization.", "watchIf": "You want to know what the value keyword changes for your existing code, and how HotSpot decides when to flatten or scalarize value objects.", "takeaway": "Download a JDK 28 early-access build and run your test suite with --enable-preview to catch breakage from the migrated wrapper classes.", "videoId": "D_AmK8-UnZg", "hearts": 183, "views": 6543, "likes": 61, "likeRate": 0.92, "thin": false, "rating": null, "numRatings": null, "seat": true}, {"id": 21666, "title": "Teaching an Old Handle New Tricks: From Core Reflection to FFM", "speakers": [{"name": "Maurizio Cimadamore", "company": "Oracle"}, {"name": "Viktor Klang", "company": "Oracle"}], "room": "Room 4", "start": "2026-10-07T13:10:00Z", "end": "2026-10-07T14:00:00Z", "type": "Conference", "track": "Java Language & Platform", "level": "ADVANCED", "minutes": 52, "keywords": ["method handles", "java runtime", "reflection", "var handles"], "themes": ["Java language & JDK", "JVM performance & runtime"], "tldr": "Cimadamore and Klang explain method handles as 'link once, execute many' and trace that idea through reflection, string concatenation, FFM downcalls and a planned marshalling API.", "watchIf": "You write frameworks, serializers or native interop code and want to understand why the JDK keeps reaching for method handles and how to get their performance.", "takeaway": "Store method handles in static final fields and call them with invokeExact using exact static types and a cast on the result.", "videoId": "4ck-T2gaMQ4", "hearts": 150, "views": 4545, "likes": 29, "likeRate": 0.67, "thin": false, "rating": null, "numRatings": null, "seat": true}, {"id": 23536, "title": "Ask the Architects", "speakers": [{"name": "Gavin Bierman", "company": "Oracle"}, {"name": "Maurizio Cimadamore", "company": "Oracle"}, {"name": "Per-Ake Minborg", "company": "Oracle"}, {"name": "Viktor Klang", "company": "Oracle"}, {"name": "Alan Bateman", "company": "Oracle"}, {"name": "Georges Saab", "company": "Oracle"}], "room": "Room 7", "start": "2026-10-08T11:50:00Z", "end": "2026-10-08T12:40:00Z", "type": "Conference", "track": "Java Language & Platform", "level": "INTERMEDIATE", "minutes": 51, "keywords": ["engineers", "runtime", "oracle", "programming"], "themes": ["Java language & JDK", "JVM performance & runtime"], "tldr": "An Oracle Java architects panel answers audience questions on virtual thread schedulers, deprecation policy, AI code generation, string internals, Valhalla, modules, string templates and ZGC automatic heap sizing.", "watchIf": "You want the JDK architects' current thinking on virtual thread schedulers, Valhalla, string templates and ZGC, straight from the people building them.", "takeaway": "Report concrete virtual thread scheduling problems, especially Netty or event-loop interop, to the loom-dev mailing list instead of reaching into internals reflectively.", "videoId": "ewCPBgOrLAE", "hearts": 160, "views": 724, "likes": 8, "likeRate": 0.97, "thin": true, "rating": null, "numRatings": null, "seat": true}, {"id": 50103, "title": "Let's dance! – Teaching your robot some moves with reinforcement learning", "speakers": [{"name": "Thomas Endres", "company": "TNG Technology Consulting GmbH"}, {"name": "Martin F.", "company": "TNG Technology Consulting GmbH"}, {"name": "Tobias Lübbe", "company": "TNG Technology Consulting GmbH"}], "room": "Room 10", "start": "2026-10-07T11:05:00Z", "end": "2026-10-07T11:45:00Z", "type": "Lunch Talk", "track": "Mind the Geek", "level": "BEGINNER", "minutes": 41, "keywords": ["imitation", "simulation", "humanoid", "reinforcement"], "themes": ["Hardware, robots & fun"], "tldr": "TNG's innovation hacking team shows how they took a Unitree G1 humanoid from falling over to dancing: reward-shaped RL in Isaac Sim, motion capture retargeting, and sim-to-real fixes.", "watchIf": "You are curious how humanoid robots learn locomotion and motion imitation, and want an accessible, demo-heavy tour of the real pitfalls.", "takeaway": "Start from a digital twin in a simulator before running any learned policy on real hardware.", "videoId": "gPtWq_LHL8o", "hearts": 114, "views": 539, "likes": 0, "likeRate": 0.51, "thin": true, "rating": null, "numRatings": null, "seat": true}, {"id": 31452, "title": "Responsibly Dimming the Lights in the Software Factory", "speakers": [{"name": "Victor Rentea", "company": "Freelance"}], "room": "Room 8", "start": "2026-10-06T11:30:00Z", "end": "2026-10-06T14:30:00Z", "type": "Deep Dive", "track": "Agentic Engineering & Tooling", "level": "BEGINNER", "minutes": 166, "keywords": ["cognitive debt", "ai-generated code", "code review", "automation"], "themes": ["AI agents & agentic coding", "Testing & quality", "Teams, careers & leadership"], "tldr": "Rentea takes a vague ticket to a PR with grilling, OpenSpec and adversarial AI review, arguing human review can be dimmed only gradually, after guardrails and months of good output.", "watchIf": "Your team ships agent-written code and is debating how much human review it still needs, and you want a concrete, step-by-step workflow rather than a slogan.", "takeaway": "Run an adversarial AI review in a fresh context on every agent-written change before a human looks at it.", "videoId": "C10mZ3pCnGo", "hearts": 502, "views": 3584, "likes": 49, "likeRate": 1.27, "thin": false, "rating": null, "numRatings": null, "seat": true}, {"id": 49255, "title": "Modernizing legacy code with AI: hands-on examples & lessons learned", "speakers": [{"name": "Koen Vanderkimpen", "company": "Smals"}, {"name": "Wim Lambrecht", "company": "Smals"}], "room": "Room 3", "start": "2026-10-08T13:00:00Z", "end": "2026-10-08T13:50:00Z", "type": "Conference", "track": "Agentic Engineering & Tooling", "level": "BEGINNER", "minutes": 51, "keywords": ["migration", "jakarta", "modernization", "legacy"], "themes": ["Legacy modernization", "AI agents & agentic coding"], "tldr": "Smals reports on seven AI-assisted legacy projects, from COBOL analysis to SAS-to-Python, and details how it moved a JBoss EAP 7 application to EAP 8/Jakarta EE with a phased, human-in-the-loop harness.", "watchIf": "You own legacy Java EE, COBOL or SAS code and want a realistic, public-sector view of where AI helps with modernisation and how to structure a migration.", "takeaway": "Before an AI-assisted migration, scrub secrets and personal data and make sure test coverage can catch regressions.", "videoId": "FvVe9J0Abjg", "hearts": 527, "views": 783, "likes": 4, "likeRate": 0.68, "thin": true, "rating": null, "numRatings": null, "seat": true}, {"id": 24245, "title": "Making sense of vector databases", "speakers": [{"name": "Balkrishna Rawool", "company": "ING Bank"}], "room": "Room 9", "start": "2026-10-08T09:50:00Z", "end": "2026-10-08T10:40:00Z", "type": "Conference", "track": "Cloud Native, Data & Infrastructure", "level": "INTERMEDIATE", "minutes": 48, "keywords": ["semantic search", "embedding models", "indexing algorithms", "vector databases"], "themes": ["Data & databases", "LLMs in Java apps"], "tldr": "Rawool explains vectors, embeddings, similarity metrics and indexing (HNSW) through seven Spring Boot demos that build from a hand-rolled Java Vector API store to RAG and a Google Lens-style image search.", "watchIf": "You use vector stores or RAG without being sure what embeddings, cosine similarity or HNSW actually do, and like learning from small runnable examples.", "takeaway": "Choose the distance metric that matches how your embedding model was trained, typically cosine similarity on normalised vectors.", "videoId": "knE4uy7IYXY", "hearts": 273, "views": 719, "likes": 4, "likeRate": 0.71, "thin": true, "rating": null, "numRatings": null, "seat": true}, {"id": 23543, "title": "The Yearly Java Performance Update for 2026", "speakers": [{"name": "Per-Ake Minborg", "company": "Oracle"}], "room": "Room 5", "start": "2026-10-08T09:50:00Z", "end": "2026-10-08T10:40:00Z", "type": "Conference", "track": "Java Language & Platform", "level": "INTERMEDIATE", "minutes": 42, "keywords": ["optimization", "performance", "java", "jdk"], "themes": ["JVM performance & runtime", "Java language & JDK"], "tldr": "Minborg explains how HotSpot's JIT optimises code, then covers JDK 26 and 27 performance work (G1 barriers, compact headers by default, library fast paths, AOT) and previews JDK 28.", "watchIf": "You plan JDK upgrades and want to know which performance gains come for free, which defaults changed under you, and how the JIT actually works.", "takeaway": "Benchmark your service on JDK 26 and 27 with default settings, then toggle the collector and compact object headers to see their effect on startup, throughput, memory and tail latency.", "videoId": "rNOzf12RORI", "hearts": 367, "views": 1710, "likes": 18, "likeRate": 0.99, "thin": true, "rating": null, "numRatings": null, "seat": true}, {"id": 21675, "title": "All you need to know about Maven 4!", "speakers": [{"name": "Matthias Bünger", "company": "ITZBund"}], "room": "Room 8", "start": "2026-10-09T07:30:00Z", "end": "2026-10-09T08:20:00Z", "type": "Conference", "track": "Development Practices", "level": "BEGINNER", "minutes": 47, "keywords": ["maven", "version", "features", "ecosystem"], "themes": ["Developer experience & tooling"], "tldr": "Bünger walks through Maven 4: Java 17 to run it, split build and consumer POMs that let the POM schema evolve, reactor and lifecycle improvements, and the migration path.", "watchIf": "You maintain Maven builds or plugins and want to know what changes in Maven 4 and how to prepare for it.", "takeaway": "Upgrade builds to the latest Maven 3.9 or 3.10 and the latest 3.x plugins, and avoid the experimental 4.x plugins.", "videoId": "TgkY2UmWtBo", "hearts": 394, "views": 5793, "likes": 49, "likeRate": 0.85, "thin": false, "rating": null, "numRatings": null, "seat": true}, {"id": 23533, "title": "Teaching a New Dog Old Tricks: Give the Agent a Debugger", "speakers": [{"name": "Anton Arhipov", "company": "JetBrains"}], "room": "Room 5", "start": "2026-10-05T15:35:00Z", "end": "2026-10-05T16:05:00Z", "type": "Tools-in-Action", "track": "Agentic Engineering & Tooling", "level": "INTERMEDIATE", "minutes": 35, "keywords": ["debugger", "logpoints", "runtime", "intellij"], "themes": ["AI agents & agentic coding", "Developer experience & tooling"], "tldr": "Arhipov recaps lesser-known IntelliJ IDEA debugger features, then shows a coding agent using them via a bundled debugger skill and MCP tools, collecting evidence with logpoints instead of print statements.", "watchIf": "You use coding agents in IntelliJ IDEA and want them to debug with runtime evidence instead of scattering print statements through your code.", "takeaway": "Enable the IntelliJ debugger skill for your project and tell the agent explicitly to debug, so it reaches for logpoints instead of println.", "videoId": "rk22mStXsGU", "hearts": 186, "views": 1055, "likes": 16, "likeRate": 1.22, "thin": true, "rating": null, "numRatings": null, "seat": true}, {"id": 49254, "title": "Mission Possible: The 45-Minute Path to Bullet-Proof Java Container Images", "speakers": [{"name": "Pasha Finkelshtein", "company": "Bellsoft"}], "room": "Room 3", "start": "2026-10-07T11:05:00Z", "end": "2026-10-07T11:45:00Z", "type": "Lunch Talk", "track": "Java Language & Platform", "level": "BEGINNER", "minutes": 43, "keywords": ["containerization", "vulnerabilities", "hardening", "provenance"], "themes": ["Security & supply chain", "Cloud native & Kubernetes"], "tldr": "Finkelshtein hardens a naive Temurin JDK 25 image in five steps, ending with a signed distroless image with SBOMs, and argues someone must own the base layer under an SLA.", "watchIf": "You ship Java in containers, own nobody's base image, and want a concrete sequence of Dockerfile and pipeline changes to get CVE counts and audit questions under control.", "takeaway": "Add USER, --cap-drop=ALL, no-new-privileges and a read-only filesystem to one production container this week.", "videoId": "1yndLHw9mbo", "hearts": 199, "views": 564, "likes": 7, "likeRate": 1.01, "thin": true, "rating": null, "numRatings": null, "seat": true}, {"id": 24227, "title": "Expanding Vector Search Beyond Similarity", "speakers": [{"name": "Alina Yurenko", "company": "Oracle"}], "room": "Room 4", "start": "2026-10-05T14:50:00Z", "end": "2026-10-05T15:20:00Z", "type": "Tools-in-Action", "track": "Agentic Engineering & Tooling", "level": "INTERMEDIATE", "minutes": 31, "keywords": ["micronaut", "graalvm", "vector search", "embeddings"], "themes": ["LLMs in Java apps", "Data & databases"], "tldr": "Yurenko demos a Micronaut, LangChain4j and GraalVM travel assistant that uses embeddings only for meaning and enforces location and price constraints in application code through tools.", "watchIf": "You are adding semantic search or a tool-calling assistant to a Java app and need results that respect real data and business rules.", "takeaway": "Implement budget, distance and similar hard constraints as filters in your search tool instead of instructions in the prompt.", "videoId": "EnHPYkSAaCo", "hearts": 90, "views": 1043, "likes": 9, "likeRate": 0.86, "thin": true, "rating": null, "numRatings": null, "seat": true}, {"id": 49751, "title": "F*ck The System, and other bad ideas for the future", "speakers": [{"name": "Jo Caudron", "company": ""}], "room": "Room 5", "start": "2026-10-08T16:55:00Z", "end": "2026-10-08T17:40:00Z", "type": "Closing Keynote", "track": "Agentic Engineering & Tooling", "level": "BEGINNER", "minutes": 59, "keywords": ["sovereignty", "innovation", "resilience", "europe"], "themes": ["Security & supply chain"], "tldr": "Caudron argues the post-war order is over and Europe should build sovereignty on energy and technology, pay for resilience, and swap the frontier AI race for broad adoption.", "watchIf": "You make technology or vendor choices and want a European geopolitical argument for weighing sovereignty and resilience alongside price.", "takeaway": "Map which parts of your stack would stop working if a US or Chinese vendor switched you off, and design a fallback path.", "videoId": "wUyQmRriCng", "hearts": 376, "views": 5195, "likes": 42, "likeRate": 0.81, "thin": false, "rating": null, "numRatings": null, "seat": true}, {"id": 21691, "title": "MCP 2.0 - Stateless, New Auth (CIMD), and Exciting Extensions", "speakers": [{"name": "James Ward", "company": "AWS"}], "room": "Room 9", "start": "2026-10-07T10:00:00Z", "end": "2026-10-07T10:50:00Z", "type": "Conference", "track": "Agentic Engineering & Tooling", "level": "INTERMEDIATE", "minutes": 51, "keywords": ["extensions", "authentication", "mcp", "stateless"], "themes": ["AI agents & agentic coding", "LLMs in Java apps", "Security & supply chain"], "tldr": "Ward walks through the 2026-07-28 MCP spec: a stateless transport, elicitation via multi-round-trip requests, CIMD replacing dynamic client registration, and official extensions for MCP Apps, Tasks and Skills over MCP.", "watchIf": "You maintain or are about to build an MCP server or client and need to know what the 2026-07-28 spec breaks and how to prepare for it.", "takeaway": "Switch your MCP servers to stateless mode now, and model state as explicit resource handles passed in tool arguments.", "videoId": "fShgG3jtkXQ", "hearts": 351, "views": 1474, "likes": 20, "likeRate": 1.17, "thin": false, "rating": null, "numRatings": null, "seat": true}, {"id": 5530, "title": "Kill All Mutants! (Intro to Mutation Testing)", "speakers": [{"name": "Dave Aronson", "company": "Codosaurus"}], "room": "Room 5", "start": "2026-10-08T13:00:00Z", "end": "2026-10-08T13:50:00Z", "type": "Conference", "track": "Development Practices", "level": "ADVANCED", "minutes": 50, "keywords": ["code quality", "test suite", "testing", "mutation"], "themes": ["Testing & quality"], "tldr": "Aronson explains how mutation testing finds lax tests and meaningless code that coverage misses, how tools generate and kill mutants, what surviving mutants mean, and where it fits in a pipeline.", "watchIf": "You trust code coverage numbers and want to see a technique that shows whether your tests would actually catch a bug.", "takeaway": "Run PIT (or your language's equivalent) on one well-tested class and see how many mutants survive despite high coverage.", "videoId": "AsVt_dzTmN4", "hearts": 294, "views": 358, "likes": 5, "likeRate": 1.01, "thin": true, "rating": null, "numRatings": null, "seat": true}, {"id": 4097, "title": "Pragmatic multi-cloud architecture: designing for resilience and portability beyond the hype", "speakers": [{"name": "Alexandre Touret", "company": "Magellan"}], "room": "Room 10", "start": "2026-10-07T13:10:00Z", "end": "2026-10-07T14:00:00Z", "type": "Conference", "track": "Cloud Native, Data & Infrastructure", "level": "INTERMEDIATE", "minutes": 35, "keywords": ["multi-cloud", "architecture", "security", "latency"], "themes": ["Cloud native & Kubernetes", "Architecture & design"], "tldr": "Touret walks through designing a multi-cloud fleet-tracking platform on AWS and GCP, arguing for one bounded context per provider, explicit cross-cloud flows, and trade-offs driven by cost, SLAs and customer needs.", "watchIf": "You are asked to spread a platform across AWS and GCP and want a concrete checklist for boundaries, SLAs, identity, data sync and egress costs.", "takeaway": "Draw a BPMN diagram with one swimlane per cloud provider and list every cross-cloud transaction before committing to a design.", "videoId": "mOmuF5fDiUM", "hearts": 68, "views": 324, "likes": 2, "likeRate": 0.78, "thin": true, "rating": null, "numRatings": null, "seat": true}, {"id": 25027, "title": "Codepocalypse Now: LangChain4j vs JetBrains Koog", "speakers": [{"name": "Viktor Gamov", "company": "Confluent"}, {"name": "Baruch Sadogursky", "company": "Port.io"}], "room": "Room 6", "start": "2026-10-05T11:30:00Z", "end": "2026-10-05T14:30:00Z", "type": "Deep Dive", "track": "Agentic Engineering & Tooling", "level": "BEGINNER", "minutes": 180, "keywords": ["java", "frameworks", "ai", "agent"], "themes": ["LLMs in Java apps", "AI agents & agentic coding"], "tldr": "Gamov (LangChain4j) and Sadogursky (Koog) build the same personal 'claw' agent side by side in seven stages, from chatbot to multi-model workflow with human review and Langfuse tracing.", "watchIf": "You want to add agent features to a JVM app and are choosing between LangChain4j and Koog, or want to see tools, memory, skills, routing and tracing built up step by step.", "takeaway": "Keep deterministic business steps as plain code and use LLMs only at the fuzzy edges (drafting, summarising, interpreting tool output).", "videoId": "GGEmc6eR8c4", "hearts": 52, "views": 699, "likes": 2, "likeRate": 0.59, "thin": true, "rating": null, "numRatings": null, "seat": true}, {"id": 10038, "title": "Eval Driven Development: Why you need it to make your Agentic Application survive Production", "speakers": [{"name": "Urs Peter", "company": "Xebia"}], "room": "Room 4", "start": "2026-10-07T11:05:00Z", "end": "2026-10-07T11:45:00Z", "type": "Lunch Talk", "track": "Agentic Engineering & Tooling", "level": "INTERMEDIATE", "minutes": 40, "keywords": ["agentic", "reliability", "development", "evaluation"], "themes": ["AI agents & agentic coding", "Testing & quality", "LLMs in Java apps"], "tldr": "Peter shows eval-driven development on a JVM agent: golden datasets, deterministic checks, LLM-as-judge and conversation simulations with the Dokimos framework, plus feeding production traces back into failing evals.", "watchIf": "You're building an agent on the JVM and need a concrete way to test it before and after it reaches production.", "takeaway": "Write down what your agent must not do, and generate and review test prompts per user profile and scenario.", "videoId": "4qeUklTLyTs", "hearts": 263, "views": 662, "likes": 4, "likeRate": 0.74, "thin": true, "rating": null, "numRatings": null, "seat": true}, {"id": 41301, "title": "Owning the inference layer: When and how to run your own models", "speakers": [{"name": "Maarten Vandeperre", "company": "Red Hat"}, {"name": "Camille Nigon", "company": "Red Hat"}], "room": "Room 3", "start": "2026-10-08T14:30:00Z", "end": "2026-10-08T15:20:00Z", "type": "Conference", "track": "Agentic Engineering & Tooling", "level": "BEGINNER", "minutes": 51, "keywords": ["sovereignty", "latency", "inference", "self-hosting"], "themes": ["LLMs in Java apps", "Cloud native & Kubernetes"], "tldr": "Using a coffee analogy and one Quarkus app wired to three backends, Vandeperre and Nigon compare hosted model APIs, self-hosted inference with vLLM and llm-d, and a governed model-as-a-service gateway.", "watchIf": "You call hosted LLM APIs today and need a beginner-level map of what self-hosting with vLLM, llm-d and a token-metering gateway would involve.", "takeaway": "Put an AI gateway with per-team keys and token metrics in front of your model access now, even if it only proxies a hosted provider.", "videoId": "QbA-rs7XLEk", "hearts": 170, "views": 392, "likes": 5, "likeRate": 0.99, "thin": true, "rating": null, "numRatings": null, "seat": true}, {"id": 7036, "title": "Building Production-Ready Agentic Systems with LangChain4j and Quarkus", "speakers": [{"name": "Mario Fusco", "company": "IBM"}, {"name": "Georgios Andrianakis", "company": "IBM"}, {"name": "Kevin Dubois", "company": "IBM"}], "room": "BOF 2", "start": "2026-10-06T14:50:00Z", "end": "2026-10-06T16:50:00Z", "type": "Hands-on Lab", "track": "Agentic Engineering & Tooling", "level": "INTERMEDIATE", "minutes": 120, "keywords": ["agentic", "governance", "testing", "langchain4j"], "themes": ["AI agents & agentic coding", "LLMs in Java apps"], "tldr": "A 2-hour lab building a production-oriented agentic application with LangChain4j and Quarkus, covering MCP, A2A, skills, guardrails, resilience and testing.", "watchIf": "You want to move a Java agent prototype toward production with proper testing and resilience.", "takeaway": "Add automated tests with mocked models and tools to any agent prototype.", "videoId": null, "hearts": 48, "views": null, "likes": null, "likeRate": null, "thin": false, "rating": null, "numRatings": null, "seat": false}, {"id": 48301, "title": "A Fleet of AI Agents, Each in Its Own Sandbox: Inside Docker's Agentic Platform", "speakers": [{"name": "Jean-Laurent de Morlhon", "company": "Docker"}], "room": "Room 8", "start": "2026-10-07T08:30:00Z", "end": "2026-10-07T08:50:00Z", "type": "Keynote", "track": "Agentic Engineering & Tooling", "level": "INTERMEDIATE", "minutes": 21, "keywords": ["autonomy", "ai agents", "sandbox", "docker"], "themes": ["AI agents & agentic coding", "Security & supply chain"], "tldr": "De Morlhon presents Docker's sandbox platform for coding agents (microVMs with filesystem and network control, shareable Kits, cloud sandboxes) and the internal 'AI factory' Docker built on it.", "watchIf": "You let coding agents run with real credentials on your machine and want a quick look at how Docker isolates them and orchestrates many in parallel.", "takeaway": "Stop giving agents broad GitHub tokens or raw API keys; run them in a sandbox that injects scoped credentials at the network layer.", "videoId": "mnoUXIT4nMY", "hearts": 481, "views": 1087, "likes": 11, "likeRate": 0.94, "thin": true, "rating": null, "numRatings": null, "seat": true}, {"id": 5502, "title": "Taming the Chaos: Security and Predictability in LLM-Powered Applications", "speakers": [{"name": "Rustam Mehmandarov", "company": "Miles"}], "room": "Room 4", "start": "2026-10-08T11:50:00Z", "end": "2026-10-08T12:40:00Z", "type": "Conference", "track": "Security, Trust & Compliance", "level": "ADVANCED", "minutes": 49, "keywords": ["guardrails", "llms", "promptinjection", "validation"], "themes": ["Security & supply chain", "LLMs in Java apps"], "tldr": "Mehmandarov builds a LangChain4j invoice-extraction pipeline layer by layer, adding guardrails, canary tokens, an intent classifier, deterministic validation, self-correction loops, multi-model voting and a mirror test.", "watchIf": "You are putting LLM output into a business process in Java and want a catalogue of layered guardrail and validation patterns with code you can copy.", "takeaway": "Add input and output guardrails to your LangChain4j AI services, including PII redaction on output and in logs.", "videoId": "vNeyNkeU4ms", "hearts": 172, "views": 437, "likes": 1, "likeRate": 0.63, "thin": true, "rating": null, "numRatings": null, "seat": true}, {"id": 15354, "title": "Will Valhalla Fix Tony's Billion-Dollar Mistake?", "speakers": [{"name": "José Paumard", "company": "Oracle"}, {"name": "Remi Forax", "company": "University Gustave Eiffel"}], "room": "Room 9", "start": "2026-10-06T07:30:00Z", "end": "2026-10-06T10:30:00Z", "type": "Deep Dive", "track": "Java Language & Platform", "level": "INTERMEDIATE", "minutes": 161, "keywords": ["performance", "valhalla", "value classes", "jvm"], "themes": ["Java language & JDK", "JVM performance & runtime"], "tldr": "Paumard and Forax explain JEP 401 value classes, previewing in JDK 28: what giving up identity changes, how scalarization and flattening perform, and why null-safety remains unsolved.", "watchIf": "You want a precise picture of what value classes change in Java semantics and performance before they preview in JDK 28, including the limitations.", "takeaway": "Download a JDK 28 early-access build and run your code with --enable-preview to see the effect of Integer, Optional and LocalDate becoming value classes.", "videoId": "vLDbzmiT7Xs", "hearts": 187, "views": 2436, "likes": 41, "likeRate": 1.47, "thin": false, "rating": null, "numRatings": null, "seat": true}, {"id": 50102, "title": "From Brainless to Brilliant — Giving an Open-Source Robot a Mind of Its Own", "speakers": [{"name": "Jan Van Wassenhove", "company": "Sopra Steria"}], "room": "Room 8", "start": "2026-10-07T07:50:00Z", "end": "2026-10-07T08:10:00Z", "type": "Keynote", "track": "Agentic Engineering & Tooling", "level": "BEGINNER", "minutes": 17, "keywords": ["firmware", "autonomy", "privacy", "robot"], "themes": ["Hardware, robots & fun", "AI agents & agentic coding"], "tldr": "Van Wassenhove shows AURA, a laptop-hosted brain for a 300-euro Reachy Mini robot that recognises family members, separates facts from memories, codes in VS Code, and needs approval for anything sensitive.", "watchIf": "You want a 15-minute live look at a hobbyist-built agentic robot with sensible privacy and approval guardrails.", "takeaway": "Put a human approval gate in front of every action that leaves the sandbox, such as commits or procedure changes, because agents will find reasons to do what you didn't intend.", "videoId": "ZDocIlx7Zvg", "hearts": 431, "views": 1421, "likes": 7, "likeRate": 0.62, "thin": true, "rating": null, "numRatings": null, "seat": true}, {"id": 5519, "title": "The Magic of OpenTelemetry: Rewriting Your App in Production", "speakers": [{"name": "Bruce Bujon", "company": "Datadog"}], "room": "Room 5", "start": "2026-10-06T15:35:00Z", "end": "2026-10-06T16:05:00Z", "type": "Tools-in-Action", "track": "Java Language & Platform", "level": "ADVANCED", "minutes": 30, "keywords": ["instrumentation", "bytebuddy", "javaagent", "opentelemetry"], "themes": ["JVM performance & runtime", "Developer experience & tooling"], "tldr": "Bujon shows how the OpenTelemetry Java agent rewrites classes as they load, diffs its changes with a meta-agent, and measures the startup cost, including under Leyden.", "watchIf": "You run the OpenTelemetry Java agent in production and want to see what it changes inside your JVM and what it costs at startup.", "takeaway": "Try OpenTelemetry on an existing container by adding -javaagent through JAVA_TOOL_OPTIONS before writing any SDK code.", "videoId": "Lf_m5dIjjqw", "hearts": 197, "views": 1839, "likes": 14, "likeRate": 0.79, "thin": true, "rating": null, "numRatings": null, "seat": true}, {"id": 49251, "title": "The Year of the Runtime", "speakers": [{"name": "Ron Pressler", "company": "Oracle"}], "room": "Room 8", "start": "2026-10-07T08:50:00Z", "end": "2026-10-07T09:10:00Z", "type": "Keynote", "track": "Java Language & Platform", "level": "INTERMEDIATE", "minutes": 15, "keywords": ["java", "runtime", "jvm", "aicoding"], "themes": ["JVM performance & runtime", "Java language & JDK"], "tldr": "Pressler argues that if AI makes language syntax matter less, the runtime matters more, and runs through the JVM's recent and upcoming GC, Valhalla, Leyden, jlink, integrity and JFR improvements.", "watchIf": "You want a 15-minute overview of where the JVM runtime is heading and Oracle's argument for why it matters more with AI coding.", "takeaway": "Upgrade your JDK regularly; most of these performance gains require no code changes.", "videoId": "tle2mA7EwWU", "hearts": 457, "views": 834, "likes": 17, "likeRate": 1.45, "thin": true, "rating": null, "numRatings": null, "seat": true}, {"id": 7032, "title": "Don’t burn your fingers in the heat of the AI (r)evolution. (And get an OpenClaw).", "speakers": [{"name": "Maarten Vandeperre", "company": "Red Hat"}], "room": "Room 4", "start": "2026-10-06T11:30:00Z", "end": "2026-10-06T14:30:00Z", "type": "Deep Dive", "track": "Agentic Engineering & Tooling", "level": "BEGINNER", "minutes": 130, "keywords": ["observability", "agentic", "governance", "enterprise"], "themes": ["AI agents & agentic coding", "Cloud native & Kubernetes", "Security & supply chain"], "tldr": "Vandeperre grows a coffee-shop app from one LLM call into a governed enterprise platform, demoing a model gateway, guardrails, sandboxes, supply chain, CDC, service mesh, rollouts and agent tracing.", "watchIf": "You are tasked with taking an AI proof of concept into an enterprise Kubernetes environment and want a tour of the platform pieces (gateway, guardrails, sandboxes, CDC, mesh, tracing) with working demos.", "takeaway": "Put one gateway in front of every model your organisation uses so you can route, rate-limit and attribute costs.", "videoId": "zDqS1U5Gnk8", "hearts": 59, "views": 514, "likes": 2, "likeRate": 0.67, "thin": true, "rating": null, "numRatings": null, "seat": true}, {"id": 9032, "title": "Agents With Seatbelts: Practical Ways to Keep AI Code Gen Under Control", "speakers": [{"name": "Jonathan Vila López", "company": "GK Software"}], "room": "Room 8", "start": "2026-10-08T08:40:00Z", "end": "2026-10-08T09:30:00Z", "type": "Conference", "track": "Development Practices", "level": "INTERMEDIATE", "minutes": 51, "keywords": ["security", "metrics", "agents", "mcp"], "themes": ["AI agents & agentic coding", "Security & supply chain", "Testing & quality"], "tldr": "Vila argues that AI coding slows teams down when done naively. He lists practical guardrails (decomposition, model choice, specs, skills over MCP, sandboxing, deterministic checks) and calls for measuring AI's value end to end.", "watchIf": "You are rolling out coding agents in a company and need concrete guardrails and an argument for measuring whether they actually pay off.", "takeaway": "Measure a productivity baseline now, covering the whole delivery pipeline and not just code generation.", "videoId": "d1SJXp9QeWE", "hearts": 672, "views": 776, "likes": 5, "likeRate": 0.75, "thin": true, "rating": null, "numRatings": null, "seat": true}, {"id": 10043, "title": "What Does it Print? The Java Quiz (Hard Edition 🤯)", "speakers": [{"name": "Clément de Tastes", "company": "SCIAM"}], "room": "BOF 1", "start": "2026-10-08T16:50:00Z", "end": "2026-10-08T17:50:00Z", "type": "BOF", "track": "Java Language & Platform", "level": "ADVANCED", "minutes": 60, "keywords": ["subtleties", "java", "pitfalls", "snippets"], "themes": ["Java language & JDK"], "tldr": "An interactive BOF where attendees analyse short, tricky Java snippets together and predict what they print, to test assumptions about the language.", "watchIf": "You enjoy Java puzzlers and want to test how well you know the language.", "takeaway": "Re-read the language spec on the rules you only think you know.", "videoId": null, "hearts": 95, "views": null, "likes": null, "likeRate": null, "thin": false, "rating": null, "numRatings": null, "seat": false}, {"id": 31453, "title": "Architecture Observability and Guardrails", "speakers": [{"name": "Victor Rentea", "company": "Freelance"}], "room": "Room 8", "start": "2026-10-07T14:40:00Z", "end": "2026-10-07T15:30:00Z", "type": "Conference", "track": "Agentic Engineering & Tooling", "level": "BEGINNER", "minutes": 52, "keywords": ["code quality", "ai-generated", "code review", "testing"], "themes": ["AI agents & agentic coding", "Architecture & design", "Testing & quality"], "tldr": "Using no slides, Rentea demos a vibe-coded review dashboard that helps humans review AI-written pull requests, built on deterministic artefacts generated from code and test runs.", "watchIf": "You review large AI-generated pull requests and want ideas for deterministic, script-generated review artefacts. Expect a chaotic, slide-free live tour.", "takeaway": "Run an adversarial review agent (or several) on any agent task longer than 10-15 minutes before a human looks at it.", "videoId": "BOcNjR2lBus", "hearts": 855, "views": 2319, "likes": 36, "likeRate": 1.37, "thin": false, "rating": null, "numRatings": null, "seat": true}, {"id": 17794, "title": "Escaping Java's Allocation Gravity with Value Classes", "speakers": [{"name": "Clément de Tastes", "company": "SCIAM"}, {"name": "Remi Forax", "company": "University Gustave Eiffel"}], "room": "Room 5", "start": "2026-10-09T07:30:00Z", "end": "2026-10-09T08:20:00Z", "type": "Conference", "track": "Java Language & Platform", "level": "ADVANCED", "minutes": 51, "keywords": ["valuetypes", "java", "projectvalhalla", "blackhole"], "themes": ["Java language & JDK", "JVM performance & runtime"], "tldr": "De Tastes and Forax introduce JEP 401 value classes, demo their semantics, and use a black hole ray tracer to show allocation and GC work disappearing once the JIT scalarizes.", "watchIf": "You write allocation-heavy numeric or domain code in Java and want to see what value classes change in semantics and allocation on a concrete workload.", "takeaway": "Move field initialization before super() in your constructors now, using flexible constructor bodies, so classes are ready for value.", "videoId": "lUB15M3mMLs", "hearts": 285, "views": 4758, "likes": 43, "likeRate": 0.9, "thin": false, "rating": null, "numRatings": null, "seat": true}, {"id": 51601, "title": "Production-Ready Java Containers: Smaller, Faster, Smarter", "speakers": [{"name": "Albert Attard", "company": "Oracle"}], "room": "Room 3", "start": "2026-10-08T15:40:00Z", "end": "2026-10-08T16:30:00Z", "type": "Conference", "track": "Java Language & Platform", "level": "BEGINNER", "minutes": 48, "keywords": ["optimization", "jlink", "java", "containers"], "themes": ["Cloud native & Kubernetes", "JVM performance & runtime", "Java language & JDK"], "tldr": "Attard works through a checklist for production Java containers: pinned and layered images, jlink runtimes on slim or scratch bases, AOT cache, compact object headers, memory flags, lifecycle and hardening.", "watchIf": "You package Java services as containers and want a practical list of image, JVM flag and hardening settings, each backed by a measurement.", "takeaway": "Check whether your containers set a heap size; if not, add -XX:MaxRAMPercentage (Attard uses 75) through JDK_JAVA_OPTIONS.", "videoId": "LPhGSgO5eKE", "hearts": 188, "views": 540, "likes": 8, "likeRate": 1.1, "thin": true, "rating": null, "numRatings": null, "seat": true}, {"id": 17556, "title": "SIMD for Java: how Elasticsearch already benefits from the Panama Vector API", "speakers": [{"name": "Piotr Przybyl", "company": "Elastic"}], "room": "Room 10", "start": "2026-10-08T09:50:00Z", "end": "2026-10-08T10:40:00Z", "type": "Conference", "track": "Cloud Native, Data & Infrastructure", "level": "INTERMEDIATE", "minutes": 52, "keywords": ["elasticsearch", "simd", "java", "vector"], "themes": ["JVM performance & runtime", "Java language & JDK", "Data & databases"], "tldr": "Przybyl explains how Lucene and Elasticsearch use the incubating Panama Vector API for dot products and BBQ quantization, and why Elasticsearch now goes further with a native C++ SIMD kernel called through FFM.", "watchIf": "You write numeric hot loops in Java, run Elasticsearch vector search, or want to know whether the incubating Vector API is ready for production use.", "takeaway": "Benchmark Vector API code against a scalar baseline that uses the same accumulator and ILP tricks before claiming a speed-up.", "videoId": "adFS-_sCTQ4", "hearts": 69, "views": 767, "likes": 8, "likeRate": 0.94, "thin": true, "rating": null, "numRatings": null, "seat": true}, {"id": 9024, "title": "Beyond assertEquals: Probabilistic Security and Bias Testing for Java LLM Applications", "speakers": [{"name": "Iryna Dohndorf", "company": "Karakun"}], "room": "Room 6", "start": "2026-10-06T15:35:00Z", "end": "2026-10-06T16:05:00Z", "type": "Tools-in-Action", "track": "Security, Trust & Compliance", "level": "INTERMEDIATE", "minutes": 36, "keywords": ["java", "security", "llm", "testing"], "themes": ["Security & supply chain", "Testing & quality", "LLMs in Java apps"], "tldr": "Dohndorf presents Tiberius, an open-source Java tool that runs LLM jailbreak and prompt-injection probes as JUnit tests, stores results as JSON fixtures and asserts statistical security contracts via PUnit.", "watchIf": "You ship a Java app with an LLM behind it and want security and guardrail tests that run in your normal JUnit build rather than a separate Python scanner.", "takeaway": "Replace one-shot LLM safety tests with multi-trial tests that assert a minimum pass rate at a stated confidence level.", "videoId": "MmSvjJhZl3A", "hearts": 67, "views": 802, "likes": 5, "likeRate": 0.74, "thin": true, "rating": null, "numRatings": null, "seat": true}, {"id": 25661, "title": "Introducing Agent Control Theory: The System Runs the Model", "speakers": [{"name": "Mark Pollack", "company": "Tuvium"}], "room": "Room 10", "start": "2026-10-08T14:30:00Z", "end": "2026-10-08T15:20:00Z", "type": "Conference", "track": "Agentic Engineering & Tooling", "level": "BEGINNER", "minutes": 52, "keywords": ["theory", "workflow", "control", "agent"], "themes": ["AI agents & agentic coding", "Testing & quality"], "tldr": "Pollack, founder of Spring AI, applies the feedback loop from control theory to coding agents: define success, judge with evidence, read the agent's session log, change one variable, repeat.", "watchIf": "You let coding agents produce real code and want a concrete, Java-centric method to verify their output and compare models on cost and quality.", "takeaway": "Write requirements in a structured form (EARS or RFC 2119 must/shall) so a judge can check the agent's output against each one with evidence.", "videoId": "KwQ76IvU59M", "hearts": 79, "views": 625, "likes": 7, "likeRate": 0.97, "thin": true, "rating": null, "numRatings": null, "seat": true}, {"id": 22942, "title": "Why Documentation Won’t Write Itself—and AI Won’t Save You", "speakers": [{"name": "Christian Heitzmann", "company": "SimplexaCode AG"}], "room": "Room 6", "start": "2026-10-07T13:10:00Z", "end": "2026-10-07T14:00:00Z", "type": "Conference", "track": "Development Practices", "level": "BEGINNER", "minutes": 51, "keywords": ["documentation", "architecture", "ai", "code"], "themes": ["Developer experience & tooling", "Architecture & design"], "tldr": "Heitzmann argues documentation needs a defined audience and altitude, belongs in the repository as AsciiDoc rather than Markdown or Confluence, and is the context AI depends on.", "watchIf": "Your team's documentation lives in a decaying wiki and you want a concrete docs-as-code setup with AsciiDoc and a clear argument for why AI does not remove the need to write docs.", "takeaway": "For your next document, write down its audience and altitude before writing anything else.", "videoId": "A8TBUalnfdc", "hearts": 209, "views": 443, "likes": 7, "likeRate": 1.1, "thin": true, "rating": null, "numRatings": null, "seat": true}, {"id": 19707, "title": "Getting started with speaking: Your first conference talk", "speakers": [{"name": "Bert Jan Schrijver", "company": "OpenValue"}], "room": "BOF 2", "start": "2026-10-07T16:50:00Z", "end": "2026-10-07T17:50:00Z", "type": "BOF", "track": "People, Practices & Career", "level": "BEGINNER", "minutes": 60, "keywords": ["speaking", "proposals", "presentations", "technical events"], "themes": ["Teams, careers & leadership"], "tldr": "A BOF where experienced speakers share how to start speaking at conferences: choosing topics, writing accepted proposals, preparing material and delivering a talk.", "watchIf": "You would like to speak at a conference but do not know where to begin.", "takeaway": "Draft a proposal for a first talk and submit it to a conference or meetup.", "videoId": null, "hearts": 93, "views": null, "likes": null, "likeRate": null, "thin": false, "rating": null, "numRatings": null, "seat": false}, {"id": 52104, "title": "In the loop, not in the dark: staying in control in the era of dark software factories", "speakers": [{"name": "Alex Gavrilescu", "company": "Backlog.md"}], "room": "Room 4", "start": "2026-10-09T07:30:00Z", "end": "2026-10-09T08:20:00Z", "type": "Conference", "track": "Agentic Engineering & Tooling", "level": "BEGINNER", "minutes": 45, "keywords": ["architecture", "blueprint", "groma", "agents"], "themes": ["AI agents & agentic coding", "Architecture & design", "Developer experience & tooling"], "tldr": "After an outage in an agent-written app he couldn't debug, Gavrilescu built Groma.md: a live, Markdown-backed C4 map of a repo, so humans review architecture instead of code.", "watchIf": "You let coding agents ship most of your code and want a way to review what the system has become without reading every diff.", "takeaway": "Practise connecting to your production logs and database yourself before an incident, even on agent-built projects.", "videoId": "jhvT8hVYSvQ", "hearts": 377, "views": 2892, "likes": 12, "likeRate": 0.51, "thin": true, "rating": null, "numRatings": null, "seat": true}, {"id": 10031, "title": "The Dark Secrets of Computer Science", "speakers": [{"name": "Fabien Marsaud", "company": "Nonstatic"}], "room": "Room 8", "start": "2026-10-08T13:00:00Z", "end": "2026-10-08T13:50:00Z", "type": "Conference", "track": "Mind the Geek", "level": "INTERMEDIATE", "minutes": 49, "keywords": ["programming", "quirks", "humor", "legacy"], "themes": ["Hardware, robots & fun", "Legacy modernization"], "tldr": "A comic, AI-free tour of computing's oddities and pains, from HTTP 418 and leap seconds to Y2K38, CORS, Excel, Doom in TypeScript types and Jira bureaucracy, illustrated with Marsaud's own war stories.", "watchIf": "You want a lighthearted hour of shared developer pain and trivia, with no AI in it.", "takeaway": "Ask partners for a byte order mark or an explicit encoding in exchanged files instead of probing UTF-8, ISO-8859-1 and Windows-1252 in turn.", "videoId": "2Yh7LKt8YNw", "hearts": 580, "views": 446, "likes": 10, "likeRate": 1.34, "thin": true, "rating": null, "numRatings": null, "seat": true}, {"id": 15360, "title": "Analyze and Optimize Your Applications with JFR", "speakers": [{"name": "Ana-Maria Mihalceanu", "company": "Oracle"}, {"name": "José Paumard", "company": "Oracle"}], "room": "BOF 1", "start": "2026-10-06T14:50:00Z", "end": "2026-10-06T16:50:00Z", "type": "Hands-on Lab", "track": "Java Language & Platform", "level": "INTERMEDIATE", "minutes": 120, "keywords": ["observability", "java", "jfr", "prometheus"], "themes": ["JVM performance & runtime"], "tldr": "A hands-on lab on using JDK Flight Recorder to capture, analyze and extend application profiling data, including custom events and Prometheus integration.", "watchIf": "You want hands-on practice using JFR to diagnose and tune Java applications.", "takeaway": "Record a JFR session of one of your services and inspect it for hotspots.", "videoId": null, "hearts": 101, "views": null, "likes": null, "likeRate": null, "thin": false, "rating": null, "numRatings": null, "seat": false}, {"id": 8166, "title": "Integration patterns for distributed systems", "speakers": [{"name": "Dirk Fröhner", "company": "Amazon Web Services"}, {"name": "Sabarish Sekar", "company": "Amazon Web Services"}], "room": "Room 3", "start": "2026-10-07T13:10:00Z", "end": "2026-10-07T14:00:00Z", "type": "Conference", "track": "Architecture & Modernization", "level": "ADVANCED", "minutes": 49, "keywords": ["integration", "idempotence", "distributed systems", "microservices"], "themes": ["Architecture & design", "Cloud native & Kubernetes"], "tldr": "Fröhner and Sekar walk through four integration fundamentals (coupling dimensions, control flow, delivery semantics and ordering, noisy neighbours in shared queues) and the patterns and trade-offs for each.", "watchIf": "You design event-driven or queue-based systems and want a clear vocabulary for coupling, delivery guarantees, ordering and multi-tenant fairness.", "takeaway": "For each integration, list which coupling dimensions it carries before choosing between REST, a queue or a topic.", "videoId": "_CtnBnmYQC0", "hearts": 441, "views": 504, "likes": 8, "likeRate": 1.13, "thin": true, "rating": null, "numRatings": null, "seat": true}, {"id": 16439, "title": "AI Security Deep Dive, Securing Agents and AI Applications", "speakers": [{"name": "Brian Vermeer", "company": "Snyk"}], "room": "Room 5", "start": "2026-10-06T11:30:00Z", "end": "2026-10-06T14:30:00Z", "type": "Deep Dive", "track": "Security, Trust & Compliance", "level": "INTERMEDIATE", "minutes": 150, "keywords": ["security", "guardrails", "ai", "promptinjection"], "themes": ["Security & supply chain", "LLMs in Java apps", "AI agents & agentic coding"], "tldr": "Vermeer attacks and then hardens a Quarkus/LangChain4j app (chat-memory poisoning, RAG poisoning, over-privileged tools, prompt injection) and shows how MCP servers and agent skills compromise developer machines.", "watchIf": "You ship LLM features in Java apps or run coding agents with MCP servers and skills, and want concrete attacks with matching fixes.", "takeaway": "Sign persisted chat history with an HMAC and refuse to load conversations that fail verification.", "videoId": "5AaJ3H9fG6M", "hearts": 292, "views": 919, "likes": 10, "likeRate": 0.97, "thin": true, "rating": null, "numRatings": null, "seat": true}, {"id": 7022, "title": "Emergent DevEx: The Good, the Bad, and the Legacy", "speakers": [{"name": "Makan Sepehrifar", "company": "Productboard"}, {"name": "Soroosh Khodami", "company": "Rabobank"}], "room": "Room 4", "start": "2026-10-07T10:00:00Z", "end": "2026-10-07T10:50:00Z", "type": "Conference", "track": "People, Practices & Career", "level": "BEGINNER", "minutes": 48, "keywords": ["bureaucracy", "developer experience", "internal tools", "scalability"], "themes": ["Teams, careers & leadership", "Developer experience & tooling"], "tldr": "Sepehrifar and Khodami argue that developer experience in large organisations is shaped less by platform programmes than by small, shared implementation details that developers themselves build and spread.", "watchIf": "You work in or alongside a platform team in a large organisation and want concrete ways to improve developer experience without waiting for a transformation programme.", "takeaway": "Turn something your team already built, such as a parent POM, hardened base image or pipeline template, into a shared component and advocate for it.", "videoId": "9MPYPejgTGo", "hearts": 200, "views": 593, "likes": 1, "likeRate": 0.56, "thin": true, "rating": null, "numRatings": null, "seat": true}, {"id": 8190, "title": "Technical Workshop on AI-Native Tooling for Java Development", "speakers": [{"name": "Juan Antonio Breña 🇪🇸🇪🇺", "company": "IG"}, {"name": "Leandro Loureiro", "company": "ING"}], "room": "BOF 1", "start": "2026-10-06T11:30:00Z", "end": "2026-10-06T14:30:00Z", "type": "Hands-on Lab", "track": "Agentic Engineering & Tooling", "level": "INTERMEDIATE", "minutes": 180, "keywords": ["java", "ai", "agents", "sdlc"], "themes": ["AI agents & agentic coding", "Developer experience & tooling"], "tldr": "A workshop on AI-native Java development: using Skills, Agents, Commands, MCP servers and spec-driven tools across the SDLC, with Spring Boot, Quarkus and Micronaut examples.", "watchIf": "You want a practical, tool-by-tool introduction to agentic workflows for Java projects.", "takeaway": "Write or review an AGENTS.md for one of your Java projects.", "videoId": null, "hearts": 83, "views": null, "likes": null, "likeRate": null, "thin": false, "rating": null, "numRatings": null, "seat": false}, {"id": 8153, "title": "Taming Microservice Overload: Our Journey to Modular Monoliths", "speakers": [{"name": "Mario Klaver", "company": "Port of Rotterdam"}], "room": "Room 8", "start": "2026-10-08T09:50:00Z", "end": "2026-10-08T10:40:00Z", "type": "Conference", "track": "Architecture & Modernization", "level": "INTERMEDIATE", "minutes": 50, "keywords": ["domain-driven design", "microservices", "spring modulith", "modular monoliths"], "themes": ["Architecture & design", "Legacy modernization", "Spring & frameworks"], "tldr": "Klaver explains how the Port of Rotterdam, with five teams running 90+ microservices, used event storming and DDD to find business boundaries and is consolidating capability by capability into Spring Modulith services.", "watchIf": "Your team owns far more microservices than people and you want a candid, in-progress account of consolidating with DDD and Spring Modulith.", "takeaway": "Before splitting or merging services, run a big-picture event storming with as many perspectives in the room as possible.", "videoId": "v86SfHA5ISw", "hearts": 513, "views": 23756, "likes": 142, "likeRate": 0.61, "thin": false, "rating": null, "numRatings": null, "seat": true}, {"id": 23519, "title": "AI Doesn’t Click Buttons: Building APIs for the Agentic Age", "speakers": [{"name": "Kim Lauwers", "company": "Quality Coders BV"}], "room": "Room 9", "start": "2026-10-07T11:05:00Z", "end": "2026-10-07T11:45:00Z", "type": "Lunch Talk", "track": "Architecture & Modernization", "level": "INTERMEDIATE", "minutes": 38, "keywords": ["ai", "agents", "api", "openapi"], "themes": ["Architecture & design", "AI agents & agentic coding"], "tldr": "Lauwers argues that APIs are becoming a primary interface and shows, with a postage-pricing example, how explicit data, meaning, machine-readable errors and next actions let agents act safely.", "watchIf": "You own a business API and want a concrete checklist for making its responses and errors usable by AI agents without letting the model make business decisions.", "takeaway": "Audit one endpoint: replace cryptic parameter names and string responses with explicit fields, units and currency.", "videoId": "QvyIzP4-OvM", "hearts": 163, "views": 555, "likes": 5, "likeRate": 0.87, "thin": true, "rating": null, "numRatings": null, "seat": true}, {"id": 17751, "title": "Cheaper or Better? The Strategic Choice Behind AI Adoption", "speakers": [{"name": "Miriam Sasse", "company": "S&N Invent"}], "room": "Room 4", "start": "2026-10-06T07:30:00Z", "end": "2026-10-06T10:30:00Z", "type": "Deep Dive", "track": "Mind the Geek", "level": "INTERMEDIATE", "minutes": 150, "keywords": ["efficiency", "value", "ai", "automation"], "themes": ["Teams, careers & leadership", "AI agents & agentic coding"], "tldr": "Sasse uses game design as a lens on AI adoption: decide whether AI makes the old game cheaper or creates a new, better one, and redesign roles, rules and gatekeepers.", "watchIf": "You shape how your team or company adopts AI and want a workshop-style framework for questioning strategy, roles and gatekeepers rather than a tool demo; expect much of the recording to be audience discussion.", "takeaway": "Write down whether your AI initiative aims at efficiency or customer value, and test the strategy against the five principles.", "videoId": "Ycdt7IT7Kdw", "hearts": 129, "views": 495, "likes": 4, "likeRate": 0.83, "thin": true, "rating": null, "numRatings": null, "seat": true}, {"id": 52101, "title": "Java Next - From Valhalla to Leyden, from Babylon to Panama, from Amber to Detroit", "speakers": [{"name": "Nicolai Parlog", "company": "Oracle"}], "room": "Room 6", "start": "2026-10-09T09:50:00Z", "end": "2026-10-09T10:40:00Z", "type": "Conference", "track": "Java Language & Platform", "level": "BEGINNER", "minutes": 52, "keywords": ["panama", "java", "valhalla", "leyden"], "themes": ["Java language & JDK", "JVM performance & runtime"], "tldr": "Parlog tours Amber's pattern work, Leyden's AOT cache, Valhalla's JDK 28 value class preview and Babylon's code reflection, then speeds through Loom, Panama, Lilliput and Detroit.", "watchIf": "You want one map of which OpenJDK project features are final, in preview or still on the whiteboard as of JDK 28.", "takeaway": "Add a Leyden training run to your build or a canary deployment and start production from the AOT cache.", "videoId": "ufhs9THbYZI", "hearts": 355, "views": 6451, "likes": 46, "likeRate": 0.73, "thin": false, "rating": null, "numRatings": null, "seat": true}, {"id": 7020, "title": "Who to blame? The AI, The Programmer, or The Prompt?", "speakers": [{"name": "Makan Sepehrifar", "company": "Productboard"}], "room": "Room 9", "start": "2026-10-08T10:55:00Z", "end": "2026-10-08T11:35:00Z", "type": "Lunch Talk", "track": "Security, Trust & Compliance", "level": "BEGINNER", "minutes": 40, "keywords": ["shared responsibility", "cve", "vulnerabilities", "ai security"], "themes": ["Security & supply chain", "AI agents & agentic coding"], "tldr": "Sepehrifar puts four real AI-tooling CVEs on mock trial, lets the audience vote on who is to blame, and concludes that model providers, tool builders and developers share responsibility.", "watchIf": "You use coding agents or MCP servers and want a concrete, entertaining tour of how real AI-tool vulnerabilities were exploited and fixed.", "takeaway": "Review agent configuration files (rules, instructions, settings) in your repositories as you would code.", "videoId": "VmPMUGImB-M", "hearts": 195, "views": 412, "likes": 0, "likeRate": 0.57, "thin": true, "rating": null, "numRatings": null, "seat": true}, {"id": 8171, "title": "0 to 350 Engineers on Claude Code: What Could Go Wrong?", "speakers": [{"name": "Nicolas Martignole", "company": "Back Market"}], "room": "Room 9", "start": "2026-10-07T13:10:00Z", "end": "2026-10-07T14:00:00Z", "type": "Conference", "track": "People, Practices & Career", "level": "BEGINNER", "minutes": 45, "keywords": ["coding", "developers", "ai", "agents"], "themes": ["AI agents & agentic coding", "Teams, careers & leadership"], "tldr": "Martignole on Back Market's organic Claude Code rollout, its four 'collisions' (huge PRs, cross-team changes, an $86,000 bill, people) and the fixes: OpenRouter, OpenCode, budgets, elected experts.", "watchIf": "You are rolling out coding agents beyond a pilot team and need to control cost, review load and cross-team ownership.", "takeaway": "Write a CONTRIBUTING.md and tidy CODEOWNERS in every repo so agents and other teams know which changes you accept.", "videoId": "HzbNrEDZkYU", "hearts": 385, "views": 852, "likes": 8, "likeRate": 0.89, "thin": true, "rating": null, "numRatings": null, "seat": true}, {"id": 25671, "title": "State of Agentic Coding", "speakers": [{"name": "Daniela Petruzalek", "company": "Google Cloud"}], "room": "Room 3", "start": "2026-10-08T09:50:00Z", "end": "2026-10-08T10:40:00Z", "type": "Conference", "track": "Development Practices", "level": "INTERMEDIATE", "minutes": 48, "keywords": ["ai-assisted", "autonomous", "softwareengineering", "agents"], "themes": ["AI agents & agentic coding", "Developer experience & tooling"], "tldr": "Petruzalek gives an opinionated tour of what works in agentic coding today: freestyle prompts with grill-me, tiny rules files, skills loaded on demand, dynamic sub-agent swarms, and Antigravity sidecars, all watched for 'rot'.", "watchIf": "You already use coding agents daily and want a practitioner's current view on rules, hooks, skills, sub-agents and Antigravity, with live demos.", "takeaway": "Trim your AGENTS.md/CLAUDE.md/GEMINI.md to the few instructions that really need to be in every session.", "videoId": "nHRSwiFNQ9E", "hearts": 545, "views": 3488, "likes": 21, "likeRate": 0.65, "thin": false, "rating": null, "numRatings": null, "seat": true}, {"id": 22916, "title": "Java and the Next Generation of Programming Models", "speakers": [{"name": "Ana-Maria Mihalceanu", "company": "Oracle"}], "room": "Room 9", "start": "2026-10-08T11:50:00Z", "end": "2026-10-08T12:40:00Z", "type": "Conference", "track": "Java Language & Platform", "level": "ADVANCED", "minutes": 40, "keywords": ["java", "inference", "foreignfunctionmemory", "codereflection"], "themes": ["LLMs in Java apps", "Java language & JDK"], "tldr": "Mihalceanu runs a local LLM from Java today by calling ONNX Runtime GenAI through jextract-generated FFM bindings, then writes a Llama model as plain Java that Project Babylon's code reflection turns into an ONNX graph.", "watchIf": "You want to run AI models from Java without Python glue, or want to see how Project Babylon's code reflection actually works.", "takeaway": "Try jextract on the C header of a native library you currently wrap with JNI or a third-party binding.", "videoId": "kuUeHD_k5fs", "hearts": 251, "views": 1052, "likes": 6, "likeRate": 0.69, "thin": true, "rating": null, "numRatings": null, "seat": true}, {"id": 24233, "title": "Build the Builder: A Java Coding Agent in Your IDE with ACP", "speakers": [{"name": "Vadim Briliantov", "company": "JetBrains"}], "room": "Room 3", "start": "2026-10-07T10:00:00Z", "end": "2026-10-07T10:50:00Z", "type": "Conference", "track": "Agentic Engineering & Tooling", "level": "INTERMEDIATE", "minutes": 50, "keywords": ["guardrails", "orchestration", "agent", "ide"], "themes": ["AI agents & agentic coding", "LLMs in Java apps", "Developer experience & tooling"], "tldr": "Briliantov vibe-codes a Koog-based coding agent with a typed workflow, connects it to IntelliJ through the Agent Client Protocol, and shows IDE skills that cut agents' token use.", "watchIf": "You want to build an in-house coding agent on the JVM, or understand how ACP lets agents and IDEs work together.", "takeaway": "Model your agent as a workflow with typed step outputs and a verify loop, using Koog, Spring AI or LangChain4j.", "videoId": "y4F1XBtQeDw", "hearts": 398, "views": 1555, "likes": 11, "likeRate": 0.76, "thin": true, "rating": null, "numRatings": null, "seat": true}, {"id": 4087, "title": "Harness Engineering: Building the System Around Your AI Coding Agent", "speakers": [{"name": "JI Darwish", "company": "Xomnia"}], "room": "Room 5", "start": "2026-10-07T12:00:00Z", "end": "2026-10-07T12:50:00Z", "type": "Conference", "track": "Architecture & Modernization", "level": "INTERMEDIATE", "minutes": 40, "keywords": ["tool calls", "ai agents", "context management", "safety guardrails"], "themes": ["AI agents & agentic coding", "Testing & quality"], "tldr": "Darwish explains a coding agent as model plus harness and argues that developers control the outer harness: guides, deterministic sensors, gated checks, bounded repair loops and explicit acceptance criteria.", "watchIf": "You use Claude Code or similar agents on a long-lived Java codebase and want a mental model for adding deterministic checks and bounded loops around them. Note that there is no live coding.", "takeaway": "Add deterministic architecture tests (ArchUnit or similar) for the boundaries your AGENTS.md describes.", "videoId": "6N6qFqn1_Uo", "hearts": 605, "views": 2357, "likes": 13, "likeRate": 0.63, "thin": true, "rating": null, "numRatings": null, "seat": true}, {"id": 15359, "title": "Is AI killing XP practices? (Or are we letting it?)", "speakers": [{"name": "Nacho Cougil", "company": "Dynatrace"}], "room": "Room 5", "start": "2026-10-09T08:40:00Z", "end": "2026-10-09T09:30:00Z", "type": "Conference", "track": "Development Practices", "level": "INTERMEDIATE", "minutes": 49, "keywords": ["refactoring", "xp", "ai", "testing"], "themes": ["Testing & quality", "AI agents & agentic coding"], "tldr": "Cougil puts seven XP practices on trial against AI's arguments for skipping them and concludes each matters more, because AI amplifies bad habits as much as good ones.", "watchIf": "Your team has adopted AI coding assistants and you need arguments for keeping TDD, pairing, CI and small releases.", "takeaway": "Audit your tests: check that they verify the behaviour the business needs rather than mirroring the implementation.", "videoId": "wrV4f83UMWI", "hearts": 300, "views": 2278, "likes": 11, "likeRate": 0.58, "thin": true, "rating": null, "numRatings": null, "seat": true}, {"id": 5537, "title": "The Quarkus advantage: Turning build-time metaprogramming into runtime performances", "speakers": [{"name": "Mario Fusco", "company": "IBM"}, {"name": "Georgios Andrianakis", "company": "IBM"}, {"name": "Francesco Nigro", "company": "IBM"}], "room": "Room 9", "start": "2026-10-05T11:30:00Z", "end": "2026-10-05T14:30:00Z", "type": "Deep Dive", "track": "Architecture & Modernization", "level": "INTERMEDIATE", "minutes": 148, "keywords": ["performance", "reflection", "quarkus", "metaprogramming"], "themes": ["JVM performance & runtime", "Spring & frameworks"], "tldr": "Three Quarkus engineers show how build-time indexing and bytecode generation replace reflection, and how a reflection-free Jackson serializer was slower until they fixed register spilling and profile pollution.", "watchIf": "You write Quarkus extensions or performance-sensitive Java libraries and want to know what reflection and JIT heuristics actually cost.", "takeaway": "Remeasure performance claims from a few years ago; JDK and library changes (JEP 416, Jackson 3 method handles) can erase or reverse an optimization.", "videoId": "SgK0zMNayq0", "hearts": 80, "views": 1140, "likes": 19, "likeRate": 1.32, "thin": true, "rating": null, "numRatings": null, "seat": true}, {"id": 16418, "title": "Solving Distributed Transactions with the Saga Pattern and Temporal", "speakers": [{"name": "Andrei Sulyz", "company": "Skyro"}], "room": "Room 5", "start": "2026-10-06T14:50:00Z", "end": "2026-10-06T15:20:00Z", "type": "Tools-in-Action", "track": "Architecture & Modernization", "level": "INTERMEDIATE", "minutes": 29, "keywords": ["saga", "distributedtransactions", "consistency", "temporal"], "themes": ["Architecture & design"], "tldr": "Sulyz live-debugs a Temporal Saga for a synchronous payment hold, fixing compensation ordering, cancellation scopes and activity cancellation races, and ends with reconciliation as the final safety net.", "watchIf": "You implement Sagas on Temporal, especially for synchronous, cancellable operations where a missed compensation leaves customer money frozen.", "takeaway": "Review Saga code copied from older Temporal examples for compensation ordering and a detached cancellation scope.", "videoId": "c45C2wNSZFc", "hearts": 145, "views": 2322, "likes": 26, "likeRate": 1.05, "thin": false, "rating": null, "numRatings": null, "seat": true}, {"id": 22911, "title": "One tool to rule them all: bash, a tiny LLM, and the birth of a coding agent", "speakers": [{"name": "Philippe Charrière", "company": "Docker"}], "room": "Room 5", "start": "2026-10-05T16:20:00Z", "end": "2026-10-05T16:50:00Z", "type": "Tools-in-Action", "track": "Agentic Engineering & Tooling", "level": "BEGINNER", "minutes": 30, "keywords": ["prompt engineering", "bash", "coding agents", "language model"], "themes": ["AI agents & agentic coding", "Developer experience & tooling"], "tldr": "Charrière builds a coding agent live in Go with Genkit and a small local model on Docker Model Runner, showing that one bash tool can replace a catalogue of dedicated tools.", "watchIf": "You use coding agents and want to see how small the core loop is, or want to run one on a local model.", "takeaway": "Try replacing a pile of narrow tools or MCP servers with one well-described bash tool when context is tight.", "videoId": "wWfj4AJahlQ", "hearts": 160, "views": 1248, "likes": 8, "likeRate": 0.72, "thin": true, "rating": null, "numRatings": null, "seat": true}, {"id": 17765, "title": "Can AI Build a Jakarta EE Runtime? The Vidocq Experiment", "speakers": [{"name": "Antoine Sabot-Durand", "company": "SCIAM"}, {"name": "yann Blazart", "company": "SCIAM"}, {"name": "Sebastien Blanc", "company": "SCIAM"}], "room": "Room 4", "start": "2026-10-05T11:30:00Z", "end": "2026-10-05T14:30:00Z", "type": "Deep Dive", "track": "Agentic Engineering & Tooling", "level": "BEGINNER", "minutes": 145, "keywords": ["cdi", "jpms", "microprofile", "jakartaee"], "themes": ["AI agents & agentic coding", "Spring & frameworks", "Testing & quality"], "tldr": "The Vidocq team explains how AI agents, held to Jakarta EE specs and TCKs, built a certified zero-dependency runtime in six months, and where the AI cheated along the way.", "watchIf": "You want a long, candid case study of AI agents building a large spec-driven Java codebase, including the cheating, rule drift and TCK gaps, plus a look at a new zero-dependency Jakarta EE runtime.", "takeaway": "Give your agent an executable judge (a TCK, contract tests or a spec suite) and make it run that judge rather than trusting its own report.", "videoId": "dq5rueVhiIM", "hearts": 24, "views": 752, "likes": 5, "likeRate": 0.76, "thin": true, "rating": null, "numRatings": null, "seat": true}, {"id": 50107, "title": "Building an Agent Ready Platform", "speakers": [{"name": "Timo Salm", "company": "Broadcom"}], "room": "Room 7", "start": "2026-10-07T14:40:00Z", "end": "2026-10-07T15:30:00Z", "type": "Conference", "track": "Agentic Engineering & Tooling", "level": "BEGINNER", "minutes": 49, "keywords": ["observability", "platform", "guardrails", "agents"], "themes": ["Cloud native & Kubernetes", "Security & supply chain", "AI agents & agentic coding"], "tldr": "Salm argues that shipping agents is a platform problem: an AI gateway, an MCP gateway with approved tools, agent identity, shared guardrails and OpenTelemetry-based cost observability belong in the platform.", "watchIf": "You are on a platform team, or a developer about to put a Spring AI agent in production, and want a checklist of what the platform should provide for agents.", "takeaway": "Ask your platform team for a single model endpoint with virtual keys instead of embedding provider API keys and model names in your agent.", "videoId": "q58Y2tyRdCE", "hearts": 120, "views": 368, "likes": 1, "likeRate": 0.67, "thin": true, "rating": null, "numRatings": null, "seat": true}, {"id": 5526, "title": "From Developer to Story Builder: Engineering a Novel, an ARG, and a Transmedia World with AI", "speakers": [{"name": "Raphael Semeteys", "company": "Worldline"}], "room": "Room 4", "start": "2026-10-06T10:40:00Z", "end": "2026-10-06T11:20:00Z", "type": "Lunch Talk", "track": "Mind the Geek", "level": "BEGINNER", "minutes": 40, "keywords": ["architecture", "ai", "devops", "transmedia"], "themes": ["AI agents & agentic coding", "Hardware, robots & fun"], "tldr": "Semeteys wrote his techno-thriller like a software system, with lore-as-code Markdown, an AI writing harness, three LLM judges and a Pandoc build, then extended it into an alternate reality game.", "watchIf": "You use spec-driven agent workflows at work and want to see the same patterns (context files, harness, multi-model review, build pipeline) applied to a long creative project.", "takeaway": "Put the bulk of the effort into the context (lore or spec) before generating anything.", "videoId": "496nv8jsWhQ", "hearts": 62, "views": 2146, "likes": 10, "likeRate": 0.57, "thin": true, "rating": null, "numRatings": null, "seat": true}, {"id": 10045, "title": "Java, LEGO & AI: Teaching the Next Generation to Build, Not Just Prompt", "speakers": [{"name": "Eddy Vos", "company": "Devoxx4Kids Netherlands"}], "room": "Room 6", "start": "2026-10-05T10:40:00Z", "end": "2026-10-05T11:20:00Z", "type": "Lunch Talk", "track": "Mind the Geek", "level": "BEGINNER", "minutes": 24, "keywords": ["java", "lego spike", "programming education", "generative ai"], "themes": ["Hardware, robots & fun", "Teams, careers & leadership"], "tldr": "Vos argues kids should still learn to code in the AI era, and recounts how Devoxx4Kids Netherlands got Java to drive LEGO SPIKE robots through a Raspberry Pi bridge.", "watchIf": "You want to teach kids programming with robots or volunteer at Devoxx4Kids and want to see how Java can drive LEGO SPIKE hardware.", "takeaway": "Volunteer for a Devoxx4Kids workshop, or offer your company as a host for a Devoxx4Kids day.", "videoId": "_VZg6dxlGaM", "hearts": 131, "views": 1658, "likes": 6, "likeRate": 0.52, "thin": true, "rating": null, "numRatings": null, "seat": true}, {"id": 24224, "title": "Post-Quantum Today: How Your Passwords Will Be Safe in a Quantum Future", "speakers": [{"name": "Julian Burr", "company": "Sonar AI"}], "room": "Room 7", "start": "2026-10-07T10:00:00Z", "end": "2026-10-07T10:50:00Z", "type": "Conference", "track": "Security, Trust & Compliance", "level": "ADVANCED", "minutes": 36, "keywords": ["quantum", "lattice", "rsa", "pqc"], "themes": ["Security & supply chain", "Hardware, robots & fun"], "tldr": "Burr explains how qubits and Shor's algorithm threaten RSA, why 'harvest now, decrypt later' makes it urgent today, and how lattice-based schemes like Kyber (ML-KEM) replace it.", "watchIf": "You want an accessible but technically honest explanation of how quantum computers threaten RSA and what post-quantum migration looks like today.", "takeaway": "Treat long-lived sensitive data as already at risk from harvest-now-decrypt-later and prioritise migrating it.", "videoId": "yUMnuh5U5k4", "hearts": 262, "views": 479, "likes": 3, "likeRate": 0.77, "thin": true, "rating": null, "numRatings": null, "seat": true}, {"id": 22920, "title": "Using Structured Concurrency and Scoped Values to Create a Virtual Threads Based Asynchronous Application", "speakers": [{"name": "Ana-Maria Mihalceanu", "company": "Oracle"}, {"name": "José Paumard", "company": "Oracle"}], "room": "BOF 1", "start": "2026-10-05T11:30:00Z", "end": "2026-10-05T14:30:00Z", "type": "Hands-on Lab", "track": "Java Language & Platform", "level": "INTERMEDIATE", "minutes": 180, "keywords": ["structuredconcurrency", "scopedvalues", "virtualthreads", "loom"], "themes": ["Java language & JDK", "JVM performance & runtime"], "tldr": "A lab building a virtual-thread web application with structured concurrency and scoped values, then comparing the result to a reactive approach.", "watchIf": "You want hands-on experience with the Loom APIs as an alternative to reactive programming.", "takeaway": "Try replacing a ThreadLocal in your code with a ScopedValue.", "videoId": null, "hearts": 75, "views": null, "likes": null, "likeRate": null, "thin": false, "rating": null, "numRatings": null, "seat": false}, {"id": 20651, "title": "From Install to Insight: A Hands-On GPU Lab for Java Developers", "speakers": [{"name": "Thanos Stratikopoulos", "company": "Beestream.ai | TornadoVM"}, {"name": "Christos Kotselidis", "company": "Beestream.ai, University of Manchester"}, {"name": "Mary Xekalaki", "company": "beestream.ai | TornadoVM"}, {"name": "Michalis Papadimitriou", "company": "Neo4j | University of Manchester"}], "room": "BOF 2", "start": "2026-10-05T14:50:00Z", "end": "2026-10-05T16:50:00Z", "type": "Hands-on Lab", "track": "Java Language & Platform", "level": "BEGINNER", "minutes": 120, "keywords": ["java", "tornadovm", "llm", "gpu"], "themes": ["LLMs in Java apps", "JVM performance & runtime", "Hardware, robots & fun"], "tldr": "A beginner lab on TornadoVM covering simplified installation, new backends, profiling tools and running a local GPU-backed LLM in pure Java connected to DevoxxGenie.", "watchIf": "You want to run LLMs or compute kernels on a GPU from Java without GPU programming experience.", "takeaway": "Install the TornadoVM SDK via SDKMAN! and try a local GPU-backed LLM.", "videoId": null, "hearts": 31, "views": null, "likes": null, "likeRate": null, "thin": false, "rating": null, "numRatings": null, "seat": false}, {"id": 4086, "title": "AI Made Building Cheap. Now Decisions Are the Bottleneck.", "speakers": [{"name": "Dave Sammut", "company": "Equal Experts"}, {"name": "João Rosa", "company": "João Rosa Consulting"}], "room": "Room 9", "start": "2026-10-07T14:40:00Z", "end": "2026-10-07T15:30:00Z", "type": "Conference", "track": "People, Practices & Career", "level": "ADVANCED", "minutes": 42, "keywords": ["discovery", "architecture", "ai", "decision-making"], "themes": ["Teams, careers & leadership", "Architecture & design", "Legacy modernization"], "tldr": "Rosa and Sammut argue that AI speeds up building but exposes how poorly organizations understand themselves, and show, through an insurance case, how AI-assisted discovery lenses and small reversible hypotheses lead to better decisions.", "watchIf": "You are an architect or engineering leader in a large enterprise whose AI rollout is producing agents nobody uses, rising token bills or a stalled migration.", "takeaway": "Before rolling out AI, spend a short, bounded period mapping where work actually hurts, using recorded conversations and an LLM to synthesize them.", "videoId": "2pSyF44THEc", "hearts": 326, "views": 327, "likes": 0, "likeRate": 0.61, "thin": true, "rating": null, "numRatings": null, "seat": true}, {"id": 9046, "title": "rm -rf self-doubt: Debugging Impostor Syndrome for Developers", "speakers": [{"name": "Vincent Mayers", "company": "Neo4j"}], "room": "Room 5", "start": "2026-10-07T15:50:00Z", "end": "2026-10-07T16:40:00Z", "type": "Conference", "track": "People, Practices & Career", "level": "INTERMEDIATE", "minutes": 39, "keywords": ["growth", "impostersyndrome", "neuroscience", "selfdoubt"], "themes": ["Teams, careers & leadership"], "tldr": "Mayers uses his own route from a pottery degree into Java DevRel to argue that impostor syndrome is a normal fight-or-flight response, and that a knowledge gap should not be treated as a judgement on yourself.", "watchIf": "You often feel you don't belong on your team or on stage, or you lead people who do, and want a practical, personal framing.", "takeaway": "When stuck, rephrase 'I don't understand this' as 'I don't understand this yet' and list what you need to learn.", "videoId": "Tfrt19d3TEA", "hearts": 288, "views": 323, "likes": 6, "likeRate": 1.13, "thin": true, "rating": null, "numRatings": null, "seat": true}, {"id": 10021, "title": "Java Script (No, Not That One): Modern Automation with Java", "speakers": [{"name": "Loïc Magnette", "company": "Oniryx"}], "room": "Room 5", "start": "2026-10-06T16:20:00Z", "end": "2026-10-06T16:50:00Z", "type": "Tools-in-Action", "track": "Java Language & Platform", "level": "BEGINNER", "minutes": 30, "keywords": ["jbang", "scripting", "java", "automation"], "themes": ["Java language & JDK", "Developer experience & tooling"], "tldr": "Magnette live-codes a 'dog' clone of cat to show that modern Java plus JBang, Picocli, GraalVM and TamboUI covers the jobs you currently write in Bash.", "watchIf": "You still reach for Bash or Python for small automation jobs and would rather use the Java you write every day.", "takeaway": "Write your next throwaway automation as a single Java file run with `java` and a shebang instead of Bash.", "videoId": "pgVJWp-SjxI", "hearts": 103, "views": 2333, "likes": 22, "likeRate": 0.92, "thin": false, "rating": null, "numRatings": null, "seat": true}, {"id": 19748, "title": "Snake in 10 Lines: Learning More by Coding Less", "speakers": [{"name": "Guus de Wit", "company": "adesso"}], "room": "Room 7", "start": "2026-10-08T07:30:00Z", "end": "2026-10-08T08:20:00Z", "type": "Conference", "track": "Mind the Geek", "level": "BEGINNER", "minutes": 48, "keywords": ["coding", "snake", "minimalism", "constraints"], "themes": ["Hardware, robots & fun", "Java language & JDK"], "tldr": "De Wit live-codes a playable Snake in Kotlin, using only the JDK, in 10 lines of at most 130 characters, arguing that constraints force better solutions and that concise code brings understanding.", "watchIf": "You enjoy code golf and live coding, or want a light talk on how constraints push you to rethink a design.", "takeaway": "Try a constrained side project (line, time or token budget) to learn APIs and shortcuts you would not touch otherwise.", "videoId": "LD2tKMpToYg", "hearts": 162, "views": 16008, "likes": 74, "likeRate": 0.48, "thin": false, "rating": null, "numRatings": null, "seat": true}, {"id": 10020, "title": "From AI to Agent: A Field Guide to Agentic Patterns in LangChain4j", "speakers": [{"name": "Loïc Magnette", "company": "Oniryx"}], "room": "Room 6", "start": "2026-10-05T07:30:00Z", "end": "2026-10-05T10:30:00Z", "type": "Deep Dive", "track": "Agentic Engineering & Tooling", "level": "INTERMEDIATE", "minutes": 144, "keywords": ["observability", "agentic", "supervisor", "workflow"], "themes": ["AI agents & agentic coding", "LLMs in Java apps", "Architecture & design"], "tldr": "Magnette walks through every workflow and agentic pattern in LangChain4j's agentic modules with live demos, arguing you should start with plain workflows and add autonomy only when needed.", "watchIf": "You build LLM features in Java and need to choose how much control to give agents, with working LangChain4j code for each option.", "takeaway": "Start new agentic features as sequence, loop, parallel or conditional workflows and move to autonomous patterns only when they fall short.", "videoId": "IXcQYp51PLM", "hearts": 118, "views": 1808, "likes": 13, "likeRate": 0.76, "thin": true, "rating": null, "numRatings": null, "seat": true}, {"id": 12751, "title": "Microservices Without the Distributed Mess", "speakers": [{"name": "Steven Hillaert", "company": "Sofico"}], "room": "Room 9", "start": "2026-10-08T14:30:00Z", "end": "2026-10-08T15:20:00Z", "type": "Conference", "track": "Architecture & Modernization", "level": "ADVANCED", "minutes": 46, "keywords": ["boundaries", "ddd", "orchestration", "microservices"], "themes": ["Architecture & design"], "tldr": "Hillaert argues that microservice failures come from ownership, boundaries and relationships rather than technology, and uses strategic DDD and context mapping, with leasing-industry examples, as tools for thinking about them.", "watchIf": "You are splitting a system into services, or living with a distributed monolith, and want a vocabulary for ownership and coupling that goes beyond the call graph.", "takeaway": "For each service, name the single accountable team and check that the data its business rules need lives inside its boundary.", "videoId": "eFSEg54mwVU", "hearts": 237, "views": 480, "likes": 5, "likeRate": 0.92, "thin": true, "rating": null, "numRatings": null, "seat": true}, {"id": 24212, "title": "GraalVM Next: Layers, Crema, and WebAssembly in Action", "speakers": [{"name": "Alina Yurenko", "company": "Oracle"}], "room": "Room 4", "start": "2026-10-07T14:40:00Z", "end": "2026-10-07T15:30:00Z", "type": "Conference", "track": "Development Practices", "level": "INTERMEDIATE", "minutes": 47, "keywords": ["graalvm", "class loading", "native image", "webassembly"], "themes": ["JVM performance & runtime", "Cloud native & Kubernetes"], "tldr": "Yurenko and GraalVM founder Thomas Wuerthinger demo three projects that loosen Native Image's limits: shared base layers, runtime class loading with Crema, and compiling Spring apps to WebAssembly.", "watchIf": "You run Java microservices as native images, or gave up on Native Image because of build times, dynamic class generation or the closed world.", "takeaway": "Upgrade to the latest GraalVM release before tuning anything; the image size and memory gains come without configuration.", "videoId": "OLQ98Ko4p3Q", "hearts": 120, "views": 918, "likes": 20, "likeRate": 1.55, "thin": false, "rating": null, "numRatings": null, "seat": true}, {"id": 50101, "title": "The Java Story | The Official Documentary", "speakers": [{"name": "Georges Saab", "company": "Oracle"}], "room": "Room 9", "start": "2026-10-06T15:35:00Z", "end": "2026-10-06T16:50:00Z", "type": "Documentary", "track": "Java Language & Platform", "level": "BEGINNER", "minutes": 75, "keywords": ["java", "jakartaee", "jvm", "openjdk"], "themes": ["Java language & JDK"], "tldr": "A screening of the official Java documentary, tracing 31 years of Java from Sun Microsystems through OpenJDK and the move from Java EE to Jakarta EE, told by those involved.", "watchIf": "You are curious about the people and history behind Java.", "takeaway": "Watch it for context on how Java's community and governance came to be.", "videoId": null, "hearts": 98, "views": null, "likes": null, "likeRate": null, "thin": false, "rating": null, "numRatings": null, "seat": true}, {"id": 4051, "title": "CRAcking Compliance - The EU Cyber Resilience Act", "speakers": [{"name": "Christian Schabetsberger", "company": "Finxit IT Solutions"}], "room": "Room 7", "start": "2026-10-07T13:10:00Z", "end": "2026-10-07T14:00:00Z", "type": "Conference", "track": "Security, Trust & Compliance", "level": "BEGINNER", "minutes": 45, "keywords": ["vulnerability management", "sbom", "dependabot", "cyber resilience act"], "themes": ["Security & supply chain"], "tldr": "Schabetsberger explains what the EU Cyber Resilience Act requires of software makers, then compares free tools for SBOMs, vulnerability scanning and dependency updates, finding they disagree widely.", "watchIf": "You ship software in the EU and need a practical engineering reading of the CRA plus an honest comparison of SBOM and scanning tools.", "takeaway": "Hold a time-boxed threat-modelling session per release and commit the result to your repository as your CRA risk assessment.", "videoId": "QQcsZNf0eWs", "hearts": 175, "views": 381, "likes": 4, "likeRate": 0.91, "thin": true, "rating": null, "numRatings": null, "seat": true}, {"id": 50106, "title": "From User Prompt to JPA Query: Comparing Tool Calling, Structured Output & Hybrid Approach", "speakers": [{"name": "Sebastian Kühnau", "company": "Vaadin"}], "room": "Room 5", "start": "2026-10-07T11:05:00Z", "end": "2026-10-07T11:45:00Z", "type": "Lunch Talk", "track": "Agentic Engineering & Tooling", "level": "BEGINNER", "minutes": 39, "keywords": ["java", "llm", "structuredoutput", "filtering"], "themes": ["LLMs in Java apps", "Spring & frameworks", "Frontend & UI"], "tldr": "Kühnau compares tool calling, structured output and a hybrid approach in Spring AI for turning natural-language filter requests into JPA queries for a Vaadin grid, benchmarked on local models.", "watchIf": "You want users to filter a data grid in plain language and need to choose between tool calling and structured output, ideally on a local model.", "takeaway": "Have the LLM produce a typed filter object and translate it into a JPA Specification yourself, rather than handing table data to the model.", "videoId": "-2vEfnB2mRw", "hearts": 73, "views": 570, "likes": 4, "likeRate": 0.79, "thin": true, "rating": null, "numRatings": null, "seat": true}, {"id": 17786, "title": "Agent Loops, Decoded: Fundamentals to Agentic Patterns in Spring AI", "speakers": [{"name": "Christian Tzolov", "company": "Spring Team @ Broadcom"}], "room": "Room 5", "start": "2026-10-05T07:30:00Z", "end": "2026-10-05T10:30:00Z", "type": "Deep Dive", "track": "Agentic Engineering & Tooling", "level": "INTERMEDIATE", "minutes": 162, "keywords": ["toolcalling", "spring ai", "rag", "agents"], "themes": ["LLMs in Java apps", "AI agents & agentic coding", "Spring & frameworks"], "tldr": "Tzolov live-codes Spring AI from ChatClient basics to skills, subagents and durable memory, showing that every agentic pattern is built from context, advisors, recursion and tool calls.", "watchIf": "You build LLM features in Java and want to see how agent skills, tool search, subagents and memory are actually implemented on top of Spring AI's advisors and tool calls.", "takeaway": "Inspect the actual payload sent to the model, with a logging advisor or an inspector, before tuning prompts.", "videoId": "Q91obeWeWNQ", "hearts": 216, "views": 2613, "likes": 34, "likeRate": 1.19, "thin": false, "rating": null, "numRatings": null, "seat": true}, {"id": 25654, "title": "Beyond Code: The Intent-Driven IDE for the Agentic Age", "speakers": [{"name": "Bruno Borges", "company": "GitHub"}], "room": "Room 4", "start": "2026-10-05T15:35:00Z", "end": "2026-10-05T16:05:00Z", "type": "Tools-in-Action", "track": "Agentic Engineering & Tooling", "level": "BEGINNER", "minutes": 35, "keywords": ["specification", "intent", "agents", "automation"], "themes": ["AI agents & agentic coding", "Developer experience & tooling"], "tldr": "Borges demos the GitHub Copilot app as an 'intent-driven environment': write a spec, split it into issues, dispatch parallel agents, and extend the tool with custom Canvas panels.", "watchIf": "You want to see what a spec-to-PR workflow with parallel agents looks like in GitHub's Copilot tooling, including where it trips up.", "takeaway": "Commit and review the specification before handing its issues to parallel agents.", "videoId": "sexe_tnhAOI", "hearts": 88, "views": 4358, "likes": 20, "likeRate": 0.52, "thin": false, "rating": null, "numRatings": null, "seat": true}, {"id": 25048, "title": "Java on Kubernetes Without Container Images: The WebAssembly Way", "speakers": [{"name": "Bruno Borges", "company": "GitHub"}], "room": "Room 4", "start": "2026-10-08T07:30:00Z", "end": "2026-10-08T08:20:00Z", "type": "Conference", "track": "Cloud Native, Data & Infrastructure", "level": "INTERMEDIATE", "minutes": 53, "keywords": ["kubernetes", "springboot", "containerd", "webassembly"], "themes": ["Cloud native & Kubernetes", "Security & supply chain", "AI agents & agentic coding"], "tldr": "Borges presents Brewlet, an experimental containerd shim and operator that runs Java JARs pushed as OCI artifacts on Kubernetes with a node-provided JDK, so platform teams can patch the JDK and dependencies without app rebuilds.", "watchIf": "You run Java on Kubernetes and are tired of rebuilding every service image for each JDK or base-image CVE, or you want to see how runtime classes and containerd shims work.", "takeaway": "Decide explicitly whether app teams or a platform team should own JDK and dependency patching in your organisation.", "videoId": "fszyRIZXjQM", "hearts": 223, "views": 5677, "likes": 61, "likeRate": 1.05, "thin": false, "rating": null, "numRatings": null, "seat": true}, {"id": 49253, "title": "Engineering for the Long Haul: Operating Open Source at Scale", "speakers": [{"name": "Sven Sellen", "company": "TuxCare Inc."}], "room": "Room 6", "start": "2026-10-07T11:05:00Z", "end": "2026-10-07T11:45:00Z", "type": "Lunch Talk", "track": "Development Practices", "level": "BEGINNER", "minutes": 33, "keywords": ["security", "open-source", "compliance", "patching"], "themes": ["Security & supply chain", "Legacy modernization"], "tldr": "A TuxCare sales pitch for its patched open-source package repository, end-of-life security support and rebootless Linux kernel patching, framed by statistics on unpatched systems and supply-chain malware.", "watchIf": "You are responsible for end-of-life Linux or open-source dependencies and want an overview of TuxCare's commercial offering; there is no technical depth or demo.", "takeaway": "Check how many of your production dependencies and base images are past upstream end of life, and decide whether to upgrade or buy extended support.", "videoId": "Pg5kS8l9CpI", "hearts": 36, "views": 376, "likes": 0, "likeRate": 0.58, "thin": true, "rating": null, "numRatings": null, "seat": true}, {"id": 21655, "title": "What’s happening with Project Amber?", "speakers": [{"name": "Gavin Bierman", "company": "Oracle"}], "room": "Room 6", "start": "2026-10-09T08:40:00Z", "end": "2026-10-09T09:30:00Z", "type": "Conference", "track": "Java Language & Platform", "level": "ADVANCED", "minutes": 52, "keywords": ["reconstruction", "deconstruction", "java", "pattern-matching"], "themes": ["Java language & JDK", "Architecture & design"], "tldr": "Bierman reviews Amber's themes and previews experiments: primitive patterns with exact conversions, deconstruction statements, constant patterns, record with-expressions, and data interfaces that give interfaces record-style patterns.", "watchIf": "You use records, sealed types and pattern matching and want to see where the Java language team is taking them next.", "takeaway": "Model a domain's variants as records under a sealed interface and process them with exhaustive switches instead of a flat class with optional getters.", "videoId": "Go76-9Q51wU", "hearts": 193, "views": 3725, "likes": 28, "likeRate": 0.77, "thin": false, "rating": null, "numRatings": null, "seat": true}, {"id": 5516, "title": "Wiring Up the AI Mesh: Building A2A-Compliant Java Agents", "speakers": [{"name": "Emmanuel Hugonnet", "company": "IBM"}, {"name": "Mario Fusco", "company": "IBM"}, {"name": "Kevin Dubois", "company": "IBM"}], "room": "BOF 1", "start": "2026-10-05T14:50:00Z", "end": "2026-10-05T16:50:00Z", "type": "Hands-on Lab", "track": "Agentic Engineering & Tooling", "level": "BEGINNER", "minutes": 120, "keywords": ["microprofile", "generativeai", "jakartaee", "langchain4j"], "themes": ["AI agents & agentic coding", "LLMs in Java apps"], "tldr": "A lab on building Agent-to-Agent (A2A) protocol agents in Java with LangChain4j and the A2A Java SDK across Spring Boot and Quarkus, plus a Python agent and a router.", "watchIf": "You want to build interoperable multi-agent systems in Java.", "takeaway": "Expose one of your agents with an AgentCard so other frameworks can discover it.", "videoId": null, "hearts": 42, "views": null, "likes": null, "likeRate": null, "thin": false, "rating": null, "numRatings": null, "seat": false}, {"id": 24247, "title": "Building AI Agents from First Principles", "speakers": [{"name": "Adib Saikali", "company": "Broadcom"}], "room": "Room 6", "start": "2026-10-06T07:30:00Z", "end": "2026-10-06T10:30:00Z", "type": "Deep Dive", "track": "Agentic Engineering & Tooling", "level": "BEGINNER", "minutes": 150, "keywords": ["memory", "architecture", "planning", "agents"], "themes": ["AI agents & agentic coding", "LLMs in Java apps", "Spring & frameworks"], "tldr": "Saikali builds a dinner-planning agent step by step in Spring AI, showing that an agent is structured output, tool calling, constraint checks and advisors wired together in plain code.", "watchIf": "You are a Java developer who uses chat assistants but has never built an agent, and want a clear mental model of what happens on the wire.", "takeaway": "Measure your agent by acceptance rate, meaning tasks users accept without correction, and collect token, latency and cost metrics from day one.", "videoId": "SC5nO6iadsU", "hearts": 119, "views": 3725, "likes": 36, "likeRate": 0.95, "thin": false, "rating": null, "numRatings": null, "seat": true}, {"id": 22932, "title": "Why Java Is Perfect for AI - Just Not the AI You’re Thinking Of", "speakers": [{"name": "Trisha Gee", "company": "Digital Aguacates"}, {"name": "Andrey Santrosyan", "company": "Redouble AI"}], "room": "Room 9", "start": "2026-10-08T13:00:00Z", "end": "2026-10-08T13:50:00Z", "type": "Conference", "track": "Agentic Engineering & Tooling", "level": "INTERMEDIATE", "minutes": 46, "keywords": ["java", "ai", "enterprise", "agents"], "themes": ["AI agents & agentic coding", "Architecture & design", "LLMs in Java apps"], "tldr": "Gee and Santrosyan argue that enterprise AI means calling models as bounded components from existing Java systems that already encode the business rules, not handing the process to an autonomous agent.", "watchIf": "You maintain enterprise Java systems and want an argument for adding AI as a bounded component instead of rewriting around agents.", "takeaway": "Find the parts of your workflows that need human judgement on messy input (spreadsheets, free text, odd log lines) and call a model just for those.", "videoId": "hHu3rLsbYog", "hearts": 259, "views": 456, "likes": 4, "likeRate": 0.86, "thin": true, "rating": null, "numRatings": null, "seat": true}, {"id": 10034, "title": "The day we stopped publishing Kafka event from our API...", "speakers": [{"name": "Virginie Zinck", "company": "Decathlon"}], "room": "Room 6", "start": "2026-10-05T14:50:00Z", "end": "2026-10-05T15:20:00Z", "type": "Tools-in-Action", "track": "Architecture & Modernization", "level": "BEGINNER", "minutes": 30, "keywords": ["kafka", "architecture", "debezium", "outbox"], "themes": ["Architecture & design", "Data & databases"], "tldr": "Zinck explains how Decathlon's gift card order API fixed its database-plus-Kafka dual write by letting Debezium stream the order tables, and how heartbeats and monitoring prevent the WAL from filling the disk.", "watchIf": "You publish Kafka events from an API that also writes to a database, or you run Debezium against Postgres in production.", "takeaway": "Check your services for any code path that writes to the database and publishes to Kafka in the same request.", "videoId": "YEy7AgRO7As", "hearts": 104, "views": 746, "likes": 11, "likeRate": 1.15, "thin": true, "rating": null, "numRatings": null, "seat": true}, {"id": 19713, "title": "Create your own AI Agents without code", "speakers": [{"name": "David Gageot", "company": "Docker"}], "room": "Room 9", "start": "2026-10-07T15:50:00Z", "end": "2026-10-07T16:40:00Z", "type": "Conference", "track": "Agentic Engineering & Tooling", "level": "INTERMEDIATE", "minutes": 45, "keywords": ["docker agent", "ai agents", "multi-agent", "coding assistants"], "themes": ["AI agents & agentic coding", "Developer experience & tooling"], "tldr": "Gageot builds specialized AI agents with no code using Docker Agent and YAML, showing tools, structured output, evals, flavors, sandboxing and multi-agent protocols on a Pokemon example standing in for business workflows.", "watchIf": "You want to build cheap, specialized AI agents over your own APIs and test them systematically without writing agent code.", "takeaway": "Build a cheap specialized agent instead of running a full coding agent for narrow, repeated tasks.", "videoId": "EJv0TMNSUjw", "hearts": 263, "views": 427, "likes": 5, "likeRate": 0.96, "thin": true, "rating": null, "numRatings": null, "seat": true}, {"id": 17558, "title": "From Laptop to Lakehouse: Analytical SQL with DuckDB", "speakers": [{"name": "Oliver Geisser", "company": "Geisser Consulting"}], "room": "Room 4", "start": "2026-10-06T15:35:00Z", "end": "2026-10-06T16:05:00Z", "type": "Tools-in-Action", "track": "Cloud Native, Data & Infrastructure", "level": "BEGINNER", "minutes": 29, "keywords": ["sql", "lakehouse", "duckdb", "analytics"], "themes": ["Data & databases", "Developer experience & tooling"], "tldr": "A slide-free demo tour of DuckDB, from the CLI and JDBC through querying CSV, Parquet, S3 and Postgres in one engine, to DuckLake and the upcoming Quack client-server protocol.", "watchIf": "You need to analyse files or join data from several databases without running a warehouse, or you are weighing a lightweight lakehouse.", "takeaway": "Try DuckDB without installing anything at shell.duckdb.org, which runs on WebAssembly.", "videoId": "B5-4mDLrWjM", "hearts": 54, "views": 945, "likes": 8, "likeRate": 0.85, "thin": true, "rating": null, "numRatings": null, "seat": true}, {"id": 19732, "title": "Tap into the NVIDIA Ecosystem from Java with TornadoVM's Hybrid API", "speakers": [{"name": "Thanos Stratikopoulos", "company": "Beestream.ai | TornadoVM"}, {"name": "Michalis Papadimitriou", "company": "Neo4j | University of Manchester"}, {"name": "Mary Xekalaki", "company": "beestream.ai | TornadoVM"}], "room": "Room 4", "start": "2026-10-06T14:50:00Z", "end": "2026-10-06T15:20:00Z", "type": "Tools-in-Action", "track": "Agentic Engineering & Tooling", "level": "INTERMEDIATE", "minutes": 27, "keywords": ["java", "gpu", "nvidia", "jni"], "themes": ["JVM performance & runtime", "Hardware, robots & fun"], "tldr": "TornadoVM's library tasks let Java code mix its own GPU kernels with NVIDIA's cuFFT, cuBLAS, cuDNN and cuDF in one task graph, without JNI, Python or C++.", "watchIf": "You want GPU acceleration or NVIDIA libraries in a Java application without JNI, Python or C++, and want to see actual performance numbers.", "takeaway": "Install TornadoVM via SDKMAN and run a first kernel on your laptop GPU before targeting NVIDIA servers.", "videoId": "6SPnBmQrYEk", "hearts": 48, "views": 628, "likes": 7, "likeRate": 0.96, "thin": true, "rating": null, "numRatings": null, "seat": true}, {"id": 31451, "title": "Welcome to Devoxx", "speakers": [{"name": "Stephan Janssen", "company": "Devoxx"}], "room": "Room 8", "start": "2026-10-07T07:30:00Z", "end": "2026-10-07T07:50:00Z", "type": "Keynote", "track": "Agentic Engineering & Tooling", "level": "BEGINNER", "minutes": 26, "keywords": ["belgium", "conference", "devoxx", "keynote"], "themes": ["AI agents & agentic coding", "Developer experience & tooling", "Hardware, robots & fun"], "tldr": "Janssen opens Devoxx Belgium 2026 with a dancing humanoid robot, argues developers are becoming builders, weighs token costs against local models, and hosts a live Groma.md multi-agent demo.", "watchIf": "You want the opening mood of Devoxx 2026 in 25 minutes, including a dancing robot and a live multi-agent architecture-map demo.", "takeaway": "Install a capable local model and try it as an agent orchestrator so you have an option if subscription prices rise.", "videoId": "aup9tNzQu7c", "hearts": 539, "views": 2685, "likes": 26, "likeRate": 0.94, "thin": false, "rating": null, "numRatings": null, "seat": true}, {"id": 13753, "title": "Hi, Belgian Java Community, we want to meet you!", "speakers": [{"name": "Frank Delporte", "company": "Azul"}, {"name": "Tom Cools", "company": "Timefold"}], "room": "BOF 1", "start": "2026-10-05T17:00:00Z", "end": "2026-10-05T18:00:00Z", "type": "BOF", "track": "People, Practices & Career", "level": "BEGINNER", "minutes": 60, "keywords": ["java", "bejug", "community", "networking"], "themes": ["Teams, careers & leadership"], "tldr": "An informal BOF where the Belgian Java User Group (BeJUG) introduces itself and invites attendees to share ideas on events and ways to get involved.", "watchIf": "You are a Java developer in Belgium who wants to meet the community.", "takeaway": "Join BeJUG and consider proposing a meetup talk.", "videoId": null, "hearts": 31, "views": null, "likes": null, "likeRate": null, "thin": false, "rating": null, "numRatings": null, "seat": false}, {"id": 7040, "title": "Wrap, Reshape, or Redesign: Retrofitting Your APIs for a World of Agents", "speakers": [{"name": "Horacio Gonzalez", "company": "Clever Cloud"}], "room": "Room 10", "start": "2026-10-07T10:00:00Z", "end": "2026-10-07T10:50:00Z", "type": "Conference", "track": "Architecture & Modernization", "level": "INTERMEDIATE", "minutes": 48, "keywords": ["tooling", "apis", "agents", "redesign"], "themes": ["AI agents & agentic coding", "Architecture & design"], "tldr": "Gonzalez shows, with Clever Cloud's 15-year-old API, how to sort each capability into wrap, reshape (intent-based endpoints) or redesign (prepare/inspect/execute, task IDs, human approval) so agents use it safely.", "watchIf": "You own a public or internal API that agents are starting to call and want a concrete way to decide what to expose as-is and what to rebuild.", "takeaway": "Audit your OpenAPI spec for operations with no description and add purpose, side effects, async behaviour and when not to use each one.", "videoId": "ymLlOAB8h_c", "hearts": 254, "views": 998, "likes": 2, "likeRate": 0.49, "thin": true, "rating": null, "numRatings": null, "seat": true}, {"id": 19730, "title": "Building LLM Inference Engines in Java with TornadoVM and the NVIDIA Ecosystem", "speakers": [{"name": "Thanos Stratikopoulos", "company": "Beestream.ai | TornadoVM"}, {"name": "Michalis Papadimitriou", "company": "Neo4j | University of Manchester"}, {"name": "Orion Papadakis", "company": "TornadoVM"}], "room": "Room 10", "start": "2026-10-07T14:40:00Z", "end": "2026-10-07T15:30:00Z", "type": "Conference", "track": "Java Language & Platform", "level": "INTERMEDIATE", "minutes": 45, "keywords": ["java", "tornadovm", "llm", "gpu"], "themes": ["LLMs in Java apps", "JVM performance & runtime"], "tldr": "The TornadoVM team presents jitLLM, a pure-Java GPU inference engine that mixes TornadoVM-generated CUDA kernels with NVIDIA libraries inside CUDA graphs to reach 90%+ of llama.cpp decode throughput.", "watchIf": "You want to run local LLMs on NVIDIA GPUs from Java without JNI or a Python sidecar, or you are curious how inference engines squeeze performance out of a GPU.", "takeaway": "Install TornadoVM through SDKMAN and run the jitLLM Quarkus demos on your own NVIDIA GPU.", "videoId": "HO5CpETzywk", "hearts": 59, "views": 393, "likes": 10, "likeRate": 1.4, "thin": true, "rating": null, "numRatings": null, "seat": true}, {"id": 4077, "title": "Do We Need a Build Language? Java Builds Java", "speakers": [{"name": "Rafael Winterhalter", "company": "Scienta"}], "room": "Room 4", "start": "2026-10-06T16:20:00Z", "end": "2026-10-06T16:50:00Z", "type": "Tools-in-Action", "track": "Java Language & Platform", "level": "BEGINNER", "minutes": 31, "keywords": ["java", "security", "build", "jenesis"], "themes": ["Developer experience & tooling", "Security & supply chain", "Java language & JDK"], "tldr": "Winterhalter demos Jenesis, a 24 KB build tool that runs from Java source, uses module-info.java as the build descriptor, and pins dependency hashes and PGP signatures by default.", "watchIf": "You are tired of POM and Gradle files, teach Java to beginners, or want dependency pinning and signature checks without extra tooling.", "takeaway": "Record hashes for all your build dependencies so a rebuild cannot silently pick up a changed artifact.", "videoId": "y9JjxyKc6gU", "hearts": 47, "views": 1355, "likes": 13, "likeRate": 0.92, "thin": true, "rating": null, "numRatings": null, "seat": true}, {"id": 25657, "title": "Safety, speed, and governance in the agentic era", "speakers": [{"name": "Oleg Šelajev", "company": "Docker"}], "room": "Room 7", "start": "2026-10-07T11:05:00Z", "end": "2026-10-07T11:45:00Z", "type": "Lunch Talk", "track": "Agentic Engineering & Tooling", "level": "BEGINNER", "minutes": 41, "keywords": ["sandboxes", "credentials", "isolation", "docker"], "themes": ["Security & supply chain", "AI agents & agentic coding"], "tldr": "Šelajev argues that containers, command allowlists and AI classifiers cannot safely contain autonomous coding agents, and demos Docker Sandboxes: microVMs with network policy, injected secrets and reusable kits.", "watchIf": "You want to run coding agents in autonomous mode on a laptop full of credentials and need an isolation setup a security team could sign off on.", "takeaway": "Run your coding agent through sbx (or another microVM sandbox) instead of directly on your laptop before enabling autonomous mode.", "videoId": "N3VyoNNLDqQ", "hearts": 192, "views": 420, "likes": 2, "likeRate": 0.72, "thin": true, "rating": null, "numRatings": null, "seat": true}, {"id": 11562, "title": "Surviving the \"AI Slopageddon\": How AI shakes the foundations of Open Source", "speakers": [{"name": "Raphael Semeteys", "company": "Worldline"}], "room": "Room 3", "start": "2026-10-07T14:40:00Z", "end": "2026-10-07T15:30:00Z", "type": "Conference", "track": "Development Practices", "level": "BEGINNER", "minutes": 49, "keywords": ["coding", "governance", "vibe", "opensource"], "themes": ["AI agents & agentic coding", "Security & supply chain", "Teams, careers & leadership"], "tldr": "Semeteys argues that AI-generated contributions are straining open source incentives, trust, shared infrastructure, security and licensing, and surveys how projects are rewriting their governance in response.", "watchIf": "You maintain or depend on open source projects and want a broad survey of how AI-generated contributions are affecting them and what governance changes projects are trying.", "takeaway": "Add an AGENTS.md to your project that spells out coding standards, test commands and what agents may and may not do.", "videoId": "vN9jWqSvJNE", "hearts": 140, "views": 344, "likes": 6, "likeRate": 1.11, "thin": true, "rating": null, "numRatings": null, "seat": true}, {"id": 10037, "title": "Quarkus Community BOF", "speakers": [{"name": "Dimitris Andreadis", "company": "IBM"}], "room": "BOF 1", "start": "2026-10-06T17:00:00Z", "end": "2026-10-06T18:00:00Z", "type": "BOF", "track": "Java Language & Platform", "level": "BEGINNER", "minutes": 60, "keywords": ["agentic ai", "quarkus", "open-source", "ecosystem"], "themes": ["Spring & frameworks", "Developer experience & tooling"], "tldr": "An open Ask Us Anything BOF with Quarkus core team members and extension contributors on the ecosystem, community involvement and the team's agentic AI work.", "watchIf": "You use or contribute to Quarkus and want to talk directly with the team.", "takeaway": "Bring a concrete Quarkus question or feature idea to the team.", "videoId": null, "hearts": 22, "views": null, "likes": null, "likeRate": null, "thin": false, "rating": null, "numRatings": null, "seat": false}, {"id": 27453, "title": "Build a private cluster for the office", "speakers": [{"name": "John Davies", "company": "Incept5"}], "room": "Room 6", "start": "2026-10-08T13:00:00Z", "end": "2026-10-08T13:50:00Z", "type": "Conference", "track": "Agentic Engineering & Tooling", "level": "INTERMEDIATE", "minutes": 47, "keywords": ["local", "governance", "models", "privacy"], "themes": ["Hardware, robots & fun", "Security & supply chain"], "tldr": "Davies shows how he runs open-weight Qwen models on a four-box NVIDIA DGX Spark cluster for privacy-bound offices, with serving numbers, pitfalls and KV-cache loading instead of RAG.", "watchIf": "You need LLMs for a regulated or privacy-sensitive organization and want concrete numbers and pitfalls from running open-weight models on a small on-premises cluster.", "takeaway": "Benchmark candidate open-weight models on your own workload, not published leaderboards, before standardizing on one.", "videoId": "rv2KpWgb0Mw", "hearts": 181, "views": 404, "likes": 4, "likeRate": 0.9, "thin": true, "rating": null, "numRatings": null, "seat": true}, {"id": 17763, "title": "Solving Multimodal and Long-Context Challenges", "speakers": [{"name": "Laurent Picard", "company": "Google Cloud"}], "room": "Room 4", "start": "2026-10-05T07:30:00Z", "end": "2026-10-05T10:30:00Z", "type": "Deep Dive", "track": "Agentic Engineering & Tooling", "level": "BEGINNER", "minutes": 179, "keywords": ["long-context", "knowledge graphs", "large language models", "multimodal"], "themes": ["LLMs in Java apps", "Data & databases"], "tldr": "Picard solves four problems live with Gemini, each with one precise prompt and no thinking tokens: video transcription with speakers, book-scale knowledge graphs, consistent image series and illustration restoration.", "watchIf": "You want concrete prompt techniques for extracting structured data from long video, documents or images with one LLM request, and do not mind Python notebooks.", "takeaway": "Before writing a complex prompt, ask the model which terms practitioners use for the task and put those terms in your prompt.", "videoId": "VTxhxCy_2lE", "hearts": 67, "views": 3236, "likes": 24, "likeRate": 0.76, "thin": false, "rating": null, "numRatings": null, "seat": true}, {"id": 9023, "title": "Rewriting the rules: How AI transforms engineering and inspiring young minds", "speakers": [{"name": "Ayoub Hammou Ahabchane", "company": "ING"}, {"name": "Kamel Zaraoui", "company": "ING"}], "room": "Room 3", "start": "2026-10-08T07:30:00Z", "end": "2026-10-08T08:20:00Z", "type": "Conference", "track": "Mind the Geek", "level": "BEGINNER", "minutes": 51, "keywords": ["education", "productivity", "ai", "innovation"], "themes": ["AI agents & agentic coding", "Teams, careers & leadership"], "tldr": "Two ING Belgium engineers describe how the bank rolled out AI coding tools under regulation, show internal agents for vulnerabilities, framework migration and front-end scaffolding, and demo the AI-built Devoxx4Kids labs they run.", "watchIf": "You work in a regulated enterprise and want a practical account of rolling out AI coding tools, or you volunteer teaching kids to code.", "takeaway": "Track AI licence usage by engagement level, not just headcount, and use it to find where people need training.", "videoId": "eSSPOCeoF0c", "hearts": 148, "views": 1859, "likes": 10, "likeRate": 0.63, "thin": true, "rating": null, "numRatings": null, "seat": true}, {"id": 24234, "title": "AsyncAPI in action: OpenAPI for event-driven applications", "speakers": [{"name": "Michel Schudel", "company": "Craftsmen"}], "room": "Room 8", "start": "2026-10-06T15:35:00Z", "end": "2026-10-06T16:05:00Z", "type": "Tools-in-Action", "track": "Architecture & Modernization", "level": "INTERMEDIATE", "minutes": 30, "keywords": ["kafka", "asyncapi", "event-driven", "openapi"], "themes": ["Architecture & design", "Developer experience & tooling"], "tldr": "Schudel shows how an AsyncAPI spec, split into shared and per-service files, generates docs, message records and a working Spring Cloud Stream Kafka publisher and consumer.", "watchIf": "You publish or consume Kafka or Pub/Sub events between teams and want contracts and code generated from one spec instead of a wiki page.", "takeaway": "Replace wiki pages describing event payloads with an AsyncAPI spec that consumers can validate and generate code from.", "videoId": "A_fp7RaG5UY", "hearts": 195, "views": 2585, "likes": 22, "likeRate": 0.85, "thin": false, "rating": null, "numRatings": null, "seat": true}, {"id": 23520, "title": "Beyond the Chatbox: Ship On-Device AI in Your Browser", "speakers": [{"name": "Marcel Bagemihl", "company": "Consid"}, {"name": "Stefan Ludwig", "company": "Consid"}], "room": "BOF 2", "start": "2026-10-06T11:30:00Z", "end": "2026-10-06T14:30:00Z", "type": "Hands-on Lab", "track": "Cloud Native, Data & Infrastructure", "level": "INTERMEDIATE", "minutes": 180, "keywords": ["webgpu", "ai", "browser", "latency"], "themes": ["Frontend & UI", "LLMs in Java apps"], "tldr": "A lab moving pose estimation from a Spring Boot/DJL backend into the browser with LiteRT.js and WebGPU, then comparing latency to decide when AI belongs on-device.", "watchIf": "You build web apps with AI features and want to run models client-side.", "takeaway": "Measure round-trip latency of an AI feature before deciding whether it needs a server.", "videoId": null, "hearts": 24, "views": null, "likes": null, "likeRate": null, "thin": false, "rating": null, "numRatings": null, "seat": false}, {"id": 19706, "title": "Software engineering in the age of AI agents: who’s in control?", "speakers": [{"name": "Bert Jan Schrijver", "company": "OpenValue"}], "room": "Room 5", "start": "2026-10-08T11:50:00Z", "end": "2026-10-08T12:40:00Z", "type": "Conference", "track": "Agentic Engineering & Tooling", "level": "INTERMEDIATE", "minutes": 48, "keywords": ["accountability", "softwareengineering", "verification", "generativeai"], "themes": ["AI agents & agentic coding", "Security & supply chain", "Teams, careers & leadership"], "tldr": "Schrijver argues that agents now do the execution, but control lies in the stop conditions and constraints engineers write, and accountability stays with them: if you bring it to a review, it's your code.", "watchIf": "You are deciding how much autonomy to give coding agents in your team and want well-sourced arguments on verification, security boundaries and accountability.", "takeaway": "Write an explicit stop condition with evidence the agent cannot alter before starting an agentic task.", "videoId": "Z64CjgCO5gk", "hearts": 585, "views": 1021, "likes": 12, "likeRate": 1.03, "thin": true, "rating": null, "numRatings": null, "seat": true}, {"id": 50105, "title": "Building, Shipping and Governing AI Agents on Kubernetes", "speakers": [{"name": "Tom O'Rourke", "company": "Solo.io"}, {"name": "Ep Heijting", "company": "Solo.io"}], "room": "Room 3", "start": "2026-10-08T11:50:00Z", "end": "2026-10-08T12:40:00Z", "type": "Conference", "track": "Agentic Engineering & Tooling", "level": "INTERMEDIATE", "minutes": 52, "keywords": ["github", "agentgateway", "toolcalls", "kubernetes"], "themes": ["AI agents & agentic coding", "Cloud native & Kubernetes", "Security & supply chain"], "tldr": "O'Rourke walks a GitHub pull-request agent from scaffold to production on Solo.io's open source stack, using agentgateway, kagent and agentregistry to cut tool-call overhead and lock the agent down.", "watchIf": "You run or plan to run AI agents on Kubernetes and want concrete patterns for cutting MCP token costs and enforcing identity, egress and credential controls at the platform layer.", "takeaway": "Count the tools and tokens your MCP servers expose before the agent does any work, and filter them down to what each agent actually needs.", "videoId": "OCCOIgkCnjE", "hearts": 132, "views": 723, "likes": 7, "likeRate": 0.9, "thin": true, "rating": null, "numRatings": null, "seat": true}, {"id": 16443, "title": "Say Goodbye to LLM API keys with Workload Identity Federation", "speakers": [{"name": "Siarhei Biarozkin", "company": "IBM / Red Hat"}], "room": "Room 7", "start": "2026-10-08T09:50:00Z", "end": "2026-10-08T10:40:00Z", "type": "Conference", "track": "Security, Trust & Compliance", "level": "INTERMEDIATE", "minutes": 44, "keywords": ["anthropic", "workload identity federation", "spiffe", "api keys"], "themes": ["Security & supply chain", "LLMs in Java apps", "Cloud native & Kubernetes"], "tldr": "Biarozkin argues static LLM API keys don't fit agentic systems and demos a Quarkus LangChain4j app on OpenShift calling Anthropic with SPIFFE-issued tokens exchanged through Workload Identity Federation.", "watchIf": "You run LLM-backed services in production with API keys in config and want a concrete path to short-lived, workload-bound credentials.", "takeaway": "Audit your organisation's LLM API keys today, revoke unused ones and set expiry dates on the rest.", "videoId": "xEAYnXyt3L4", "hearts": 95, "views": 648, "likes": 1, "likeRate": 0.54, "thin": true, "rating": null, "numRatings": null, "seat": true}, {"id": 23522, "title": "Hardwood: Building a Parquet Parser From Scratch (With a Little Help From AI)", "speakers": [{"name": "Gunnar Morling", "company": "Confluent"}], "room": "Room 10", "start": "2026-10-08T11:50:00Z", "end": "2026-10-08T12:40:00Z", "type": "Conference", "track": "Cloud Native, Data & Infrastructure", "level": "INTERMEDIATE", "minutes": 52, "keywords": ["performance", "java", "ai", "parquet"], "themes": ["Data & databases", "JVM performance & runtime", "AI agents & agentic coding"], "tldr": "Morling explains Parquet's internals and how Hardwood, a dependency-free Java Parquet library, gets its speed, and how he built it with Claude Code under tight test feedback.", "watchIf": "You read Parquet files on the JVM and are tired of Hadoop dependencies, or want a realistic account of using Claude Code on performance-critical library code.", "takeaway": "Before letting an agent build a component, find or build an oracle for it: reference test files, test vectors or a second implementation to diff against.", "videoId": "6mzvcPr30kg", "hearts": 42, "views": 883, "likes": 6, "likeRate": 0.76, "thin": true, "rating": null, "numRatings": null, "seat": true}, {"id": 24218, "title": "Conquest, time travel and explosions using Git", "speakers": [{"name": "Alexander Chatzizacharias", "company": "JDriven"}], "room": "Room 6", "start": "2026-10-08T11:50:00Z", "end": "2026-10-08T12:40:00Z", "type": "Conference", "track": "Mind the Geek", "level": "BEGINNER", "minutes": 47, "keywords": ["rebase", "branches", "merge", "git"], "themes": ["Developer experience & tooling", "Hardware, robots & fun"], "tldr": "Chatzizacharias plays a real-time strategy game whose map is a Git repository, and uses it to explain snapshots, staging, three-way merges, custom merge drivers, the .git folder, rebasing and how history can be destroyed.", "watchIf": "You use Git every day but couldn't explain what a rebase, the reflog or a merge driver really does, and you'd like to learn it from a game with buildings exploding.", "takeaway": "Open your repository's .git folder and look at HEAD, refs, logs and objects to see what your daily commands actually change.", "videoId": "wbzpP0T9w6I", "hearts": 316, "views": 853, "likes": 10, "likeRate": 1.01, "thin": true, "rating": null, "numRatings": null, "seat": true}, {"id": 21690, "title": "Exquisite Evals for Skills & MCPs", "speakers": [{"name": "Christian Tzolov", "company": "Spring Team @ Broadcom"}, {"name": "James Ward", "company": "AWS"}], "room": "Room 6", "start": "2026-10-06T16:20:00Z", "end": "2026-10-06T16:50:00Z", "type": "Tools-in-Action", "track": "Development Practices", "level": "INTERMEDIATE", "minutes": 32, "keywords": ["documentation", "javadocs", "evals", "mcp"], "themes": ["LLMs in Java apps", "Testing & quality", "AI agents & agentic coding"], "tldr": "Ward and Tzolov build a task/arm/judge eval matrix with Spring AI to compare the javadocs.dev MCP server with shell and web-search agents, and show evals exposing their own bias.", "watchIf": "You maintain an MCP server or agent skill and want evidence, not anecdotes, that it beats letting the agent use a shell or web search.", "takeaway": "Build an eval matrix for your MCP server or skill that compares it against model-only, shell and web-search baselines.", "videoId": "mebPFAKSpzw", "hearts": 96, "views": 1436, "likes": 7, "likeRate": 0.62, "thin": true, "rating": null, "numRatings": null, "seat": true}, {"id": 10802, "title": "A Deserter Returns: What the Java community helped me rediscover", "speakers": [{"name": "Thierry Chantier", "company": "Dataiku"}], "room": "Room 3", "start": "2026-10-07T15:50:00Z", "end": "2026-10-07T16:40:00Z", "type": "Conference", "track": "Mind the Geek", "level": "BEGINNER", "minutes": 42, "keywords": ["java", "quarkus", "tamboui", "picocli"], "themes": ["Developer experience & tooling", "Java language & JDK", "Teams, careers & leadership"], "tldr": "Chantier, a Python advocate who left Java around Java 8-11, tells how friends, JBang, picocli and TamboUI drew him back, comparing modern Java and Python in short demos.", "watchIf": "You drifted from Java to Python or shell scripting and want a light, personal look at whether modern Java tooling is worth another try.", "takeaway": "Try JBang with picocli for your next small command-line tool instead of a shell or Python script.", "videoId": "i_VXe0mNicY", "hearts": 50, "views": 432, "likes": 1, "likeRate": 0.64, "thin": true, "rating": null, "numRatings": null, "seat": true}, {"id": 4100, "title": "Life in the Ivory Tower: Confessions of a Former Developer Who Turned Into an Architect", "speakers": [{"name": "Robin Custers", "company": "Cegeka"}], "room": "Room 8", "start": "2026-10-07T11:05:00Z", "end": "2026-10-07T11:45:00Z", "type": "Lunch Talk", "track": "Architecture & Modernization", "level": "INTERMEDIATE", "minutes": 35, "keywords": ["collaboration", "psychology", "itdecisionmaking", "enterprisearchitecture"], "themes": ["Teams, careers & leadership", "Architecture & design"], "tldr": "Custers, a developer turned enterprise architect, argues that the role's value is translating business needs into IT decisions, changing things in small experiments, and staying hands-on enough to stay credible.", "watchIf": "You're moving from developer into an architecture role, or keep losing arguments with enterprise architects, and want practical tactics on governance and influence.", "takeaway": "Pitch architecture decisions in the business's own units and language, not in product features.", "videoId": "nhVRKv8VHKU", "hearts": 499, "views": 555, "likes": 5, "likeRate": 0.87, "thin": true, "rating": null, "numRatings": null, "seat": true}, {"id": 16435, "title": "LangChain4j Community BOF", "speakers": [{"name": "Dimitris Andreadis", "company": "IBM"}], "room": "BOF 2", "start": "2026-10-05T17:00:00Z", "end": "2026-10-05T18:00:00Z", "type": "BOF", "track": "Agentic Engineering & Tooling", "level": "BEGINNER", "minutes": 60, "keywords": ["java", "llm", "langchain4j", "framework"], "themes": ["LLMs in Java apps", "AI agents & agentic coding"], "tldr": "An open BOF with the LangChain4j community on the project's current state, adoption stories, challenges and roadmap.", "watchIf": "You use or are considering LangChain4j and want to talk with its community.", "takeaway": "Bring feedback or feature requests for LangChain4j to the maintainers.", "videoId": null, "hearts": 26, "views": null, "likes": null, "likeRate": null, "thin": false, "rating": null, "numRatings": null, "seat": false}, {"id": 10016, "title": "Measuring the Unmeasurable: Evaluating Generative AI in Practice", "speakers": [{"name": "Erin Pacquetet", "company": "SCIAM"}], "room": "Room 8", "start": "2026-10-06T10:40:00Z", "end": "2026-10-06T11:20:00Z", "type": "Lunch Talk", "track": "Agentic Engineering & Tooling", "level": "INTERMEDIATE", "minutes": 41, "keywords": ["rag", "llm", "generativeai", "evaluation"], "themes": ["Testing & quality", "LLMs in Java apps"], "tldr": "Using a client-facing insurance RAG chatbot as the example, Pacquetet lays out an evaluation pipeline built on a written evaluation dataset, narrow pass/fail metrics and human-calibrated LLM judges.", "watchIf": "You have an LLM or RAG prototype that works in demos and need a defensible way to show it is good enough for real users.", "takeaway": "Write expected answers for a set of ideal, realistic and adversarial questions together with your business team before tuning prompts.", "videoId": "Qdn-I3q50Ww", "hearts": 185, "views": 663, "likes": 6, "likeRate": 0.87, "thin": true, "rating": null, "numRatings": null, "seat": true}, {"id": 17800, "title": "🚀 From Java to AI: Build Your First Intelligent Agent System!", "speakers": [{"name": "Emmanuel Hugonnet", "company": "IBM"}, {"name": "Antoine Sabot-Durand", "company": "SCIAM"}, {"name": "yann Blazart", "company": "SCIAM"}], "room": "BOF 2", "start": "2026-10-05T07:30:00Z", "end": "2026-10-05T10:30:00Z", "type": "Hands-on Lab", "track": "Agentic Engineering & Tooling", "level": "BEGINNER", "minutes": 180, "keywords": ["java", "ai", "agents", "langchain4j"], "themes": ["AI agents & agentic coding", "LLMs in Java apps"], "tldr": "A beginner lab on building AI agents in Java with LangChain4j and CDI: AI services, memory, function calling, agent workflows and the Supervisor pattern.", "watchIf": "You are a Java developer wanting a first hands-on path into building agents.", "takeaway": "Expose one existing Java service as a tool an LLM agent can call.", "videoId": null, "hearts": 91, "views": null, "likes": null, "likeRate": null, "thin": false, "rating": null, "numRatings": null, "seat": false}, {"id": 8167, "title": "Open Source in the Age of AI: Where's the value?", "speakers": [{"name": "Trisha Gee", "company": "Digital Aguacates"}], "room": "BOF 2", "start": "2026-10-08T16:50:00Z", "end": "2026-10-08T17:50:00Z", "type": "BOF", "track": "Development Practices", "level": "ADVANCED", "minutes": 60, "keywords": ["ai", "opensource", "community", "business"], "themes": ["Teams, careers & leadership", "AI agents & agentic coding"], "tldr": "A BOF led by Trisha Gee asking where the value of open source lies in the age of AI, covering funding, commercial boundaries and community.", "watchIf": "You maintain or fund an open source project and wonder how AI changes its value.", "takeaway": "Clarify why your organization or project is open source before deciding what to charge for.", "videoId": null, "hearts": 112, "views": null, "likes": null, "likeRate": null, "thin": false, "rating": null, "numRatings": null, "seat": false}, {"id": 9025, "title": "The Quantum Tech Lead: Hands-On and Abstract at the Same Time", "speakers": [{"name": "Cristian Schuszter", "company": "CERN"}], "room": "Room 9", "start": "2026-10-09T07:30:00Z", "end": "2026-10-09T08:20:00Z", "type": "Conference", "track": "People, Practices & Career", "level": "INTERMEDIATE", "minutes": 47, "keywords": ["leadership", "technical", "influence", "transition"], "themes": ["Teams, careers & leadership"], "tldr": "Schuszter of CERN maps the move from engineer to tech lead: wearing several hats, influencing without authority, writing decisions down, delegating, and staying selectively hands-on so impact multiplies through others.", "watchIf": "You have just become a tech lead or staff engineer and miss the feedback of writing code all day.", "takeaway": "Write your next technical decision as a one-screen ADR and share it beyond your own team.", "videoId": "Spxl1eDQJKs", "hearts": 237, "views": 823, "likes": 4, "likeRate": 0.67, "thin": true, "rating": null, "numRatings": null, "seat": true}, {"id": 12758, "title": "Knocking on the Wrong Door: The Six Developers You’ll Meet in Security", "speakers": [{"name": "Enrique Larios Vargas", "company": "OWASP"}], "room": "Room 4", "start": "2026-10-08T14:30:00Z", "end": "2026-10-08T15:20:00Z", "type": "Conference", "track": "People, Practices & Career", "level": "BEGINNER", "minutes": 41, "keywords": ["security culture", "com-b", "psychological safety", "secure development"], "themes": ["Security & supply chain", "Teams, careers & leadership"], "tldr": "Larios Vargas argues security programmes fail by prescribing training for every problem, and presents a COM-B diagnosis with six developer behaviour patterns to match interventions to barriers.", "watchIf": "You run a security champions programme or lead a team where security training keeps being repeated without fewer findings.", "takeaway": "Before launching another security training, find out whether your team's real blocker is skill, time, tooling, autonomy or trust.", "videoId": "qrtd6svRUnk", "hearts": 137, "views": 313, "likes": 6, "likeRate": 1.14, "thin": true, "rating": null, "numRatings": null, "seat": true}, {"id": 16401, "title": "Beyond encryption at rest: computing on encrypted data", "speakers": [{"name": "Andrea Iacono", "company": ""}], "room": "Room 7", "start": "2026-10-07T15:50:00Z", "end": "2026-10-07T16:40:00Z", "type": "Conference", "track": "Security, Trust & Compliance", "level": "BEGINNER", "minutes": 51, "keywords": ["cryptography", "homomorphic encryption", "fully homomorphic encryption", "confidential ai"], "themes": ["Security & supply chain"], "tldr": "Iacono explains homomorphic encryption from RSA through BFV noise budgets and Gentry's bootstrapping, then runs an OpenFHE health-score demo that is about 20,000 times slower than plaintext.", "watchIf": "You handle sensitive data such as health or financial records and want an accessible, maths-light explanation of what homomorphic encryption can do today and what it costs.", "takeaway": "Keep encryption keys for cloud-hosted databases outside the provider that hosts the data.", "videoId": "ePQyy9sLmsY", "hearts": 124, "views": 568, "likes": 1, "likeRate": 0.57, "thin": true, "rating": null, "numRatings": null, "seat": true}, {"id": 9008, "title": "G1, ZGC, Shenandoah, ... with all these GCs in Java, which one do I choose?", "speakers": [{"name": "Antoine DESSAIGNE", "company": "Axway"}], "room": "Room 7", "start": "2026-10-07T12:00:00Z", "end": "2026-10-07T12:50:00Z", "type": "Conference", "track": "Java Language & Platform", "level": "BEGINNER", "minutes": 49, "keywords": ["garbage collection", "zgc", "java", "shenandoah"], "themes": ["JVM performance & runtime"], "tldr": "Dessaigne explains how each HotSpot garbage collector works, from Java 1's mark-and-sweep to generational ZGC and Shenandoah, and gives a simple guide for which GC fits which workload.", "watchIf": "You run Java services and want a clear mental model of how each GC works and a practical rule for picking one.", "takeaway": "Pick the collector by workload (overhead, throughput or latency) rather than defaulting to the newest one.", "videoId": "8nY-kqwoD_A", "hearts": 151, "views": 538, "likes": 10, "likeRate": 1.25, "thin": true, "rating": null, "numRatings": null, "seat": true}, {"id": 19714, "title": "Specialized AI Agents: from concept to production", "speakers": [{"name": "David Gageot", "company": "Docker"}], "room": "Room 6", "start": "2026-10-06T11:30:00Z", "end": "2026-10-06T14:30:00Z", "type": "Deep Dive", "track": "Agentic Engineering & Tooling", "level": "INTERMEDIATE", "minutes": 139, "keywords": ["optimization", "ai", "agents", "docker"], "themes": ["AI agents & agentic coding", "Testing & quality"], "tldr": "Gageot builds a Pokémon-arena agent in YAML with Docker Agent, measures it with repeated evals, fixes failures with tools rather than bigger models, and serves it over HTTP or MCP.", "watchIf": "You want to build a narrow, cost-controlled agent on top of existing APIs and need a practical eval loop to prove it works before shipping.", "takeaway": "Expose only the API operations your agent needs and trim tool responses to the fields it uses.", "videoId": "npSWywhFDHo", "hearts": 85, "views": 612, "likes": 5, "likeRate": 0.84, "thin": true, "rating": null, "numRatings": null, "seat": true}, {"id": 4096, "title": "Deterministic by Design - Achieving Reproducible Builds with Maven", "speakers": [{"name": "Maarten Mulders", "company": "Info Support"}], "room": "Room 4", "start": "2026-10-07T12:00:00Z", "end": "2026-10-07T12:50:00Z", "type": "Conference", "track": "Development Practices", "level": "INTERMEDIATE", "minutes": 48, "keywords": ["maven", "compliance", "determinism", "reproducibility"], "themes": ["Security & supply chain", "Developer experience & tooling"], "tldr": "Mulders shows why bit-for-bit reproducible Maven builds matter for supply-chain security and EU compliance, and how to diagnose, fix and independently verify them with the Maven Artifact plugin, Diffoscope and Reproducible Central.", "watchIf": "You publish Maven artifacts or work under audit or EU regulation and need to prove your binaries match your source.", "takeaway": "Add project.build.outputTimestamp to your POM and run mvn artifact:check-buildplan to find the remaining blockers.", "videoId": "9vtYvAI2Dtk", "hearts": 149, "views": 410, "likes": 1, "likeRate": 0.65, "thin": true, "rating": null, "numRatings": null, "seat": true}, {"id": 9017, "title": "AI in the Dark: Running LLMs in Air-Gapped Production Environments", "speakers": [{"name": "Mihaela Gheorghe-Roman", "company": "Systematic"}], "room": "Room 6", "start": "2026-10-08T15:40:00Z", "end": "2026-10-08T16:30:00Z", "type": "Conference", "track": "Agentic Engineering & Tooling", "level": "INTERMEDIATE", "minutes": 51, "keywords": ["air-gapped", "hallucination", "llm", "production"], "themes": ["LLMs in Java apps", "AI agents & agentic coding", "Security & supply chain"], "tldr": "Gheorghe-Roman shows how a Spring AI agent for military resource allocation runs fully air-gapped: local models behind one ChatClient, self-hosted MCP tools, precise tool descriptions and human approval for writes.", "watchIf": "You build agents for regulated or isolated environments such as defence, healthcare or finance and need a concrete pattern for local models, tool design and human approval of writes.", "takeaway": "Classify every agent tool as read or write before writing code, and route all writes through an explicit human approval step.", "videoId": "kaT6UpLrzhU", "hearts": 129, "views": 496, "likes": 5, "likeRate": 0.91, "thin": true, "rating": null, "numRatings": null, "seat": true}, {"id": 41302, "title": "\"You May Now Optimize the Wedding\": Optimizing the Seating, Bachelor Party, and Honeymoon with Constraint AI", "speakers": [{"name": "Geoffrey De Smet", "company": "Timefold"}, {"name": "Tom Cools", "company": "Timefold"}], "room": "Room 4", "start": "2026-10-08T10:55:00Z", "end": "2026-10-08T11:35:00Z", "type": "Lunch Talk", "track": "Agentic Engineering & Tooling", "level": "BEGINNER", "minutes": 37, "keywords": ["optimization", "routing", "planning", "scheduling"], "themes": ["Architecture & design", "Hardware, robots & fun"], "tldr": "De Smet and Cools use a wedding seating plan, honeymoon itinerary and bachelor-party pub crawl to show why planning problems defeat brute force and how Timefold Solver models them.", "watchIf": "You have a scheduling, routing or assignment problem and want a light-hearted introduction to modelling it with constraints in Java.", "takeaway": "Do not brute-force or vibe-code a custom search algorithm for scheduling or assignment problems; use a solver such as Timefold Solver.", "videoId": "lsvVJaigNp8", "hearts": 138, "views": 678, "likes": 5, "likeRate": 0.8, "thin": true, "rating": null, "numRatings": null, "seat": true}, {"id": 24202, "title": "Beyond the Pod: Architecting and Scaling AI Agents on Kubernetes", "speakers": [{"name": "Abdellfetah SGHIOUAR", "company": "Google Cloud"}], "room": "Room 4", "start": "2026-10-08T13:00:00Z", "end": "2026-10-08T13:50:00Z", "type": "Conference", "track": "Cloud Native, Data & Infrastructure", "level": "BEGINNER", "minutes": 43, "keywords": ["kubernetes", "isolation", "agents", "scalability"], "themes": ["Cloud native & Kubernetes", "AI agents & agentic coding"], "tldr": "Sghiouar demos Agent Substrate, which multiplexes mostly idle, sandboxed agents onto a shared Kubernetes worker pool, snapshotting memory and disk so agents suspend and wake in under 500 ms.", "watchIf": "You run, or plan to run, many coding or AI agents on Kubernetes and want to see a working way to sandbox them and stop paying for idle pods.", "takeaway": "Measure how much of the time your agent pods are actually busy before giving every agent its own pod.", "videoId": "3bIiAoYtAPs", "hearts": 122, "views": 512, "likes": 7, "likeRate": 1.05, "thin": true, "rating": null, "numRatings": null, "seat": true}, {"id": 16444, "title": "Faster Development with Java, Python, and Micronaut", "speakers": [{"name": "Graeme Rocher", "company": "Oracle"}, {"name": "Thomas Wuerthinger", "company": "Oracle"}], "room": "Room 7", "start": "2026-10-08T13:00:00Z", "end": "2026-10-08T13:50:00Z", "type": "Conference", "track": "Java Language & Platform", "level": "INTERMEDIATE", "minutes": 48, "keywords": ["micronaut", "graalvm", "python", "graalpy"], "themes": ["Spring & frameworks", "JVM performance & runtime", "Developer experience & tooling"], "tldr": "Rocher and Wuerthinger launch Pyronaut: a pre-compiled, PGO-optimised Micronaut and GraalVM base onto which Python or Java application code is loaded dynamically, giving millisecond restarts without native image builds.", "watchIf": "You want fast edit-test loops for Java or Python services, for yourself or your coding agents, without giving up native-image start-up in production.", "takeaway": "Run `pip install pyronaut` and try `pyronaut dev` on an existing Micronaut Maven project to measure the restart and test loop.", "videoId": "zo6L7bxn4OY", "hearts": 108, "views": 778, "likes": 19, "likeRate": 1.62, "thin": true, "rating": null, "numRatings": null, "seat": true}, {"id": 5541, "title": "Dream Machines & Walled Gardens", "speakers": [{"name": "Anders Norås", "company": "Indipendant "}], "room": "Room 10", "start": "2026-10-08T08:40:00Z", "end": "2026-10-08T09:30:00Z", "type": "Conference", "track": "Mind the Geek", "level": "BEGINNER", "minutes": 49, "keywords": ["computing", "ted nelson", "hypertext", "decentralization"], "themes": ["Hardware, robots & fun"], "tldr": "A biography of Ted Nelson, from a nexialist boyhood to hypertext, Computer Lib and Xanadu, asking how the web might have turned out had Xanadu been a protocol rather than a product.", "watchIf": "You enjoy computing history and want the story of hypertext's inventor told as an argument about open protocols versus walled gardens.", "takeaway": "Read Vannevar Bush's As We May Think and browse Computer Lib/Dream Machines; Norås says both are freely findable online.", "videoId": "am8adCMJQzk", "hearts": 101, "views": 689, "likes": 1, "likeRate": 0.53, "thin": true, "rating": null, "numRatings": null, "seat": true}, {"id": 21671, "title": "The Rise of Agent Enablement: Coding Agents Don't Scale Themselves. Neither Do Your Teams.", "speakers": [{"name": "Patrick Debois", "company": "Tessl"}], "room": "Room 6", "start": "2026-10-08T09:50:00Z", "end": "2026-10-08T10:40:00Z", "type": "Conference", "track": "People, Practices & Career", "level": "BEGINNER", "minutes": 47, "keywords": ["organization", "platform", "readiness", "agents"], "themes": ["AI agents & agentic coding", "Teams, careers & leadership", "Developer experience & tooling"], "tldr": "Debois argues scaling coding agents is organizational, not technical: treat context like code with its own lifecycle, enable team, platform and org, and the real new problem is concurrency control across many agents.", "watchIf": "You lead or enable engineering teams past 'we gave them coding tools' and want a framework for scaling agentic coding across an organization.", "takeaway": "Treat your team's context (skills, AGENT.md, specs) as a versioned, tested, shared artifact, not per-laptop files.", "videoId": "wxNQRGPnJ24", "hearts": 264, "views": 1012, "likes": 11, "likeRate": 0.98, "thin": true, "rating": null, "numRatings": null, "seat": true}, {"id": 50104, "title": "Engineering Lessons from Working on Software Older Than Me", "speakers": [{"name": "Guust Ysebie", "company": "Apryse"}], "room": "Room 3", "start": "2026-10-08T10:55:00Z", "end": "2026-10-08T11:35:00Z", "type": "Lunch Talk", "track": "Development Practices", "level": "BEGINNER", "minutes": 34, "keywords": ["performance", "security", "pdf", "compatibility"], "themes": ["Legacy modernization", "Teams, careers & leadership"], "tldr": "Ysebie, an iText developer at Apryse, draws lessons on adoption from PDF's history and from his own push to get Brotli compression, about 20% smaller files, into the PDF standard and the major viewers.", "watchIf": "You maintain a library, protocol or format others depend on and want to get a change adopted across an ecosystem.", "takeaway": "Before writing a parser for an unstructured format, ask whoever produces the data for a structured version.", "videoId": "VaT7DgmVSms", "hearts": 114, "views": 492, "likes": 4, "likeRate": 0.84, "thin": true, "rating": null, "numRatings": null, "seat": true}, {"id": 50109, "title": "Keeping Spring Applications Secure in the Age of AI", "speakers": [{"name": "Neven Cvetkovic", "company": "VMware Tanzu"}], "room": "Room 7", "start": "2026-10-08T10:55:00Z", "end": "2026-10-08T11:35:00Z", "type": "Lunch Talk", "track": "Java Language & Platform", "level": "BEGINNER", "minutes": 41, "keywords": ["security", "spring", "automation", "upgrades"], "themes": ["Security & supply chain", "Spring & frameworks", "Legacy modernization"], "tldr": "Cvetkovic argues AI-driven vulnerability discovery makes continuous patching mandatory for Spring apps, and demos Broadcom's commercial Spring Application Advisor upgrading Pet Clinic from Boot 2.7 to 3.5.", "watchIf": "You maintain Spring Boot 2.7 or 3.x apps and want to see what Broadcom's commercial upgrade tooling and extended support actually do.", "takeaway": "Run a scheduled pipeline that applies patch-level dependency updates several times a day, not just on a monthly cycle.", "videoId": "-LMWT5OCneA", "hearts": 170, "views": 585, "likes": 5, "likeRate": 0.85, "thin": true, "rating": null, "numRatings": null, "seat": true}]};
